1084 #uuid "3572fdc7-7e23-5b3f-bd3b-2640512eff6b" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228721 1081 "((x + 2)^{2})/(x)" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228721 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228721 1084 #uuid "6eba9d4a-c1b7-57c9-9602-191b9bb3ce60" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228722 1069 "e-19309" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228722 1071 "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228722 1072 #uuid "2532a9fb-231a-5c31-a38d-a59a10c914c1" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228722 1074 "To find the sale price, we calculate the discount and subtract it from the original price.\n\nFirst, we calculate $14\\%$ of $\\[math]78{:}$\n\n$$\n0.14 \\times 78 = 10.92\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract this amount from the original price to obtain\n\n$$\n78 - 10.92 = 67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the boots is $\\[/math]67.08$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228723 1069 "e-19311" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228723 1071 "$63$ is $7\\%$ of what number?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228723 1072 #uuid "ae8cd5f4-afa4-5d79-9d56-5da94de92fbe" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228723 1074 "To find the number that $63$ is $7\\%$ of, we divide $63$ by $0.07{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{63}{0.07} = 900\n$$\n\nSo, $63$ is $7\\%$ of $900$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228724 1069 "e-19313" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228724 1071 "Out of $640{,}000$ cans, $384{,}000$ are recycled. What percentage of the cans are recycled?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228724 1072 #uuid "333b0267-ca71-5282-b5eb-bba906fe4d87" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228724 1074 "To find what percentage of the cans are recycled, we divide the number of recycled cans by the total number of cans and multiply by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{384{,}000}{640{,}000} \\times 100 = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\times 100 = 60\\%\n$$\n\nSo, $60\\%$ of the cans are recycled." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228725 1069 "e-19314" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228725 1071 "What percent of $900$ is $495$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228725 1072 #uuid "ca1638c1-d6d1-5dab-a2f9-8945b9e16d5a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228725 1074 "To find what percent of $900$ is $495$, we divide $495$ by $900$ and multiply the result by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{495}{900} \\times 100 = \\dfrac{11}{20} \\times 100 = 55\\%\n$$\n\nSo, $495$ is $55\\%$ of $900$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228726 1069 "e-19330" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228726 1071 "A scientist identified $64$ known species in a survey, which represented $80\\%$ of all the species present. How many species were present in total?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228726 1072 #uuid "6912249f-26c5-5f80-8248-3cc79e9597ac" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228726 1074 "We are told that $64$ species is $80\\%$ of the total number of species.\n\nLet $x$ represent the total number of species. We can write the equation\n$0.80x = 64$\nto represent the situation.\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $0.80$ to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{64}{0.80} \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of species present was $80$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228727 1069 "e-19331" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228727 1071 "An artist earned $\\[math]20$ from a print sale. This amount represented $p\\%$ of the total print price of $\\[/math]25$. What is the value of $p$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228727 1072 #uuid "7d16b4bf-455c-5b8c-b489-dee93ef32468" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228727 1074 "We are told that an earning of $\\[math]20$ is $p\\%$ of the print’s $\\[/math]25$ price.\n\nFirst, we express $p\\%$ of $25$ as a fraction and set it equal to $20$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 25 = 20\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 25 &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= \\frac{20}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= 0.8 \\\\\np &= 0.8 \\cdot 100 \\\\\np &= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is ${80}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228728 1069 "e-19491" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228728 1071 "A t-shirt costs $\\[math]18$ before tax. If a $9\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228728 1072 #uuid "080ceecb-c0c4-56b2-abf7-217815d78af5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228728 1074 "To find the total cost after applying the tax, we increase the price by $9\\%$, which means multiplying by:\n\n$$\n100\\% + 9\\% = 109\\% = 1.09\n$$\n\nNow, we compute\n\n$$\n18 \\times 1.09 = 19.62\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $\\[math]19.62$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228729 1069 "e-19492" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228729 1071 "What number is $40\\%$ greater than $360$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228729 1072 #uuid "d4b18b62-4a62-5673-9c1b-e5189e6b858d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228729 1074 "A number $40\\%$ greater means applying a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140\\% = \\dfrac{140}{100} = 1.40\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $40\\%$ greater than $360$, we multiply $360$ by $1.40{:}$\n\n$$\n1.40 \\cdot 360 = 504\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $40\\%$ greater than $360$ is $504$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228730 1069 "e-19494" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228730 1071 "An hour ago, the download speed of an Internet connection was $24.0\\text{Mbps}$. Now the download speed is $30.0\\text{Mbps}$. To the nearest tenth of a percent, by what percent did the speed increase?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228730 1072 #uuid "992e49b2-2fec-58e7-a002-dd43eb73ff2d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228730 1074 "To find the percent increase, we start by computing the absolute increase in price:\n\n$$\n30.0-24.0=6.0\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the percent increase by dividing the increase by the original value:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6.0}{24.0}=0.25\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a percentage:\n\n$$\n0.25\\times 100\\%=25.0\\%\n$$\n\nSo, the speed increased by $25.0\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228731 1069 "e-19495" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228731 1071 "The number of employees at a startup increased from $25$ to $125$. What was the percent increase in employees?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228731 1072 #uuid "c0ce3e72-dbbf-511a-afb9-336a9dc74335" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228731 1074 "To find the percent increase, we first calculate the absolute increase in quantity:\n\n$$\n125 - 25 = 100\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the percent increase by dividing the increase by the original amount:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100}{25} = 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a percentage:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 100 = 400\\%\n$$\n\nSo, the number of employees increased by $400\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228732 1069 "e-19496" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228732 1071 "After a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228732 1072 #uuid "681d38cd-4669-5711-a4d1-7b858be5212a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228732 1074 "A $35\\%$ increase means the final fee is $135\\%$ of the original fee.\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nLet the original fee be $x$. So, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told the increased value is $\\[math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original fee was $\\[/math]300$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228733 1069 "e-19505" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228733 1071 "$90$ is $k\\%$ greater than $20$. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228733 1072 #uuid "6a6bc058-be3d-553d-a6de-e7df7846e5a5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228733 1074 "We are told that $90$ is $k\\%$ greater than $20$, so the total increase is\n\n$$\n20 + \\dfrac{k}{100} \\cdot 20\n$$\n\nThat gives us the equation:\n\n$$\n20 + \\dfrac{k}{100} \\cdot 20 = 90\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 + \\frac{k}{100} \\cdot 20 &= 90 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{100} \\cdot 20 &= 90 - 20 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{100} \\cdot 20 &= 70 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{100} &= \\frac{70}{20} \\\\\n\\frac{k}{100} &= 3.5 \\\\\nk &= 350\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $k$ is $350$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228734 1069 "e-19507" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228734 1071 "A collector sold part of a marble collection. After $45\\%$ of the original marbles were sold, $484$ marbles remained. How many marbles were sold?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228734 1072 #uuid "eacfdf0a-12f0-52b1-89a3-377ca6da126a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228734 1074 "Let $x$ represent the original number of marbles.\n\nIf $45\\%$ of them were removed, then $55\\%$ of them remained. So we have the equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{55}{100} \\cdot x = 484\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{55}{100} \\cdot x &= 484 \\\\\n0.55x &= 484 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{484}{0.55} \\\\\nx &= 880\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of marbles was $880$, we find the number removed by taking $45\\%$ of $880{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{45}{100} \\cdot 880 = 396\n$$\n\nSo, $396$ marbles were sold." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228735 1069 "e-19508" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228735 1071 "An IT team reported resolved support tickets. By the end of the day, the total was $1{,}050$ tickets. The percent increase in resolved tickets from the morning update to the end of the day was $50\\%$. How many tickets were reported in the morning update?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228735 1072 #uuid "ace675e6-8dc3-5499-bb88-6dfeff0cff70" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228735 1074 "Let $x$ represent the number of resolved tickets in the morning update.\n\nA $50\\%$ increase means that by the end of the day, the total became\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{50}{100} \\cdot x\n$$\n\nThat gives us the equation:\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{50}{100} \\cdot x = 1{,}050\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + \\frac{50}{100} \\cdot x &= 1,050 \\\\\nx + 0.50 \\cdot x &= 1,050 \\\\\n1.50x &= 1,050 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1,050}{1.50} \\\\\nx &= 700\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there were $700$ tickets reported in the morning update." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228736 1069 "e-19510" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228736 1071 "After increasing a quantity $x$ by $1{,}900\\%$, the result is $740$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228736 1072 #uuid "3224e487-3d7b-5014-ac11-e6b88be415c3" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228736 1074 "We are told that increasing a quantity $x$ by $1,900\\%$ results in $740$. Let's find the value of $x$.\n\nA $1{,}900\\%$ increase means the final amount is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 1{,}900\\% = 2{,}000\\%\n$$\n\nof the original.\n\nWe convert $2{,}000\\%$ to a decimal:\n\n$$\n2{,}000\\% = \\dfrac{2{,}000}{100} = 20\n$$\n\nSo, we have the equation:\n$20x = 740$\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{740}{20} \\\\\n&= 37\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $x$ is $37$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228737 1069 "e-19512" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228737 1071 "Lena bought a drawing tablet for $\\[math]480$. She estimates that the value of the tablet drops by $25\\%$ each month. What is the estimated value of the tablet exactly $2$ months after she bought it?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228737 1072 #uuid "ee942b6d-b755-59a9-9fd7-760863ed33d4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228737 1074 "We're told that the tablet's value decreases by $25\\%$ each month. This means that its value in each month is\n\n$$\n100\\% - 25\\% = 75\\%\n$$\n\nof the value of the previous month.\n\nWe are also told that at the time of purchase, the tablet was worth $\\[math]480$. So,\n\n- the tablet's value after the first month is $(0.75)(\\[/math]480) = \\[math]360$, and\n- the tablet's value after the second month is $(0.75)(\\[/math]360) = \\[math]270$.\n\nTherefore, the estimated value of the tablet after $2$ months is $\\[/math]270$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228738 1069 "e-19516" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228738 1071 "Online course enrollments increased by $70\\%$ from June to July, but then dropped by $30\\%$ from July to August. What was the net percentage increase in enrollments from June to August?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228738 1072 #uuid "5ca25b8f-ceb1-57da-970d-7954233b1f78" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228738 1074 "Let $A$ be the number of enrollments in June.\n\nWe are told that enrollments increased by $70\\%$ from June to July. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nEnrollments in July &= (1 + 0.70)A \\\\\n&= 1.70A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that enrollments dropped by $30\\%$ from July to August. This means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nEnrollments in August &= (1 - 0.30)(1.70A) \\\\\n&= 0.70(1.70A) \\\\\n&= 1.19A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of enrollments in August was $1.19$ times the number of enrollments in June. This is equivalent to $119\\%$ of the original number of enrollments, which represents a net increase of $19\\%$ from the original $100\\%$.\n\nTherefore, the net percentage increase in enrollments from June to August was $19\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228739 1069 "e-19517" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228739 1071 "The number $k$ is $130\\%$ greater than the positive number $l$. The number $m$ is $45\\%$ less than $k$. The number $m$ is how many times $l$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228739 1072 #uuid "d25f1ce4-1d1a-51ae-a5db-1fe6055ff9cd" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228739 1074 "Since the number $k$ is $130\\%$ greater than the positive number $l$, we have\n\n$$\nk = \\left(1 + \\dfrac{130}{100}\\right) \\cdot l\n$$\n\n$$\nk = (1 + 1.3) \\cdot l\n$$\n\n$k = 2.3l$.\n\nBecause the number $m$ is $45\\%$ less than $k$, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\left(1 - \\dfrac{45}{100}\\right) \\cdot k\n$$\n\n$$\nm = (1 - 0.45) \\cdot k\n$$\n\n$m = 0.55k$.\n\nSubstituting $2.3l$ for $k$ in $m = 0.55k$, we obtain\n\n$$\nm = 0.55(2.3l)\n$$\n\n$$\nm = (0.55 \\cdot 2.3)l\n$$\n\n$m = 1.265l$.\n\nTherefore, the number $m$ is ${1.265}$ times the number $l$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228740 1069 "e-19518" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228740 1071 "The size of a company’s data archive increased by $17\\%$ from quarter $1$ to quarter $2$, and then by $8\\%$ from quarter $2$ to quarter $3$. If the archive was $z$ times larger in quarter $3$ than in quarter $1$, what is the value of $z$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228740 1072 #uuid "d41324d0-176b-559b-b47b-9022b4977ff8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228740 1074 "Let $D_1$, $D_2$, and $D_3$ be the archive size in quarters $1$, $2$, and $3$, respectively.\n\nWe are told that, in quarter $2$, the archive grew by $17\\%$ over quarter $1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\left(1 + \\dfrac{17}{100}\\right)D_1 = 1.17D_1\n$$\n\nWe are also told that, in quarter $3$, the archive grew by $8\\%$ over quarter $2$. This means\n\n$$\nD_3 = \\left(1 + \\dfrac{8}{100}\\right) D_2 = 1.08D_2 = (1.08)(1.17D_1) = 1.2636D_1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the archive was $1.2636$ times larger in quarter $3$ than in quarter $1$.\n\nSince it was $z$ times as large, we conclude that $z = 1.2636$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228741 1069 "e-19520" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228741 1071 "The number $p$ is $180\\%$ greater than the number $q$. The number $q$ is $60\\%$ less than $50$. What is the value of $p$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228741 1072 #uuid "bd69989d-3ba7-59f3-bac4-4d902ef031da" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228741 1074 "We are told that $q$ is $60\\%$ less than $50$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq &= 50 - 50 \\cdot 60% \\\\\n&= 50 - 50 \\cdot \\frac{60}{100} \\\\\n&= 50 - 30 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are also told that $p$ is $180\\%$ greater than $q$. This means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= q + q \\cdot 180% \\\\\n&= q + q \\cdot \\frac{180}{100} \\\\\n&= q + 1.8q \\\\\n&= (1 + 1.8)q \\\\\n&= 2.8q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $q = 20$ into $p = 2.8q$, we get\n\n$$\np = 2.8(20) = 56\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is $56$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228742 1069 "e-19538" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228742 1071 "Five salespeople made a total of $\\[math]1,000$ in sales. It is known that one of the salespeople made $\\[/math]400$ in sales. Which of the following can be determined from this information?\n\n1. The average sales per person.\n2. The greatest sales made by any one salesperson.\n3. The least sales made by any one salesperson." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228742 1072 #uuid "0f3d903d-22f3-5741-a28f-bc718efb9fab" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228742 1074 "The average (or mean) is calculated by dividing the sum of the data points by the number of data points.\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Consider statement I. We are given the total sales amount $(\\[math]1,000)$ and the number of salespeople $(5)$. So, we can compute the average, given by\n$\\text{Average} = \\dfrac{1,000}{5} = \\[/math]200$.\nTherefore, the average sales per person *can* be determined.\n- Consider statement II. We know one salesperson made $\\[math]400$, but we do not know the sales amounts of the others. It is possible that another salesperson made more. Therefore, the greatest sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n- Consider statement III. Similarly, we cannot determine the least sales without knowing the sales for each person. Therefore, the least sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228743 1069 "e-19541" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228743 1071 "$95,\\: 97,\\: 99,\\: 100,\\: 101,\\: 102,\\: 104,\\: 106,\\: 108$\n\nWhat is the median of the data values given above?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228743 1072 #uuid "78157fa5-2238-5494-b0be-51ec7059a90c" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228743 1074 "The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nTo find the median, we first check that the data is in increasing order. In this case, it is already sorted:\n\n$$\n95,\\: 97,\\: 99,\\: 100,\\: 101,\\: 102,\\: 104,\\: 106,\\: 108\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ data values, which is an odd number. When the number of values is odd, the median is the value in the exact middle. That means we take the $5$th value:\n\n$$\n95,\\: 97,\\: 99,\\: 100,\\: \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{101},\\: 102,\\: 104,\\: 106,\\: 108\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is $101$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228744 1069 "e-19545" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228744 1071 "If $c$ is the mean and $d$ is the median of six consecutive negative integers, what is the value of $\\dfrac{d}{c}$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228744 1072 #uuid "eafeb086-10a6-54da-9654-587922b5ea62" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228744 1074 "Let $x$ be the largest of the numbers. Then, the six consecutive negative integers are\n\n$$\nx - 5,\\quad x - 4,\\quad x - 3,\\quad x - 2,\\quad x - 1,\\quad x\n$$\n\nSo, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= ((x - 5) + (x - 4) + (x - 3) + (x - 2) + (x - 1) + x)/(6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6x - 15}{6} \\\\\n&= x - 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $6$ values, the median is the average of the third and fourth values:\n\n$$\nx - 5,\\quad x - 4,\\quad \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{x - 3},\\quad \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{x - 2},\\quad x - 1,\\quad x\n$$\n\nThus, the median is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= ((x - 3) + (x - 2))/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x - 5}{2} \\\\\n&= x - 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{d}{c} = \\dfrac{x - 2.5}{x - 2.5} ={1}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228745 1069 "e-19546" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228745 1071 "$11,\\: w,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 20$\nThe mean and the median of the numbers above are not equal. Which of the following could be the value of $w$?\n\n1. $13$\n2. $14$\n3. $19$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228745 1072 #uuid "7497374c-3e83-5435-9d07-9b727085e1eb" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228745 1074 "We are told that the mean and median of the five numbers are not equal.\n\nLet’s begin by writing an expression for the mean. The mean of the five values is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11 + w + 14 + 16 + 20}{5} = \\dfrac{61+w}{5}\n$$\n\nAlso, since there are five numbers, the median will be the $3$rd value when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, let’s test each value of $w$ in turn.\n\n- If $w = 13$, then the list arranged in order from smallest to greatest is\n$11,\\: 13,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 20$.\nIn this case, the median is $14$, and the mean is\n$\\frac{61 + 13}{5} = \\frac{74}{5} = 14.8$.\nSince the mean and median are not equal, $13$ *is* a possible value of $w$.\n- If $w = 14$, then the list arranged in order from smallest to greatest is\n$11,\\: 14,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 20$.\nIn this case, the median is $14$, and the mean is\n$\\frac{61 + 14}{5} = \\frac{75}{5} = 15$.\nSince the mean and median are not equal, $14$ *is* a possible value of $w$.\n- If $w = 19$, then the list arranged in order from smallest to greatest is\n$11,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 19,\\: 20$.\nIn this case, the median is $16$, and the mean is\n$\\frac{61 + 19}{5} = \\frac{80}{5} = 16$.\nSince the mean and median are equal, $19$ *is not* a possible value of $w$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228746 1069 "e-19548" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228746 1071 "A city tracks the water usage of $31$ households:\n\n- Group $A$ includes $8$ households that used more than $400$ gallons of water.\n- Group $B$ includes $15$ households that used between $150$ and $400$ gallons (inclusive).\n- Group $C$ includes $8$ households that used less than $150$ gallons.\n\nWhich of the following could be the median water usage?\n\n1. $200$ gallons\n2. $300$ gallons\n3. $400$ gallons" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228746 1072 #uuid "cb4c3049-7e38-5b19-a1ae-63df0c258be0" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228746 1074 "There are a total of $31$ households. So, the median is the $16$th value when all usage amounts are listed in increasing order.\n\nNow, let’s look at how the values are distributed.\n\n- The first $8$ values are from Group $C$. These are all less than $150$ gallons. These values occupy positions $1$ through $8$.\n- The next $15$ values are from Group $B$, which includes usage between $150$ and $400$ gallons (inclusive). These values occupy positions $9$ through $23$.\n- The final $8$ values are from Group $A$, which are all more than $400$ gallons. These values occupy positions $24$ through $31$.\n\nHence, the $16$th value falls within Group $B$'s usage range and is between $150$ and $400$ gallons.\n\nTherefore, of the given options, $200$ gallons, $300$ gallons, and $400$ gallons are all possible values for the median, and the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228747 1069 "e-19549" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228747 1071 "$7,\\: 14,\\: 21,\\: 18,\\: 35,\\: 2,\\: 10,\\: 27$\nWhat is the median of the data values given above?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228747 1072 #uuid "b2e48565-cbdf-52fc-9f68-87b431f382f5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228747 1074 "The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nTo find the median, we must first order the numbers from least to greatest:\n\n$$\n2, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\: 14, \\: 18, \\: 21, \\: 27, \\: 35\n$$\n\nThere are $8$ numbers in the data set. Since $8$ is even, we find the average of the two middle numbers.\n\n$$\n2, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\: \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{14}, \\: \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{18}, \\: 21, \\: 27, \\: 35\n$$\n\nThe two middle numbers are $14$ and $18$, so the median is\n\n$$\n\\frac{14 + 18}{2} = \\frac{32}{2} = 16\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228748 1069 "e-19550" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228748 1071 "$6,\\: 10,\\: 14,\\: 18,\\: 22,\\: 26$\n\nThe mean of the list of numbers above is what fraction of the sum? Write your answer in its simplified form." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228748 1072 #uuid "232dedb9-b9a1-5a4f-bfdc-d210239f3230" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228748 1074 "The mean of a dataset is the sum of the values divided by the number of values.\n\nIn this case, there are $6$ values. So, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{6 + 10 + 14 + 18 + 22 + 26}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(6 + 10 + 14 + 18 + 22 + 26)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$\n\nof the sum." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228749 1069 "e-19552" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228749 1071 "Dataset $X$ consists of the working hours of $20$ employees and has a mean of $20$ hours. Dataset $Y$ consists of the working hours of $30$ employees and has a mean of $40$ hours. Dataset $Z$ consists of the working hours of the $50$ employees from datasets $X$ and $Y$. What is the mean of dataset $Z$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228749 1072 #uuid "4ea3aef0-9a0b-5834-8d84-cc9e42881188" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228749 1074 "We are told that dataset $X$ contains $20$ employees and has a mean of $20$ hours. So, the sum $S_X$ of all hours in dataset $X$ is\n\n$$\nS_X = 20 \\cdot 20 = 400\n$$\n\nWe are also told that dataset $Y$ contains $30$ employees and has a mean of $40$ hours. So, the sum $S_Y$ of all hours in dataset $Y$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = 30 \\cdot 40 = 1\\,200\n$$\n\nNow, since dataset $Z$ includes all $50$ employees from $X$ and $Y$, the sum $S_Z$ of all hours in dataset $Z$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{Z} &= S_{X} + S_{Y} \\\\\n&= 400 + 1200 \\\\\n&= 1600hours\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean of dataset $Z$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{1\\,600}{50} = {32}\\,\\text{hours}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228750 1069 "e-19553" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228750 1071 "Dataset $Q$ consists of $10$ positive integers less than $70$. The list below gives $9$ of the integers from dataset $Q{:}$\n\n$44,\\: 47,\\: 49,\\: 57,\\: 45,\\: 46,\\: 48,\\: 47,\\: 49$\n\nThe mean of these $9$ values is $48$. If the mean of dataset $Q$ is an integer greater than $48$, what is the value of the largest integer from dataset $Q$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228750 1072 #uuid "81065ebf-c0f1-5b96-9994-276556e084b9" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228750 1074 "We are told that the mean of the $9$ known values is $48$. Using this, we can compute the sum $S$ of those $9$ values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{S}{9} \\\\\nS &= 9 \\cdot Mean \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 48 \\\\\n&= 432\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery value in dataset $Q$ is a positive integer less than $70$, and the mean of dataset $Q$ is an integer that is greater than $48$. Let's consider possible values for the mean, starting with the smallest.\n\n- If the mean of $Q$ is $49$, then the total sum of the dataset is\n$10 \\cdot 49 = 490$.\nSubtracting the sum of the nine given integers, the missing value is\n$490 - S = 490 - 432 = 58$.\nSince $58 < 70$, this value *can* belong to the dataset.\n- If the mean of $Q$ is $50$, then the total sum of the dataset is\n$10 \\cdot 50 = 500$.\nSubtracting the sum of the nine given integers, the missing value is\n$500 - S = 500 - 432 = 68$.\nSince $68 < 70$, this value *also* can belong to the dataset.\n- If the mean of $Q$ is $51$, then the total sum of the dataset is\n$10 \\cdot 51 = 510$.\nSubtracting the sum of the nine given integers, the missing value is\n$510 - S = 510 - 432 = 78$.\nSince $78 > 70$, this value *cannot* belong to the dataset.\n\nWe stop here because increasing the mean any further would only increase the missing value, which would continue to violate the constraint that all values must be less than $70$.\n\nTherefore, the largest possible missing value, and hence, the largest value in dataset $Q$, is ${68}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228751 1069 "e-19554" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228751 1071 "$6, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 7, \\: 8, \\: 8, \\: 9, \\: 9, \\: 9$\n\nConstruct a frequency table to summarize the data above." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228751 1072 #uuid "36b2d7c5-8014-5130-9549-7b3ca19d9cec" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228751 1074 "To create a frequency table, we first count the number of times each value appears in the data set. This count is known as the frequency of a value.\n\n- The frequency of $6$ is $4$ because the value $6$ appears $4$ times in the data set.\n- The frequency of $7$ is $1$ because the value $7$ appears $1$ time in the data set.\n- The frequency of $8$ is $2$ because the value $8$ appears $2$ times in the data set.\n- The frequency of $9$ is $3$ because the value $9$ appears $3$ times in the data set.\n\nTherefore, the frequency table is as follows:\n\n| Number | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $9$ | $3$ |" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228752 1069 "e-19555" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228752 1071 "A librarian recorded the number of books read by each student in a reading club. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Student | Books read |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 1 | $5$ |\n| 2 | $8$ |\n| 3 | $6$ |\n| 4 | $9$ |\n| 5 | $4$ |\n| 6 | $7$ |\n| 7 | $10$ |\n\nWhat is the range of the number of books read among the $7$ students?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228752 1072 #uuid "78b2d0b5-bb74-5cd2-a454-d31f502c291a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228752 1074 "The range of a data set is the difference between the greatest and smallest values.\n\nIn this case, the greatest value is $10$ and the smallest value is $4$.\n\nTherefore, the range of the number of books read is\n$10 - 4 = 6$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228753 1069 "e-19557" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228753 1071 "| Streaming platform | Average monthly users (millions) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Platform X | $215$ |\n| Platform Y | $160$ |\n| Platform Z | $95$ |\n\nBased on the table above, how many more million users does Platform X have compared to Platform Z?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228753 1072 #uuid "56e40e0f-82a4-5de3-a60d-c162d65a5aeb" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228753 1074 "To determine how many more million users Platform X has than Platform Z, we subtract the number of users on Platform Z from the number on Platform X.\n\nFrom the table, we can see that\n\n- Platform X has $215$ million users, and\n- Platform Z has $95$ million users.\n\nTherefore, Platform X has\n\n$$\n215 - 95 = 120\n$$\n\nmore million users than Platform Z." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228754 1069 "e-19561" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228754 1071 "| Rent | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $7$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $3$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $10$ | $5$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |\n| $15$ | $1$ |\n\nThe table above shows the monthly rental costs (in hundreds of dollars) for $20$ units in an apartment complex. Which of the following gives the correct order of the mean, median, and mode of the rent amounts?\n\n1. $\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}$\n2. $\\text{median} < \\text{mean}$\n3. $\\text{mean} < \\text{median}$\n4. $\\text{median} = \\text{mean} = \\text{mode}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228754 1072 #uuid "5a781e2b-8de0-5e83-9499-df73129dea12" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228754 1074 "We proceed to calculate the mean, median, and mode separately.\n\n- The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.Therefore, for our data set, the mean is\n$mean|= (7(2) + 8(3) + 9(6) + 10(5) + 11(3) + 15(1))/(20); = \\frac{14 + 24 + 54 + 50 + 33 + 15}{20}; = \\frac{190}{20}; = 9.5$.\n- The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.Since there are $20$ rent amounts ordered, the median is the average of the two middle values, the $10$th and $11$th, which for these data are both $9$. Then, the median is\n$median|= \\frac{9 + 9}{2}; = \\frac{18}{2}; = 9$.\n- The mode is the value that occurs most often in the data set.For the data shown, we have\n$\\textrm{mode} = 9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct order is\n\n$$\n\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228755 1069 "e-19563" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228755 1071 "The table below summarizes the frequency of values in a data set.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $99$ | $3$ |\n| $101$ | $2$ |\n| $98$ | $4$ |\n| $100$ | $1$ |\n| $97$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the minimum value of the data set?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228755 1072 #uuid "6ef5cfe3-9104-56d8-88cd-8b08b3b3d789" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228755 1074 "To find the minimum value of the data in the set, we must identify the smallest number in the \"Value\" column of the table.\n\nThe table shows that the unique values in the data set are $99, 101, 98, 100$, and $97$. Of these values, $97$ is the smallest value that appears at least once.\n\nTherefore, the smallest data value that appears in the data set is ${97}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228756 1069 "e-19566" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228756 1071 "The table below shows the number of daily users for each of the $35$ different mobile apps reviewed in a tech report.\n\n| Number of daily users | Number of apps |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $100$ to $199$ | $5$ |\n| $200$ to $299$ | $4$ |\n| $300$ to $399$ | $6$ |\n| $400$ to $499$ | $5$ |\n| $500$ to $599$ | $6$ |\n| $600$ to $699$ | $5$ |\n| $700$ to $799$ | $4$ |\n\nWhich of the following could be the median number of daily users per app?\n\n1. $450$\n2. $350$\n3. $600$\n4. $250$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228756 1072 #uuid "d8fb7e69-a0b7-55e4-834b-7cc8a0cce5e4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228756 1074 "The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nSince there are $35$ different apps included in the data set, and the numbers of daily users are listed in ascending order, it follows that the $18$th app in the list will represent the median number of users.\n\nNow, let's find the cumulative frequencies to locate the $18$th app.\n\n- The first $5$ apps have $100$ to $199$ users (apps $1-5$).\n- The next $4$ apps have $200$ to $299$ users (apps $6-9$).\n- The next $6$ apps have $300$ to $399$ users (apps $10-15$).\n- The next $5$ apps have $400$ to $499$ users (apps $16-20$).\n- The next $6$ apps have $500$ to $599$ users (apps $21-26$).\n- The next $5$ apps have $600$ to $699$ users (apps $27-31$).\n- The last $4$ apps have $700$ to $799$ users (apps $32-35$).\n\nThe $18$th app falls within the range of $400$ to $499$ users, because apps $16$ through $20$ are in this category.\n\nSince the median falls within the $400$ to $499$ range, the median number of users could be any value within this range. Therefore, among the answer options given, $450$ could be the median number of daily users per app." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228757 1069 "e-19567" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228757 1071 "| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $9$ | $z$ |\n| $19$ | $3z$ |\n| $29$ | $6z$ |\n| $39$ | $3z$ |\n| $49$ | $z$ |\n\nThe frequency table above displays a set of data, where $z$ is a positive integer. By how much does the mean of the data set exceed the median?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228757 1072 #uuid "7704f6a6-43bf-50d0-b0d7-8104530fb161" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228757 1074 "We observe that the set of data has a distribution symmetric around the central value of $29$. Indeed,\n\n- $9$ and $49$ each have a frequency of $z$,\n- $19$ and $39$ each have a frequency of $3z$, and\n- $29$ has a frequency of $6z$.\n\nIn any symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal. Consequently, the mean does not exceed the median.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $0$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228758 1069 "e-19568" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228758 1071 "Each of the following frequency tables represents a data set. Which one has the greatest mean? Round to two decimal places where necessary.\n\n**I.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |\n\n**II.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $2$ |\n\n**III.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $1$ |\n\n**IV.**\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $1$ |" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228758 1072 #uuid "132c6b44-c9b7-5c12-a63d-e82210333b23" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228758 1074 "The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nWe proceed to determine the mean of the data sets from each of the given frequency tables.\n\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$1$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$3$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(1) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{61}{8}; \\approx 7.63$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$2$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(2))/(8); = \\frac{52}{8}; = 6.5$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$3$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$1$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(3) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{43}{8}; \\approx 5.38$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$3$$8$$2$$11$$1$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(2) + 5(3) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{46}{8}; = 5.75$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$2$$8$$1$$11$$3$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(1) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{55}{8}; \\approx 6.88$.\n\nTherefore, the frequency table whose data set has the greatest mean is as follows:\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228759 1069 "e-19570" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228759 1071 "A bag contains $5$ yellow marbles, $4$ red marbles, and $9$ blue marbles. If one marble is selected at random from the bag, what is the probability of selecting a red marble?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228759 1072 #uuid "70bfb1a7-f461-5488-8c79-1298c8d0f2e6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228759 1074 "The probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n\nWe are told that a bag contains $5$ yellow, $4$ red, and $9$ blue marbles. So, the total number of marbles in the bag is\n\n$$\n5 + 4 + 9 = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the number of favorable outcomes (red marbles) is $4$, and the total number of possible outcomes (all marbles) is $18$, the desired probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{The selected marble is red}) = \\dfrac{4}{18} = \\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228760 1069 "e-19572" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228760 1071 "Consider the following data set of three numbers:\n$-4, \\: 2, \\: 5$\nIf a number from this data set is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a positive number?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228760 1072 #uuid "c3dab105-38fc-5164-a8c2-7e6b23d070b0" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228760 1074 "The probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n\nWe are told that a data set contains three numbers: $-4, 2$, and $5$, of which $2$ and $5$ are positive.\n\nTherefore, since the number of favorable outcomes (positive numbers) is $2$, and the total number of possible outcomes (all numbers in the set) is $3$, the desired probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{The selected number is positive}) = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228761 1069 "e-19580" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228761 1071 "A cup contains $6$ black pens, $11$ blue pens, $9$ red pens, and $4$ green pens. If one pen is randomly selected from the cup, what is the probability that it is not black or red?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228761 1072 #uuid "75ab75ff-0619-5d98-9b5b-667777cc3ab6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228761 1074 "The probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n\nWe are told that a cup contains $6$ black, $11$ blue, $9$ red, and $4$ green pens. The total number of pens in the cup is\n\n$$\n6 + 11 + 9 + 4 = 30\n$$\n\nWe are also told that the randomly selected pen is neither black nor red, which means it must be either blue or green. The number of pens that are either blue or green is\n\n$$\n11 + 4 = 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the number of favorable outcomes (blue or green pens) is $15$, and the total number of possible outcomes (all pens) is $30$, the desired probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{The selected pen is not black or red}) = \\dfrac{15}{30} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228762 1069 "e-19958" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228762 1071 "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{-2}{q + 1} + \\dfrac{5}{q - 4}\\right)}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228762 1072 #uuid "3dd3aede-14f4-5bc8-82f4-04cc31beae9e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228762 1074 "In order to convert this to a simple fraction, we must obtain a common denominator for our fractions in the denominator of the entire fraction. The common denominator is $(q + 1)(q - 4)$, as the terms are distinct linear factors.\n\nWe now multiply the entire fraction by $1$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(q + 1)(q - 4)}{(q + 1)(q - 4)}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n(1)/((\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4})) \\cdot ((q + 1)(q - 4))/((q + 1)(q - 4))\n$$\n\n- First, we simplify the numerator: $1\\cdot(q + 1)(q - 4) = q^2 - 3q - 4$\n- Then, we simplify the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4})(q + 1)(q - 4) &= (-2(q + 1)(q - 4))/(q + 1) + (5(q + 1)(q - 4))/(q - 4) \\\\\n&=-2(q - 4) + 5(q + 1) \\\\\n&=-2q + 8 + 5q + 5 \\\\\n&= 3q + 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the full simplified fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{q^2 - 3q - 4}{3q + 13}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228763 1069 "e-19959" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228763 1071 "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the expression?\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4\\right)}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228763 1072 #uuid "f4da0fb3-c486-537e-9704-bcfc6c1059f7" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228763 1074 "In order to convert this to a simple fraction, we must obtain a common denominator for our fractions in the denominator of the entire fraction. The common denominator is $r + 1$, as it is the only denominator of the terms in the denominator of the whole expression.\n\nWe now multiply the entire fraction by $1$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r + 1}{r + 1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n(1)/((\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4)) \\cdot \\frac{r + 1}{r + 1}\n$$\n\n- First, we simplify the numerator: $1 \\cdot (r + 1) = r + 1$\n- Then, we simplify the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4) \\cdot (r + 1) &= (2(r + 1))/(r + 1) + (r - 4)(r + 1) \\\\\n&= 2 + r^{2} + r - 4r - 4 \\\\\n&= r^{2} - 3r - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the full simplified fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r + 1}{r^2 - 3r - 2}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228764 1069 "e-19968" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228764 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $3x - \\dfrac{x + y}{x}$, where $x > 0$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{2x + y}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{3x^2 + x - y}{x}$\n3. $\\dfrac{3x^2 - x - y}{x}$\n4. $\\dfrac{3x^2 - x + y}{x}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228764 1072 #uuid "438e8572-1d21-5419-953f-f949929519a5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228764 1074 "Notice that all the answer choices are single fractions, so we start by obtaining a common denominator for our expression. Since\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{LCM}(1, x) = x\n$$\n\nour common denominator is $x$. We may rewrite our expression by multiplying the numerator and denominator of our first term by $x$ and simplifying as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - \\frac{x + y}{x} &= \\frac{x}{x} \\cdot 3x - \\frac{x + y}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3x^{2}}{x} - \\frac{x + y}{x} \\\\\n&= (3x^{2} - (x + y))/(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3x^{2} - x - y}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"III.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228765 1069 "e-19969" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228765 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{3}{m}+\\dfrac{5}{4}$, where $m\\neq0$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{12+5m}{4m}$\n2. $\\dfrac{3+20m}{4m}$\n3. $\\dfrac{12+5m}{4}$\n4. $\\dfrac{15+4m}{4m}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228765 1072 #uuid "b86de64f-a038-5da0-953d-88c8daf250db" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228765 1074 "Here, we wish to add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{m}+\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we must first obtain a common denominator. As $m$ is a variable, our common denominator is\n\n$$\nm\\cdot 4=4m\n$$\n\nand so we multiply the top and bottom of our first fraction by $4$, and multiply the top and bottom of our second fraction by $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{m} + \\frac{5}{4} &= \\frac{4}{4} \\cdot \\frac{3}{m} + \\frac{m}{m} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{4m} + \\frac{5m}{4m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 + 5m}{4m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the result is already simplified, this is our final answer which is expression \"I.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228766 1069 "e-19975" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228766 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to\n$\\dfrac{4s + 8}{r - 4} + \\dfrac{s(r - 4)}{r^2 s - 4 r s}$,\nwhere $r > 4$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{4rs + 9r + 4}{r(r - 4)}$\n2. $\\dfrac{4rs + 9r - 4}{r(r - 4)}$\n3. $\\dfrac{4s + 8 + r - 4}{r(r - 4)}$\n4. $\\dfrac{4s(r - 4) + 1}{r(r - 4)}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228766 1072 #uuid "5b367b64-b5c1-56a6-a6bf-58d3480ecea1" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228766 1074 "We are asked to simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4s + 8}{r - 4} + \\dfrac{s(r - 4)}{r^2 s - 4 r s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we simplify the first term by factoring out $4$ from the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4s + 8}{r - 4} = \\dfrac{4(s + 2)}{r - 4}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the second term by factoring out $rs$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s(r - 4))/(r^{2}s - 4rs) &= (s(r - 4))/(rs(r - 4)) \\\\\n&= (s(r - 4))/(rs(r - 4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r > 4$, we know\n\n$$\nr-4 \\ne 0\n$$\n\n, we can cancel the common factor $(r - 4)$.\n\nNow we add the final form of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4(s + 2)}{r - 4} + \\dfrac{1}{r}\n$$\n\nTo add the rational expressions, we first find a common denominator, which is the product of the two denominators $x(r - 4)$.\n\nNow we rewrite each fraction with the common denominator, then combine the expressions by adding the numerators over the common denominator and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4(s + 2))/(r - 4) + \\frac{1}{r} &= (4r(s + 2))/(r(r - 4)) + (r - 4)/(r(r - 4)) \\\\\n&= (4r(s + 2) + (r - 4))/(r(r - 4)) \\\\\n&= (4rs + 8r + r - 4)/(r(r - 4)) \\\\\n&= (4rs + 9r - 4)/(r(r - 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4s + 8}{r - 4} + \\dfrac{s(r - 4)}{r^2 s - 4 r s} = \\dfrac{4rs + 9r - 4}{r(r - 4)}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is expression \"II.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228767 1069 "e-19977" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228767 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{4}{3y+1}+\\dfrac{5}{y-2}$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{19y + 3}{(3y+1)(y-2)}$\n2. $-\\dfrac{19y - 3}{(3y+1)(y-2)}$\n3. $\\dfrac{3y - 19}{(3y+1)(y-2)}$\n4. $\\dfrac{19y - 3}{(3y+1)(y-2)}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228767 1072 #uuid "301ad141-81dd-525d-a152-c9a7952a3a99" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228767 1074 "First, we observe that the denominators are not factorable, and the expressions are already in lowest terms. To simplify this sum, we must obtain a common denominator. Since\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{GCF}(3y+1, y-2) = 1\n$$\n\nour common denominator is $(3y+1)(y-2)$. We rewrite the expression as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{3y + 1} + \\frac{5}{y - 2} &= (4(y - 2))/((3y + 1)(y - 2)) + (5(3y + 1))/((3y + 1)(y - 2)) \\\\\n&= (4y - 8 + 15y + 5)/((3y + 1)(y - 2)) \\\\\n&= (19y - 3)/((3y + 1)(y - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our equivalent expression is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{19y - 3}{(3y+1)(y-2)}\n$$\n\nwhich is expression \"IV.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228768 1069 "e-20493" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228768 1071 "A museum surveyed visitors about four exhibits, asking each to choose the one they enjoyed most. Each response was submitted either at an on-site kiosk or via an email link. The table below shows the distribution of preferences, in percent, by response method. The total number of email responses was four times the number of kiosk responses.\n\n|  | Kiosk | Email |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Sculpture | $18\\%$ | $22\\%$ |\n| Painting | $37\\%$ | $28\\%$ |\n| Science | $22\\%$ | $30\\%$ |\n| History | $23\\%$ | $20\\%$ |\n| **Total** | **$100\\%$** | **$100\\%$** |\n\nIf a visitor who preferred the sculpture exhibit is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor responded via email?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228768 1072 #uuid "cc1e41f4-ad88-5505-b6cd-572f543bb73e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228768 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of visitors who preferred the sculpture exhibit and responded via email to the total number of visitors who preferred the sculpture exhibit. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Email} \\mid \\text{Sculpture}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of sculpture preferences via email}}{\\text{Total number preferring sculpture}}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of kiosk responses. Since the number of email responses was four times the number of kiosk responses, the number of email responses is $4x$.\n\nWe begin by calculating the total number of visitors who preferred the sculpture exhibit.\n\n- From the table, we can see that $18\\%$ of kiosk respondents preferred the sculpture exhibit. This can be represented by\n$0.18 \\cdot x = 0.18x$.\n- From the table, we can see that $22\\%$ of email respondents preferred the sculpture exhibit. This can be represented by\n$0.22 \\cdot 4x = 0.88x$.\n\nTherefore, the total number of visitors who preferred the sculpture exhibit is\n\n$$\n0.18x + 0.88x = {\\color{red}1.06x}\n$$\n\nOf these, ${\\color{blue}0.88x}$ is the number of email respondents who preferred the sculpture exhibit.\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Email∣Sculpture) &= \\frac{0.88x}{1.06x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{88x}{106x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 44x}{2 \\cdot 53x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 44x}{2 \\cdot 53x} \\\\\n&= 44 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228769 1069 "e-20494" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228769 1071 "A bird sanctuary contains $8$ forest paths and $3$ riverside paths. Each forest path has $9$ sightings of owls and $6$ sightings of woodpeckers. Each riverside path has $5$ sightings of owls and $7$ sightings of woodpeckers. A sighting from one of these paths is selected at random. What is the probability that the sighting occurred on a riverside path, given that the animal spotted was an owl?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228769 1072 #uuid "49ad3511-c4cd-5427-8fd6-09f710d164c6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228769 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of sightings that occurred on a riverside path and were of an owl to the total number of sightings of an owl.\nThis is given by\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Riverside path} \\mid \\text{Owl spotted}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of owl sightings on riverside paths}}{\\text{Total number of owl sightings}}\n$$\n\nWe begin by calculating the total number of owl sightings.\n\n- Each of the $8$ forest paths had $9$ owl sightings. So, the number of owl sightings on the forest paths is $8 \\times 9 = 72$.\n- Each of the $3$ riverside paths had $5$ owl sightings. Thus, the number of owl sightings on the riverside paths is $3 \\times 5 = 15$.\n\nTherefore, the total number of owl sightings is\n\n$$\n72 + 15 = {\\color{red}87}\n$$\n\nOf these, ${\\color{blue}15}$ occurred on riverside paths.\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Riverside path∣Owl spotted) &= \\frac{15}{87} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 3}{29 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 3}{29 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228770 1069 "e-20496" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228770 1071 "An electronics shop records whether a buyer’s main purchase was a laptop or a tablet, and how the order was placed. The data is summarized in the table below.\n\n|  | In-store | Online | Phone |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Laptops | $35$ | $20$ | $12$ |\n| Tablets | $21$ | $14$ | $x$ |\n\nIf a person who bought a tablet is selected at random, the probability that the person ordered by online is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228770 1072 #uuid "4cbe755a-e2ad-5422-bbac-80dbf84bf0eb" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228770 1074 "To find the value of $x$, we use the given conditional probability: the probability that a person ordered by online, given they bought a tablet, is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThe conditional probability is calculated as the ratio of the number of people who ordered by phone and bought a tablet to the total number of people who bought a tablet. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of tablet buyers who ordered by online}}{\\text{Total number who bought a tablet}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that there are\n\n$$\n21 + 14 + x = {\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\npeople who bought a tablet. Of these, ${\\color{blue}14}$ buyers ordered online.\n\n|  | In-store | Online | Phone |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Laptops | $35$ | $20$ | $12$ |\n| Tablets | $21$ | $\\color{blue}14$ | $x$ |\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}14}{\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\nSince this must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{14}{35 + x} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{35 + x}{14} &= \\frac{6}{1} \\\\\n35 + x &= 84 \\\\\nx &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228771 1069 "e-20497" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228771 1071 "A group of $30$ students, during each of $3$ different practice exams, answered $5$ questions. Each correct response was worth $1$ point. The table shows how many students earned each possible score on each exam.\n\n| Number of Students by Score and Exam | $5$ out of $5$ | $4$ out of $5$ | $3$ out of $5$ | $2$ out of $5$ | $1$ out of $5$ | $0$ out of $5$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Exam $1$ | $6$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| **Total** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$15$** | **$14$** | **$18$** | **$17$** | **$90$** |\n\nNo student received the same score on two different exams. If a student is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student earned a perfect score on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$, given that the student earned a perfect score on one of the three exams?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228771 1072 #uuid "b93f19b9-a2a1-568e-8492-9339d433454f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228771 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of students who received a score of $5$ on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$ to the total number of students who received a score of $5$ on one of the three exams. This is given by\n$1$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of ${\\color{red}12}$ students received a score of $5$. Of this amount,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} + {\\color{purple}4} = 10\n$$\n\nstudents received that score in either Exam $1$ or Exam $3$.\n\n| Number of Students by Score and Exam | $5$ out of $5$ | $4$ out of $5$ | $3$ out of $5$ | $2$ out of $5$ | $1$ out of $5$ | $0$ out of $5$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Exam $1$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $3$ | $\\color{purple}4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| **Total** | ${\\color{red}12}$ | **$14$** | **$15$** | **$14$** | **$18$** | **$17$** | **$90$** |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Exam1or3∣Perfect Score) &= \\frac{6 + 4}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228772 1069 "e-20500" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228772 1071 "The table below shows the number of teachers in the English and History departments, categorized by whether they teach honors classes.\n\n|  | English | History |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Teach honors | $8$ | $5$ |\n| Do not teach honors | $4$ | $7$ |\n\nOne teacher from the English or History department is selected at random. If the teacher is from the English department, what is the probability that they do not teach honors classes?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228772 1072 #uuid "bed86e31-3eb3-5b59-ac40-a8de9d24f82c" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228772 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of English teachers who do not teach honors to the total number of English teachers. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Not honors} \\mid \\text{English})=\\frac{\\text{Number of English not teaching honors}}{\\text{Total number of English}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of\n\n$$\n8+4={\\color{red}12}\n$$\n\nEnglish teachers in the department. Of this amount, ${\\color{blue}4}$ do not teach honors.\n\n|  | English | History |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Teach honors | $8$ | $5$ |\n| Do not teach honors | $\\color{blue}4$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Not honors∣English) &= \\frac{4}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 4}{3 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 4}{3 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228773 1069 "e-20507" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228773 1071 "A veterinary clinic tracked incoming calls on a single day, recording whether callers inquired about appointments or pet food. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n|  | Pet food request | No pet food request | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Appointment request | $55$ | $345$ | $400$ |\n| No Appointment request | $45$ | $55$ | $100$ |\n| **Total** | **$100$** | **$400$** | **$500$** |\n\nIf a caller who asked about pet food is selected at random, what is the probability that the caller also asked about an appointment?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228773 1072 #uuid "630c3a2f-b1b6-54e0-a3fd-417c74ab53e8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228773 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of callers who asked about pet food and an appointment to the total number of callers who asked about pet food. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Appointment} \\mid \\text{Pet food}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of requests for appointment and pet food}}{\\text{Total number of requests for pet food}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that there are a total of ${\\color{red}100}$ callers who asked about pet food. Of this amount, ${\\color{blue}55}$ also asked about an appointment.\n\n|  | Pet food request | No pet food request | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Appointment request | $\\color{blue}55$ | $345$ | $400$ |\n| No Appointment request | $45$ | $55$ | $100$ |\n| **Total** | ${\\color{red}100}$ | **$400$** | **$500$** |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Appointment∣Pet food) &= \\frac{55}{100} \\\\\n&= 0.55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228774 1069 "e-20552" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228774 1071 "A sleep researcher measured the nightly sleep duration of adults. A random sample of $520$ adults was analyzed, and the estimated mean sleep duration was $7.3$ hours, with a margin of error of $0.4$ hours.\n\nWhich of the following interpretations is most reasonable?\n\n1. Plausible values for the mean sleep duration of all adults are less than $6.9$ hours or greater than $7.7$ hours.\n2. The mean sleep duration of all adults is less than $7$ hours.\n3. Plausible values for the mean sleep duration of all adults are between $6.9$ hours and $7.7$ hours.\n4. The mean sleep duration of all adults is greater than $7$ hours." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228774 1072 #uuid "84c03e2c-a46a-559c-80f5-25946303ce53" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228774 1074 "The estimated mean sleep duration is $7.3$ hours with a margin of error of $0.4$ hours. We calculate the plausible bounds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{Lower bound} = 7.3 - 0.4 = 6.9\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Upper bound} = 7.3 + 0.4 = 7.7\n$$\n\nThe margin of error bounds the *population mean*, not individual sleep durations, so it gives a plausible interval for the true average across all adults.\n\nWith that in mind, we have the following:\n\n- Statement I is false, because values outside this range are not plausible for the mean.\n- Statement II is false, because the interval includes values above $7$.\n- Statement III is true, because the interval is $6.9$ to $7.7$.\n- Statement IV is false, because the interval includes values below $7$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228775 1069 "e-20553" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228775 1071 "In a survey, a random sample of $1{,}200$ households with cable television was selected. Based on the survey, it is estimated that $38\\%$ of all households with cable television watch sports channels regularly, with a margin of error of $3.5\\%$.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions about all households with cable television is most reasonable?\n\n1. Between $34.5\\%$ and $41.5\\%$ of all households with cable television watch sports channels regularly.\n2. More than $41.5\\%$ of all households with cable television watch sports channels regularly.\n3. Since the survey included households with cable television and not just those who watch sports channels, no conclusion can be made.\n4. Since the survey did not include every household that watches sports channels, no conclusion can be made." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228775 1072 #uuid "b3982198-b203-59ad-8c81-964676e7c6a8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228775 1074 "The estimated proportion of households that watch sports channels regularly is $38\\%$ with a margin of error of $3.5\\%$. We calculate the plausible bounds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{Lower bound} = 38\\% - 3.5\\% = 34.5\\%\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Upper bound} = 38\\% + 3.5\\% = 41.5\\%\n$$\n\nThe margin of error bounds the *population proportion*, not individual households, so it is normal that not every household is included in the sample. The results allow us to make inferences about the whole population of households with cable television.\n\nWith that in mind, we have the following:\n\n- Statement I is true, because the plausible interval is $34.5\\%$ to $41.5\\%$.\n- Statement II is false, because the upper bound is exactly $41.5\\%$, not greater.\n- Statement III is false, because the sample was designed to represent households with cable television, so conclusions can be drawn.\n- Statement IV is false, because not surveying every household is typical; that is precisely why margins of error are reported.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228776 1069 "e-20555" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228776 1071 "A health survey estimated that the proportion of adults in a city who meet weekly exercise guidelines is $0.36$, with a margin of error of $0.04$.\n\nBased on this information, what conclusion can reasonably be made about true population proportion?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228776 1072 #uuid "40f2489b-3e68-5c94-9c4f-b5c14cf46fc3" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228776 1074 "The estimated proportion of adults meeting exercise guidelines is $0.36$ with a margin of error of $0.04$. We calculate the plausible bounds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{Lower bound}=0.36-0.04=0.32\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Upper bound}=0.36+0.04=0.40\n$$\n\nThe margin of error provides a plausible interval for the *population proportion*, not an exact value, so values outside this interval are **not** considered plausible for the true proportion.\n\nTherefore, it is plausible that the true proportion is between $0.32$ and $0.40$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228777 1069 "e-20561" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228777 1071 "In a midterm exam, each student receives a score between $50$ and $100$. The table below shows the score distribution for the $13$ students in Class $M$ and the $11$ students in Class $N$.\n\n| Score | Class $M$ | Class $N$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $50$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $55$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $60$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $65$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $70$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n| $75$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $80$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $90$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $100$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| Total | $13$ | $11$ |\n\nThe median score for Class $N$ is how many points greater than the median score for Class $M$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228777 1072 #uuid "6645ac8d-7fcd-54bf-af55-e67556f188da" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228777 1074 "The median of a data set is the middle value, meaning half of the data points lie above it and half lie below it, when the values are arranged from smallest to largest.\n\nSince the table shows the frequencies of the scores for each class, to list the data set we write each score from the table, repeating it as many times as its frequency indicates.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to find the median for each data:\n\n- Class $M$ has $13$ students, which can be listed from lowest to highest as:\n$50,\\: 55,\\: 60,\\: 60,\\: 65,\\: 65,\\: 70,\\: 70,\\: 70,\\: 75,\\: 75,\\: 80,\\: 90$\nSince there are $13$ scores (an odd number), the median is the $7$th element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(M) = 70$\n- Class $N$ has $11$ students, which can be listed from lowest to highest as:\n$55,\\: 60,\\: 65,\\: 70,\\: 70,\\: 75,\\: 75,\\: 75,\\: 80,\\: 90,\\: 100$\nSince there are $11$ scores (an odd number), the median is the $6$th element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(N) = 75$\n\nSo, the difference between the medians is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian(N) - Median(M) &= 75 - 70 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median score for Class $N$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{5}$ points greater than the median score for Class $M$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228778 1069 "e-20562" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228778 1071 "| Value | Data set A frequency | Data set B frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $12$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $16$ | $6$ | $5$ |\n| $20$ | $7$ | $6$ |\n| $24$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| $28$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nData set $A$ and data set $B$ each contain $27$ values. The table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set. Which of the following statements best compares the means of the two data sets? Round to two decimal places where necessary.\n\n1. The mean of the data set $A$ is less than the mean of the data set $B$.\n2. The mean of the data set $A$ is equal to the mean of the data set $B$.\n3. The mean of the data set $A$ is greater than the mean of the data set $B$.\n4. There is not enough information to compare the means of the two datasets." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228778 1072 #uuid "3c1eab1d-87ef-5d90-9418-d3d4c734b85f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228778 1074 "The mean (or average) in a data set is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nSince the table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set, we find the total sum by multiplying each data value by its frequency and then adding all these products together.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean of each data set:\n\n- The mean of the data set $A$ is\n$Mean(A)|= (12(5) + 16(6) + 20(7) + 24(5) + 28(4))/(27); = \\frac{528}{27}; \\approx 19.56$,\nrounded to two decimal places.\n- The mean of the data set $B$ is\n$Mean(B)|= (12(3) + 16(5) + 20(6) + 24(7) + 28(6))/(27); = \\frac{572}{27}; \\approx 21.19$,\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(A) < \\textrm{Mean}(B)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228779 1069 "e-20564" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228779 1071 "For which of the following data sets is the mean greater than the median?\n\n1. $12,\\: 12,\\: 12,\\: 12,\\: 12,\\: 12,\\: 12$\n2. $3,\\: 6,\\: 12,\\: 24,\\: 48,\\: 96,\\: 192$\n3. $15,\\: 25,\\: 35,\\: 45,\\: 55,\\: 65,\\: 75$\n4. $7,\\: 17,\\: 27,\\: 37,\\: 47,\\: 57,\\: 67$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228779 1072 #uuid "acb398d6-d5be-5d5a-b354-bcff83499a8a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228779 1074 "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set.\n\n- Data set I is symmetric with $7$ values (an odd number). Thus, the mean and median of this data set are the $4$th element, which is equal to $12$.\n- Data set II is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean|= \\frac{3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + 48 + 96 + 192}{7}; \\approx 54.4$.\nOn the other hand, since it contains $7$ ordered from smallest to greatest elements, its median is\n$\\textrm{Median} = 24$.\nSo, $\\text{Mean}>\\text{Median}$.\n- Data set III is symmetric with $7$ values (an odd number). Thus, the mean and median of this data set are the $4$th element, which is equal to $45$.\n- Data set IV is symmetric with $7$ values (an odd number). Thus, the mean and median of this data set are the $4$th element, which is equal to $37$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228780 1069 "e-20567" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228780 1071 "| Museum | $2012$ | $2013$ | $2014$ | $2015$ | $2016$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| North Museum | $540$ | $460$ | $520$ | $500$ | $480$ |\n| South Museum | $300$ | $480$ | $500$ | $510$ | $520$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of visitors at two museums in each of the five years. No visitor was counted at both museums.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the number of visitors over the $5$ years shown?\n\n1. The mean number from North Museum is greater than the mean number from South Museum.\n2. The median number from North Museum is greater than the median number from South Museum." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228780 1072 #uuid "220ca44a-bacb-5a06-b1cf-77cdc1c69cd8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228780 1074 "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set associated with each museum.\n\n- North Museum data points are not ordered, so we order them first from smallest to largest:\n$480,\\: 500,\\: 520, \\: 540, \\: 560$\nThis data set is symmetric and contains $5$ elements, which is an odd number, so the mean and median are both equal to the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(N) = \\textrm{Median}(N) = 520$\n- South Museum data points are already ordered from smallest to largest:\n$300, \\: 480, \\: 500, \\: 510, \\: 520$\nThis data set is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(S)|= \\frac{300 + 480 + 500 + 510 + 520}{5}; = \\frac{2310}{5}; = 462$.\nSince it contains $5$ elements, its median is the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(S) = 500$\n\nSince North Museum has a higher mean and median than South Museum, both statements are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228781 1069 "e-20569" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228781 1071 "The data $P$ is given by\n$8, \\: 12, \\: 12, \\: 16$,\nand the data set $Q$ is given by\n$8, \\: 12, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: 32$.\nWrite the statement that compares the means of data set $P$ and data set $Q$?\n\n1. The mean of the data set $P$ is less than the mean of the data set $Q$.\n2. The mean of the data set $P$ is greater than the mean of the data set $Q$.\n3. The means of the data set $P$ and data set $Q$ are equal.\n4. The mean of each data set is $15$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228781 1072 #uuid "a23e764d-5586-5e1d-b096-fc59f9a6e305" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228781 1074 "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $P$ is symmetric with $4$ data values in ascending order; it follows that the mean is between the second and third data values, that is, its mean is $12$.\n- Data set $Q$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(Q)|= \\frac{8 + 12 + 12 + 16 + 32}{5}; = \\frac{80}{5}; = 16$.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(P) < \\textrm{Mean}(Q)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228782 1069 "e-20570" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228782 1071 "The data set $G$ is given by\n$52, \\: 55, \\: 58, \\: 61, \\: 64$,\nand the data set $H$ is given by\n$40, \\: 57, \\: 60, \\: 63, \\: y$,\ncontain $5$ values each. If the mean of data set $G$ is the same as the mean of data set $H$, what is the value of $y$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228782 1072 #uuid "41010a1d-e45a-589c-828d-f8ffc5514c48" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228782 1074 "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $G$ is symmetric with $5$ values (an odd number), so its mean is the $3$rd data value in the list:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(G) = 58$\n- Data set $H$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(H)|= \\frac{40 + 57 + 60 + 63 + y}{5}; = \\frac{220 + y}{5}$.\n\nSince the mean of the data set $G$ is the same as the mean of the data set $H$, we equate them and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(H) &= Mean(G) \\\\\n\\frac{220 + y}{5} &= 58 \\\\\n220 + y &= 58 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n220 + y &= 290 \\\\\ny &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $70$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228783 1069 "e-20575" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228783 1071 "![](images/fb/fb1b7bf451f90f2be8877470b5327621b1bcf168b736fc75d72224e6e4c1355a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. The median of data set $A$ is equal to the median of data set $B$.\n2. The standard deviation of data set $A$ is equal to the standard deviation of data set $B$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228783 1072 #uuid "992543d0-e249-553f-b1f5-51bd3237f04a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228783 1074 "In a dot plot, the median is the middle value when the data are ordered.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I:Each data set has $6$ values, an even number, so the median is the average of the $3$rd and $4$th values. For data set $A$: the values in order are $91,91,92,94,95,95$ so the $3$rd and $4$th values are $92$ and $94$, making the median $93$.For data set $B$: identical to data set $A$, so the median is also $93$. Thus, the medians are equal, and statement I is true.\n- Statement II:Since the plots are identical, both the means and the spreads are the same. Thus, their standard deviations are equal, and statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I and II.”" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228784 1069 "e-20576" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228784 1071 "The two box plots show the distribution of fuel efficiencies (in miles per gallon) measured for two car models.\n\nWhat is the positive difference between the ranges of the fuel efficiencies for the two models?\n\n![](images/b8/b817b463f2497fe90ead95728f8aa94b0fc68f4b531073571976b4770df10087.png)" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228784 1072 #uuid "299c8ff1-9174-51ca-ab18-a0cf88fc2024" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228784 1074 "The range of a data set equals the difference between the maximum and the minimum.\n\n- For Model $A$, the maximum is $42$ and the minimum is $22$, so the range is\n$\\text{Range}(A) = 42 - 22 = 20$.\n- For Model $B$, the maximum is $38$ and the minimum is $18$, so the range is\n$\\text{Range}(B) = 38 - 18 = 20$.\n\nSubtracting, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\bigl|\\text{Range}(A) - \\text{Range}(B)\\bigr| = \\mid 20 - 20 \\mid = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the positive difference is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228785 1069 "e-20577" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228785 1071 "The box plots summarize the lengths, in centimeters, of two groups of river fish.\n\n![](images/47/4737910c00c3c40dc4db2b118ebcb787cca71c66b8fc334c6b7c3cd7393a649a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. The median length of Group $B$ is greater than the median length of Group $A$.\n2. The median length of Group $A$ is greater than the median length of Group $B$.\n3. The mean length of Group $A$ is greater than the mean length of Group $B$.\n4. The mean length of Group $B$ is greater than the mean length of Group $A$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228785 1072 #uuid "7349ba76-f4a2-5c8f-a2be-9d0ea19dc400" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228785 1074 "In a box plot:\n\n- The median is the thick line inside the box, so medians can be read directly.\n- The mean cannot be determined from a box plot alone.\n\nFrom the plots:\n\n- Group $A$’s median is at $33 \\text{cm}$.\n- Group $B$’s median is at $37 \\text{cm}$.\n\nTherefore, the median for Group $B$ is greater than the median for Group $A$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228786 1069 "e-20578" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228786 1071 "The dot plots above show the times (in minutes) it took students to complete a mile run in two different PE classes, Group $P$ and Group $Q$. Which statement best compares the standard deviations of the two groups?\n\n![](images/a2/a27beed9d500a62ebe3445e467f8fdc7feddfecfbeb4e477648701e8ae06a67e.png)\n\n1. The standard deviation for Group $P$ is equal to the standard deviation for Group $Q$.\n2. The standard deviation for Group $P$ is less than the standard deviation for Group $Q$.\n3. The standard deviation for Group $P$ is greater than the standard deviation for Group $Q$.\n4. There is not enough information to compare these standard deviations." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228786 1072 #uuid "1603217e-281b-523f-af3d-fcdad92f0878" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228786 1074 "Standard deviation is a measure of the spread of a data set from its mean.\n\nThe two dot plots have identical shapes and frequencies. Group $Q$ is obtained by adding the constant $5$ to every value in Group $P$, which produces a horizontal shift to the right.\n\nAdding $5$ to all data values increases the mean by $5$ but does not affect deviations from the mean, so the standard deviation remains unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is that “The standard deviation for Group $P$ is equal to the standard deviation for Group $Q$.”" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228787 1069 "e-20579" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228787 1071 "The heights (in centimeters) of $17$ newly planted saplings are summarized in the box plot above.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb1ae191d5c82fcafa04093928ba73640d3c0c25566078e23a5cc241df4e873.png)\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the median height?\n\n1. $119$\n2. $55$\n3. $141$\n4. $175$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228787 1072 #uuid "58743312-9274-5258-bd1c-042ee0ac1810" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228787 1074 "In a box plot, the median of the data set is represented by the thick line inside the box.\n\nIn this case, the median is located at about $120$.\n\nTherefore, among the choices, the value closest to the median height is $119$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228788 1069 "e-20580" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228788 1071 "The box plot above shows the distribution of the number of hours of sleep per night for $21$ adults. What is the median number of hours of sleep?\n\n![](images/f5/f5b2ec0334efbaf9804d3ba9cac671a79df571da29bacd9e86be48a672e1345d.png)" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228788 1072 #uuid "2957e635-a61e-5e37-8f9d-6a7234239b24" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228788 1074 "In a box plot, the median of the data set is represented by the thick line inside the box.\n\nIn this case, the median is located at $7$.\n\nTherefore, the median number of hours of sleep is $7$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228789 1069 "e-20601" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228789 1071 "A painting machine is covering walls at a rate of $480,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$. Express this rate in square meters per minute. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{m} = 3.28\\,\\textrm{ft}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228789 1072 #uuid "4d96e83c-2c32-56ef-9e04-81974190ccd6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228789 1074 "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The painting rate is given as $480,000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{m}{ft})^{2}|= \\frac{1^{2}}{3.28^{2}}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n480, 000\\frac{ft^{2}}{h} &= 480, 000\\frac{ft^{2}}{h} \\cdot \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\frac{h}{min} \\\\\n&= (480, 000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10.7584} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\frac{ft^{2}}{h} \\cdot \\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{h}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{480, 000}{10.7584 \\cdot 60}\\frac{m^{2}}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{480, 000}{645.504}\\frac{m^{2}}{min} \\\\\n&\\approx 744\\frac{m^{2}}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the machine is painting at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n744\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228790 1069 "e-20602" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228790 1071 "A maglev train accelerates at a rate of $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$. What is this acceleration rate, in miles per minute squared, rounded to the nearest tenth?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,609\\,\\textrm{m}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228790 1072 #uuid "e80a46e5-d2ab-5fdc-be40-47a460179ecc" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228790 1074 "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ into $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The acceleration rate given in the problem statement is $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,609$ meters per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter.\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of seconds squared in one minute squared, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{60s}{min})^{2}|= \\frac{60^{2}s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = \\frac{3, 600s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5.6\\frac{m}{s^{2}} &= 5.6\\frac{m}{s^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 609}\\frac{mi}{m} \\cdot 3, 600\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&= (5.6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 609} \\cdot 3, 600)\\frac{m}{s^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{mi}{m} \\cdot \\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5.6 \\cdot 3, 600}{1, 609}\\frac{mi}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20, 160}{1, 609}\\frac{mi}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&\\approx 12.5\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the maglev train is accelerating at a speed of approximately\n\n$$\n12.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228791 1069 "e-20603" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228791 1071 "A professional sports complex covers $3,872,000 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2$. What is the area of the complex in square miles? Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228791 1072 #uuid "872d0931-7cb1-553c-b669-a879bd1e9782" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228791 1074 "We want to convert the area from square yards to square miles.\n\nWe are given that there are $1,760$ yards per mile, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,760 \\, \\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{mi}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1, 760yd}{mi})^{2} &= \\frac{1, 760^{2}yd^{2}}{mi^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3, 097, 600yd^{2}}{mi^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{yd})^2$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{mi})^2$, we need to multiply $3,872,000 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}^2}{3,097,600 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $3,872,000 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3, 872, 000yd^{2} &= 3, 872, 000yd^{2} \\cdot \\frac{mi^{2}}{3, 097, 600yd^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3, 872, 000yd^{2} \\cdot \\frac{mi^{2}}{3, 097, 600yd^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3, 872, 000}{3, 097, 600}mi^{2} \\\\\n&= 1.25mi^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the area is approximately $1.25\\,\\textrm{mi}^2$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228792 1069 "e-20604" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228792 1071 "An archaeological site spans $1.1\\,\\textrm{mi}^2$. What is the total area of the site in square yards?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228792 1072 #uuid "c971c3ec-1cdd-5de5-b271-1dd9c31f469f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228792 1074 "We want to convert the area of the site from square miles to square yards.\n\nWe are told that there are $1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$ per $\\textrm{mi}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{mi}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1, 760yd}{mi})^{2} &= \\frac{1, 760^{2}yd^{2}}{mi^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3, 097, 600yd^{2}}{mi^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1.1\\,\\textrm{mi}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.1mi^{2} &= 1.1mi^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3, 097, 600yd^{2}}{mi^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1.1mi^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3, 097, 600yd^{2}}{mi^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1.1 \\cdot 3, 097, 600yd^{2} \\\\\n&= 3, 407, 360yd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the site is $3,407,360\\,\\textrm{yd}^2$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228793 1069 "e-20605" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228793 1071 "Which of the following speeds is equivalent to $18\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}$?\n\n1. $2.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n2. $5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n3. $4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1,000\\,\\textrm{m}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228793 1072 #uuid "4f4cb34a-9eda-5680-b6ea-693b6a1e240a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228793 1074 "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ into $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $18 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,000$ meters per kilometer.\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n$1h|= 60min; = 60 \\cdot 1min; = 60 \\cdot 60s; = 3, 600s$.\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18\\frac{km}{h} &= 18\\frac{km}{h} \\cdot 1, 000\\frac{m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3, 600}\\frac{h}{s} \\\\\n&= (18 \\cdot 1, 000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3, 600})\\frac{km}{h} \\cdot \\frac{m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{h}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18, 000}{3, 600}\\frac{m}{s} \\\\\n&= 5\\frac{m}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n18\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228794 1069 "e-20606" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228794 1071 "A rescue boat moves at a steady pace of $0.012 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{s}}$. What is this speed in miles per hour?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228794 1072 #uuid "b84e716d-6ef2-5b7d-931a-83b7c0c40f8d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228794 1074 "We want to convert the boat's speed from miles per second to miles per hour.\n\nThere are $60\\,\\textrm {min}$ per $\\textrm{hour}$, and also $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{60min}{hour} &= \\frac{60min}{hour} \\cdot \\frac{60s}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60min}{hour} \\cdot \\frac{60s}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3, 600s}{hour}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying\n\n$$\n0.012\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.012\\frac{mi}{s} &= 0.012\\frac{mi}{s} \\cdot \\frac{3, 600s}{h} \\\\\n&= 0.012\\frac{mi}{s} \\cdot \\frac{3, 600s}{h} \\\\\n&= 0.012 \\cdot 3, 600\\frac{mi}{h} \\\\\n&= 43.2\\frac{mi}{h} \\\\\n&\\approx 43\\frac{mi}{h}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the boat's speed is approximately\n\n$$\n43\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228795 1069 "e-20610" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228795 1071 "A group of friends is hiking a trail. Its steepest section is $880\\,\\textrm{ft}$ long. How long is this section in miles?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$ and $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228795 1072 #uuid "44832814-f0ba-58e9-8567-ec32f5bb8b7a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228795 1074 "We are given the units $\\textrm{ft}$, and we want to convert this into $\\textrm{mi}$. We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{ft}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}$ to $\\textrm{yd}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{yd}$ into $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{ft} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{yd}} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The length given in the problem statement is $880 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\n- We are given that there are $3$ feet per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ yards per foot.\n- We are also given that there are $1,760$ yards per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,760}$ miles per yard.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n880ft &= 880ft \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{yd}{ft} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 760}\\frac{mi}{yd} \\\\\n&= (880 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 760})ft \\cdot \\frac{yd}{ft} \\cdot \\frac{mi}{yd} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{880}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 760})mi \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}mi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the steepest section is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nlong." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228796 1069 "e-20613" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228796 1071 "A race is $44$ furlongs $(\\textrm{fur})$ long. How many feet is this distance?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{fur} = 220\\,\\textrm{yd}$ and $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228796 1072 #uuid "02839f5e-b056-5e85-a7c0-4abaad59cbac" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228796 1074 "We are given the units $\\textrm{fur}$, and we want to convert this into $\\textrm{ft}$. We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{fur}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{fur}$ to $\\textrm{yd}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{fur}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{fur}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{yd}$ into $\\textrm{ft}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{fur}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{fur}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}} = \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{fur} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{fur}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}} = \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The length given in the problem statement is $44 \\, \\textrm{fur}$.\n- We are given that there are $220$ yards per furlong.\n- We are also given that there are $3$ feet per yard.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n44fur &= 44fur \\cdot 220\\frac{yd}{fur} \\cdot 3\\frac{ft}{yd} \\\\\n&= (44 \\cdot 220 \\cdot 3)fur \\cdot \\frac{yd}{fur} \\cdot \\frac{ft}{yd} \\\\\n&= 29, 040ft\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $44\\,\\textrm{fur}$ is equivalent to $29,040\\,\\textrm{ft}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228797 1069 "e-20614" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228797 1071 "How many fluid ounces are equivalent to $25\\,\\textrm{qt}$?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{cup} = 8\\,\\textrm{fl oz}$ and $1\\,\\textrm{qt} = 4 \\, \\textrm{cups}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228797 1072 #uuid "10324e6d-ce5a-5dca-b765-8b063d6b68d4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228797 1074 "We want to convert the volume from quarts to fluid ounces.\n\nWe are also given that there are $8$ fluid ounces per cup, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8 \\, \\textrm{fl oz}}{\\textrm{cup}}\n$$\n\nIn the same way, we are given that there are $4$ cups per quart, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\, \\textrm{cups}}{\\textrm{qt}}\n$$\n\nTo convert quarts to fluid ounces, we multiply the given value by the conversion factors above. So, multiplying $25\\,\\textrm{qt}$ by our conversion factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25qt &= 25qt \\cdot \\frac{8fl oz}{cup} \\cdot \\frac{4cups}{qt} \\\\\n&= 25qt \\cdot \\frac{8fl oz}{cup} \\cdot \\frac{4cups}{qt} \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 4fl oz \\\\\n&= 800fl oz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25\\,\\textrm{qt}$ is equivalent to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{800}\\,\\textrm{fl oz}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228798 1069 "e-20615" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228798 1071 "![](images/b1/b1b46bcc65e4a2213656f7144d27947ff9abe0fcd8ebc916f1d40b98e1f31a30.png)\n\nA bowl (shown above) has a capacity of $64$ fluid ounces. How many bowls can be filled with $9$ gallons of water?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{gal} = 128\\,\\textrm{fl oz}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228798 1072 #uuid "addfa521-471f-568f-87ca-5f0155b35636" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228798 1074 "We want to find how many bowls can be filled, which first requires converting the total volume of water from gallons to fluid ounces.\n\nWe are given that there are $128$ fluid ounces per gallon, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{128 \\, \\textrm{fl oz}}{\\textrm{gal}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $9\\,\\textrm{gal}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9gal &= 9gal \\cdot \\frac{128fl oz}{gal} \\\\\n&= 9gal \\cdot \\frac{128fl oz}{gal} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 128fl oz \\\\\n&= 1, 152fl oz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find how many bowls can be filled, we divide the total fluid ounces by the capacity of one bowl:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of bowls} = \\dfrac{1,152 \\, \\textrm{fl oz}}{64 \\, \\textrm{fl oz/bowl}} = 18 \\, \\text{bowls}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18$ bowls can be filled with $9$ gallons of water." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228799 1069 "e-20617" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228799 1071 "A toy car moves at a speed of $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}$. What is its speed in yards per second?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228799 1072 #uuid "7e9d9752-5cca-5afa-867c-d3e145e0fc15" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228799 1074 "We want to convert the toy car's speed from feet per second to yards per second.\n\nWe are given that there are $3$ feet per yard, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\, \\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{ft})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{yd})$, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{3 \\, \\textrm{ft}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{50}\\frac{ft}{s} &= \\frac{1}{50}\\frac{ft}{s} \\cdot \\frac{1yd}{3ft} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\frac{ft}{s} \\cdot \\frac{1yd}{3ft} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{50} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3})\\frac{yd}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{150}\\frac{yd}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the toy car's speed is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228800 1069 "e-20618" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228800 1071 "A postal truck maintained an average speed of $15\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}$ while delivering packages. What was its speed in yards per hour?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228800 1072 #uuid "c44f4ad6-3437-5d8d-bc86-0c89cb8d34f0" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228800 1074 "We want to convert the postal truck’s speed from miles per hour to yards per hour.\n\nWe are given that there are $1,760$ yards per mile, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,760 \\, \\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{mi}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying\n\n$$\n15\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nby our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15\\frac{mi}{h} &= 15\\frac{mi}{h} \\cdot \\frac{1, 760yd}{mi} \\\\\n&= 15\\frac{mi}{h} \\cdot \\frac{1, 760yd}{mi} \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 1, 760\\frac{yd}{h} \\\\\n&= 26, 400\\frac{yd}{h}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truck's speed is\n\n$$\n26,400\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228801 1069 "e-20619" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228801 1071 "How many tablespoons are equivalent to $36$ teaspoons?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{tbsp} = 3 \\, \\textrm{tsp}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228801 1072 #uuid "b06a86d5-2400-5fa4-9ce5-85d3095d28c4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228801 1074 "We want to convert the amount from teaspoons to tablespoons.\n\nWe are given that there are $3$ teaspoons per tablespoon, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\, \\textrm{tsp}}{\\textrm{tbsp}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit $(\\textrm{tsp})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{tbsp})$, we need to multiply $36\\,\\textrm{tsp}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{tbsp}}{3 \\, \\textrm{tsp}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $36\\,\\textrm{tsp}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36tsp &= 36tsp \\cdot \\frac{1tbsp}{3tsp} \\\\\n&= 36tsp \\cdot \\frac{1tbsp}{3tsp} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{3}tbsp \\\\\n&= 12tbsp\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $36$ teaspoons are quivalent to ${12}$ tablespoons." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228802 1069 "e-20622" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228802 1071 "A factory uses $2.4\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of plastic to mold a casing. How many pounds of plastic are needed to mold $66$ casings?\n\nHint: $1 \\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228802 1072 #uuid "c1fe4fca-ab02-5807-a348-3f635dfad709" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228802 1074 "We need to calculate the total amount of plastic required in ounces.\n\nSince each casing requires $2.4\\,\\textrm{oz}$, the total amount of plastic needed to mold the $66$ casings is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n66 \\cdot 2.4 &= 158.4oz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we are told that there are $16$ ounces per pound, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{16\\,\\textrm{oz}}{\\textrm{lb}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{oz})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{lb})$, we need to multiply $158.4 \\,\\text{oz}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{16\\,\\textrm{oz}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $158.4 \\,\\text{oz}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n158.4oz &= 158.4oz \\cdot \\frac{lb}{16oz} \\\\\n&= 158.4oz \\cdot \\frac{lb}{16oz} \\\\\n&= 158.4 \\cdot \\frac{lb}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{158.4}{16}lb \\\\\n&= 9.9lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $9.9\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of plastic are needed to mold the $66$ casings." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228803 1069 "e-20623" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228803 1071 "A swimming pool lane marker measures $2,200\\,\\textrm{cm}$ in length. The coach wants to log this measurement in meters. How many meters long is the lane marker?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{m} = 100\\,\\textrm{cm}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228803 1072 #uuid "7db60b5d-a5d3-5bc4-9acc-66b3edd15db2" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228803 1074 "We want to convert the lane marker length from centimeters to meters.\n\nWe are given that there are $100$ centimeters per meter, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100 \\, \\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{cm})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{m})$, we need to multiply $2,200\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{100 \\, \\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $2,200\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2, 200cm &= 2, 200cm \\cdot \\frac{m}{100cm} \\\\\n&= 2, 200cm \\cdot \\frac{m}{100cm} \\\\\n&= 2, 200 \\cdot \\frac{m}{100} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2, 200}{100}m \\\\\n&= 22m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lane marker is $22\\,\\textrm{m}$ long." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228804 1069 "e-20624" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228804 1071 "A wolf was tracked migrating a distance of $1,936 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ through a forest corridor. How many miles did it travel?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760 \\, \\textrm{yd}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228804 1072 #uuid "b85ce90c-119e-5d5e-b447-bea409949ea3" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228804 1074 "We are told that there are $1,760$ yards per mile, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,760 \\, \\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{mi}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{yd})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{mi})$, we need to multiply $1,936\\,\\textrm{yd}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{1,760 \\, \\textrm{yd}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1,936\\,\\textrm{yd}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1, 936yd &= 1, 936yd \\cdot \\frac{mi}{1, 760yd} \\\\\n&= 1, 936yd \\cdot \\frac{mi}{1, 760yd} \\\\\n&= 1, 936 \\cdot \\frac{mi}{1, 760} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1, 936}{1, 760}mi \\\\\n&= 1.1mi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf traveled $1.1\\,\\textrm{mi}$ through the corridor." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228805 1069 "e-20626" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228805 1071 "A construction worker is installing a pipe that measures $252\\,\\textrm{in}$ in length. How many yards of pipe are needed?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 36 \\, \\textrm{in}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228805 1072 #uuid "21ae8a2b-0a72-520b-a8cc-20a2bf10d3f3" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228805 1074 "We are given that there are $36$ inches per yard, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36 \\, \\textrm{in}}{\\textrm{yd}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{in})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{yd})$, we need to multiply $252\\,\\textrm{in}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{36 \\, \\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $252\\,\\textrm{in}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n252in &= 252in \\cdot \\frac{yd}{36in} \\\\\n&= 252in \\cdot \\frac{yd}{36in} \\\\\n&= 252 \\cdot \\frac{yd}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{252}{36}yd \\\\\n&= 7yd\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the construction worker needs $7\\,\\textrm{yd}$ of pipe." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228806 1069 "e-20632" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228806 1071 "Two runners, $A$ and $B$, each move at constant speeds. The speed of runner $A$ is one fourth the speed of runner $B$. Runner $A$ covers a distance of $8q$ meters in $t$ seconds. Which expression represents the time, in seconds, it takes runner $B$ to travel a distance of $48q$ meters?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228806 1072 #uuid "6c993b01-aa12-5875-b61b-e60f59ae0517" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228806 1074 "The formula that relates distance, speed, and time is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}}\n$$\n\nLet’s start with runner $A$. We’re told he travels a distance of $8q$ meters in time $t$. So, his speed is\n\n$$\n\\text{Speed}_A = \\dfrac{8q}{t}\n$$\n\nNow, we are given that runner $A$ moves at one-fourth the speed of runner $B$. That means runner $B$ is four times as fast as runner $A$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSpeed_{B} &= 4 \\cdot Speed_{A} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{8q}{t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{32q}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate how long it takes runner $B$ to travel $48q$ meters:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTime_{B} &= (48q)/((32q/t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{48q \\cdot t}{32q} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 16q \\cdot t}{2 \\cdot 16q} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 16q \\cdot t}{2 \\cdot 16q} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3t}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3t}{2}\n$$\n\nrepresents the time, in seconds, it takes runner $B$ to travel a distance of $48q$ meters." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228807 1069 "e-20633" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228807 1071 "Veterinarians sometimes estimate a dog's age in years by multiplying its weight in pounds by a constant growth factor that varies depending on the breed. The table below shows the growth factors for several dog breeds.\n\n| Dog Breed | Growth<br>factor |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Labrador Retriever | $5$ |\n| German Shepherd | $5.5$ |\n| Golden Retriever | $5$ |\n| Bulldog | $5$ |\n| Beagle | $4$ |\n| Boxer | $5.5$ |\n| Poodle | $4.5$ |\n| Rottweiler | $6$ |\n\nSuppose a Golden Retriever and a Poodle each currently weigh $14$ pounds. Which of the following is closest to the difference, in pounds, between their weights $11$ years from now?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228807 1072 #uuid "eba77823-aa2d-52b5-bc6e-ec9596a9190d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228807 1074 "We’re told that a dog’s age can be estimated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Age} = \\text{weight} \\times \\text{growth factor}\n$$\n\nFor a Golden Retriever with weight $14$ pounds, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Age}_{\\text{golden}} = 14 \\times 5 = 70\n$$\n\nFor a Poodle with the same weight, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Age}_{\\text{poodle}} = 14 \\times 4.5 = 63\n$$\n\nNow, after $11$ years, the Golden Retriever’s age increases to\n\n$$\n70 + 11 = 81\n$$\n\nwhile the Poodle’s age becomes\n\n$$\n63 + 11 = 74\n$$\n\nTo compare their weights, we solve for weight:\n\n$$\n\\text{weight} = \\frac{\\text{age}}{\\text{growth factor}}\n$$\n\nFor the Golden Retriever:\n\n$$\n\\text{weight}_{\\text{golden,new}} = \\frac{81}{5} = 16.2\n$$\n\nand for the Poodle:\n\n$$\n\\text{weight}_{\\text{poodle,new}} = \\frac{74}{4.5} \\approx 16.44\n$$\n\nFinally, the difference is\n\n$$\n\\mid 16.44 - 16.2 \\mid \\approx 0.24\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the closest value to the difference is $0.2$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228808 1069 "e-20635" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228808 1071 "In $1932$, Wiley Post made the first solo flight around the world. Suppose he traveled $90$ miles in $72$ minutes. If he maintained this pace, what was his average speed, in miles per hour, to the nearest tenth?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228808 1072 #uuid "bb6ab7d9-685b-5a78-a5b1-650f43bb3714" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228808 1074 "We're asked to determine the average speed in miles per hour. Therefore, we must first transform the given number of minutes into hours.\n\nThere are $60\\,\\textrm{min}$ per $\\textrm{h}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of time $(\\textrm{min})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{h})$, we need to multiply $72\\,\\textrm{min}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{60\\,\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $72\\,\\textrm{min}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n72min &= 72min \\cdot \\frac{h}{60min} \\\\\n&= 72min \\cdot \\frac{h}{60min} \\\\\n&= 72 \\cdot \\frac{h}{60} \\\\\n&= \\frac{72}{60}h \\\\\n&= 1.2h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $72\\,\\textrm{min}$ is equivalent to $1.2\\,\\textrm{h}$.\n\nNext, we can use the formula that relates distance, speed, and time:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Speed} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Distance}}{\\textrm{Time}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values ​​in the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSpeed &= \\frac{90}{1.2} \\\\\n&= 75.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average speed of the flight was $75.0$ miles per hour." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228809 1069 "e-20637" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228809 1071 "A planet has two satellites. The first satellite orbits the planet every $300$ days, and the second satellite orbits the planet every $350$ days. How many more days does it take the second satellite to orbit the planet $60$ times than it takes the first satellite to orbit the planet $60$ times?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228809 1072 #uuid "ce1a9f69-3834-5800-8017-8adc3526229a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228809 1074 "We are given that the first satellite orbits the planet every $300$ days. Therefore, it takes the first satellite\n\n$$\n300(60) = 18000\n$$\n\ndays to orbit the planet $60$ times.\n\nWe are also given that the second satellite orbits the planet every $350$ days. Therefore, it takes the second satellite\n\n$$\n350(60) = 21000\n$$\n\ndays to orbit the planet $60$ times.\n\nSince it takes the first satellite $18000$ days and the second satellite $21000$ days, it takes the second satellite\n\n$$\n21000 - 18000 = 3000\n$$\n\nmore days than it takes the first satellite to orbit the planet $60$ times." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228810 1069 "e-20638" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228810 1071 "The total area of a lakeside town is $240.0$ square miles, of which $80.0$ square miles is water. If the town had a population of $1,300,000$ people in the year $2022$, which of the following is closest to the population density, in people per square mile of land area, of the town at that time?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228810 1072 #uuid "d37e778d-1a28-52d0-9e64-9ad516b3bd02" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228810 1074 "We are given that the total area of the lakeside town is $240.0$ square miles, of which $80.0$ square miles is water, and the town had a population of $1,300,000$ people in the year $2022$.\n\nThe land area of the town can be found by subtracting the area of the water from the total area of the town:\n\n$$\n240.0 - 80.0 = 160.0\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate the population density of the town, we divide its population by the land area:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,300,000}{160.0} = 8125\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population density is approximately $8125$ people per square mile." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228811 1069 "e-20642" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228811 1071 "A motorcycle travels at an average speed of $55$ meters per second. At this rate, how many meters will the motorcycle travel in $16$ seconds?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228811 1072 #uuid "330cb7b4-b736-5ffd-ae6f-e7a7524aefe8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228811 1074 "We are given that the motorcycle travels for $16$ seconds at an average speed of $55$ meters per second.\n\nTo solve our problem, we can use the formula that relates distance, speed, and time:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Speed} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Distance}}{\\textrm{Time}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values ​​in the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n55 &= \\frac{Distance}{16} \\\\\nDistance &= 55 \\cdot 16 \\\\\nDistance &= 880\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the motorcycle will travel $880$ meters in $16$ seconds." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228812 1069 "e-20644" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228812 1071 "A cyclist pedals at a constant speed of $4$ meters per second. At this speed, how many seconds will it take the cyclist to travel $100$ meters?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228812 1072 #uuid "ce9056b4-ddb6-52d3-9d5b-3d8f9306286a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228812 1074 "We are given the cyclist travels $100$ meters at a constant speed of $4$ meters per second.\n\nTo solve our problem, we can use the formula that relates distance, speed, and time:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Speed} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Distance}}{\\textrm{Time}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values ​​in the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= \\frac{100}{Time} \\\\\nTime &= \\frac{100}{4} \\\\\nTime &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take the cyclist $25$ seconds to travel $100$ meters." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228813 1069 "e-20645" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228813 1071 "An oil extractor produces $18$ liters of oil in $7$ minutes. At this rate, how many minutes does it take the extractor to produce $90$ liters of oil?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228813 1072 #uuid "e629107e-9a91-5127-bffc-0049ea468c52" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228813 1074 "We are given that the oil extractor produces $18$ liters of oil in $7$ minutes. This rate can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18\\,\\textrm{L}}{7\\,\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of minutes it takes the oil extractor to produce $90$ liters of oil. Then, the value of $x$ can be calculated by solving the equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18\\,\\textrm{L}}{7\\,\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{90\\,\\textrm{L}}{x\\,\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten more simply as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{7} = \\dfrac{90}{x}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{18}{7} &= \\frac{90}{x} \\\\\n18 \\cdot x &= 90 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n18x &= 630 \\\\\nx &= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes $35$ minutes to produce $90$ liters of oil." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228814 1069 "e-20655" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228814 1071 "If $\\dfrac{2.7\\,m}{n} = 5.4$ and $\\dfrac{m}{nt} = 16$, what is the value of $t$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228814 1072 #uuid "5b14f7c9-f020-5140-9304-60772b7e2fed" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228814 1074 "We are given two equations\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.7\\,m}{n}=5.4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{m}{nt}=16\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the first equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m}{n}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m}{n}=\\dfrac{5.4}{2.7}=2\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m}{n}=2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{m}{nt} &= 16 \\\\\n\\frac{m}{n} \\cdot \\frac{1}{t} &= 16 \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{t} &= 16 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{t} &= 16 \\\\\n2 &= 16t \\\\\nt &= \\frac{2}{16} \\\\\nt &= 0.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $t$ is $0.125$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228815 1069 "e-20656" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228815 1071 "A teacher distributes $12$ pencils and $18$ pens to students in a fixed ratio. Later, she prepares another set using the same ratio of pencils to pens. If she has $20$ pencils in the second set, how many pens should she include?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228815 1072 #uuid "b7c5afb3-880b-55cf-961b-821d61a8e3a7" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228815 1074 "The ratio of pencils to pens in the first set is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{18}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThis means that for every $2$ pencils, there are $3$ pens.\n\nIn the second set, the teacher has $20$ pencils. To keep the same ratio, we set up the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{20}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 3 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n2x &= 60 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{60}{2} \\\\\nx &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the teacher should include $30$ pens in the second set." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228816 1069 "e-20657" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228816 1071 "If $\\dfrac{a}{b} = 10$ and $\\dfrac{50a}{m b} = 5$, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228816 1072 #uuid "f9222451-62df-5a2b-a55c-81b2f75077f6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228816 1074 "We are given the relations\n\n- $\\dfrac{a}{b} = 10$,\n- $\\dfrac{50a}{m b} = 5$.\n\nLet's substitute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=10\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{50a}{mb} &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{50}{m} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b} &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{50}{m} \\cdot 10 &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{500}{m} &= 5 \\\\\n500 &= 5m \\\\\nm &= \\frac{500}{5} \\\\\nm &= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $m$ is $100$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228817 1069 "e-20666" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228817 1071 "Laptop screen $P$ has length $32$ and width $s$. Laptop screen $Q$ has width $20$ and the same length-to-width ratio as laptop screen $P$. What is the **length** of laptop screen $Q$ in terms of $s$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228817 1072 #uuid "8d57f799-644c-52ce-92c8-22f969aa7bbd" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228817 1074 "The ratio of length to width for laptop screen $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{s}\n$$\n\nSince laptop screen $Q$ has the same ratio, we set up the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{s} = \\dfrac{L_Q}{20}\n$$\n\nwhere $L_Q$ is the length of laptop screen $Q$.\n\nNext, we solve for $L_Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{32}{s} &= \\frac{L_{Q}}{20} \\\\\n\\frac{32}{s} \\cdot 20 &= L_{Q} \\\\\nL_{Q} &= \\frac{32 \\cdot 20}{s} \\\\\nL_{Q} &= \\frac{640}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the length of laptop screen $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{640}{s}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228818 1069 "e-20668" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228818 1071 "In a basket of fruits, the ratio of bananas to grapes is $4$ to $3$. If there are $28$ bananas in the basket, how many grapes are there?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228818 1072 #uuid "84ba4ca0-2fbf-5e18-8514-a31bab2dcc8d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228818 1074 "The ratio of bananas to grapes is $4:3$. This means for every $4$ bananas, there are $3$ grapes.\n\nIf the number of bananas is $28$, we can write the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{28}{a} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the number of grapes.\n\nNow, we solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{28}{a} &= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n28 \\cdot 3 &= 4 \\cdot a \\\\\n84 &= 4a \\\\\na &= \\frac{84}{4} \\\\\na &= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the basket contains $21$ grapes." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228819 1069 "e-20669" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228819 1071 "The ratio $t$ to $u$ is equivalent to the ratio $9$ to $2$. If the value of $t$ is $144$, what is the value of $u$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228819 1072 #uuid "a4325470-daf5-567f-913d-31ce60b8cce6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228819 1074 "To find the value of $u$, we set up a proportion using the given equivalent ratios:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{t}{u} = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $t=144$, and solve for $u{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{t}{u} &= \\frac{9}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{144}{u} &= \\frac{9}{2} \\\\\n144 \\cdot 2 &= 9u \\\\\n288 &= 9u \\\\\nu &= \\frac{288}{9} \\\\\nu &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $u$ is $32$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228820 1069 "e-20685" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228820 1071 "![](images/8a/8a7920a87602811cd31c03f6200198c69f837b28df34b4034cd724d3f347139f.png)\n\nThe scatterplot shows the miles driven $d$ (in hundreds) and fuel remaining $F$ (in liters). Which of the following could be an equation for a line of best fit for the data?\n\n1. $F=-5d+60$\n2. $F=-5d+40$\n3. $F=-2d+60$\n4. $F=5d-60$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228820 1072 #uuid "e73b7923-77f5-5f1f-862a-fc7160f02cd5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228820 1074 "We are asked to identify the equation whose graph most closely represents the line of best fit for the data shown in the form\n\n$$\nF = md + b\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the slope and $b$ is the $F$-intercept.\n\nFrom the shape of the data points, the line of best fit should pass roughly through the points\n\n$$\n(d_1, F_1) = (2, 50)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(d_2, F_2) = (4, 40)\n$$\n\nThus, these two points can be used to find an approximate slope for the line of best fit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{F_{2} - F_{1}}{d_{2} - d_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{40 - 50}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{10}{2} \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to estimate the $F$-intercept, we substitute a point on the line, such as $(2, 50)$, into the equation to solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= md + b \\\\\n50 &= -5(2) + b \\\\\n50 &= -10 + b \\\\\nb &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the options, the most appropriate model for the line of best fit of the data shown is\n\n$$\nF=-5d+60\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228821 1069 "e-20686" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228821 1071 "![](images/61/618b00e5a59a0d5feb63aad9e24a87af2d1d9399e6d1362aa9b2e1dfcf38aa75.png)\n\nThe scatterplot shows the gas mileage and the average speed for $7$ fuel-efficient vehicles, where $v$ represents the average speed, in miles per hour (mph), and $g$ represents the gas mileage, in miles per gallon (mpg). A line of best fit for the data is also shown. Which of the following could be an equation of the line of best fit?\n\n1. $g = -0.12v + 48$\n2. $g = -0.12v - 48$\n3. $g = 0.12v + 48$\n4. $g = 0.12v - 48$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228821 1072 #uuid "859f8f56-7b79-56c1-b56d-edbc070b37b2" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228821 1074 "We are asked to identify an equation that represents the line of best fit. This line has the form:\n\n$$\ng = mv + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $d$-intercept.\n\nFrom the graph, we note that the line passes approximately through the points\n\n$$\n(v_1, g_1) = (75, 39)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(v_2, g_2) = (100, 36)\n$$\n\nSo, we use these two points to find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{g_{2} - g_{1}}{v_{2} - v_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36 - 39}{100 - 75} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{25} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute a point on the line, such as $(100, 36)$, into the equation to solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng &= mv + b \\\\\n36 &= -0.12(100) + b \\\\\n36 &= -12 + b \\\\\nb &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that represents the line of best fit is\n\n$$\ng = -0.12v + 48\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228822 1069 "e-20691" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228822 1071 "![](images/dd/dd7fe5cf4435509166b8df89f479dcc84dae906f8ae4dbf79397e614389ba71e.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the relationship between variables $x$ and $y$, along with a line of best fit. Which of the following is closest to the slope of the line of best fit shown?\n\n1. $-0.98$\n2. $-0.50$\n3. $0.90$\n4. $1.90$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228822 1072 #uuid "d8f32374-5fd2-5691-b81d-c59684907f82" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228822 1074 "To estimate the slope of the line of best fit, we pick two points on the line (not from the scattered data).\n\nFirst, we observe from the graph shown that the line of best fit passes approximately through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (0, 12)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (8, 4)\n$$\n\nSo, we use these two points to find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 12}{8 - 0} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{8} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the choices, the slope closest to our estimated value of $-1$ for the line of best fit is $-0.98$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228823 1069 "e-20699" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228823 1071 "![](images/ce/ce8a574c8d7d4f15e1b8a55a4ea25b02f87bdba1e4f8afb0685062786317ab83.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the heights and number of tomatoes harvested by 10 tomato plants. A line of best fit for the data is also shown. Which of the following statements best describes the association between the height and the number of tomatoes harvested?\n\n1. As the height increases, the crop yield tends to increase.\n2. As the height increases, the crop yield tends to decrease.\n3. As the height decreases, the crop yield tends to increase.\n4. There is no association between the height and the crop yield." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228823 1072 #uuid "19e48d5e-bebc-57a3-a04a-82a0c2088d8d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228823 1074 "We are given a scatterplot that shows how the number of tomatoes harvested varies with the plant's height, along with a line of best fit. Each point represents a measurement of the crop yield of a tomato plant with a specific height.\n\nFrom the graph, we observe that as height increases along the horizontal axis, the crop yield increases along the vertical axis. This upward trend is confirmed by the line of best fit, which slopes upward.\n\nAn upward-sloping trend line shows a positive association, meaning that as the explanatory variable height increases, the response variable crop yield tends to increase.\n\nTherefore, the correct interpretation is\n$\\text{As the height increases, the crop yield tends to increase.}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228824 1069 "e-20700" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228824 1071 "![](images/74/74fee32000dc4fd852054e03e85f80041cdfb98245aed6570c821bacc9bf1ba6.png)\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the slope of this line of best fit?\n\n1. $0.8$\n2. $0.5$\n3. $0.3$\n4. $-0.5$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228824 1072 #uuid "412c0b7f-3c6e-5314-b0c8-dbbd30c1465e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228824 1074 "We want to find the slope of the line of best fit.\n\nFirst, we observe from the graph shown that the line of best fit passes through the points $(0, 9)$ and $(5, 13)$.\n\nSo, we use these two points to find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{13 - 9}{5 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line of best fit is $0.8$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228825 1069 "e-20703" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228825 1071 "![](images/1a/1ad978b569540a79e2aa24a29c8068990fb7265d31263ac461eef596401dff6b.png)\n\nThe scatterplot shows the data on how many hours children of different ages slept in a day, collected by a doctor in a survey that involved $5$ participants. A line of best fit is also shown. How long (in hours) does a $7$-year-old child sleep in a day, as predicted by the line of best fit?\n\n1. $8.14$ hours\n2. $13.54$ hours\n3. $3.77$ hours\n4. $10.85$ hours" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228825 1072 #uuid "729a3623-6e2a-53ba-b5a0-5c5e4feb7f4a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228825 1074 "We want to estimate the sleep duration (in hours) of a $7$-year-old child using the line of best fit.\n\nFrom the scatterplot, we locate $x = 7$ and see where it meets the line of best fit. Visually, this point is slightly above $8$ hours on the $y$-axis.\n\nAmong the given answer choices, the closest value to this estimate is $8.14$ hours.\n\nTherefore, a $7$-year-old child is predicted to sleep for about $8.14$ hours in a day." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228826 1069 "e-20704" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228826 1071 "![](images/82/82b22078c84050b39cc218a3219f29e886b5e35031126ee6111e1a2cf2e0c855.png)\n\nAt $x=13$, which of the following is closest to the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit?\n\n1. $10.3$\n2. $11.6$\n3. $12.4$\n4. $9.1$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228826 1072 #uuid "5e484f0f-6091-5abe-bf07-babb7924494d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228826 1074 "We want to find the closest value to the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit at $x = 13$.\n\nFrom the scatterplot, we locate $x = 13$ and see where it meets the line of best fit. Visually, this point is between $10$ and $11$ on the $y$-axis.\n\nAmong the given answer choices, the closest value to this estimate is $10.3$.\n\nTherefore, the predicted $y$-value at $x = 13$ is $10.3$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228827 1069 "e-20718" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228827 1071 "The scatterplot shows the relationship between the time $y$, in hours, that a tagged owl spent in nocturnal flight, and the number of days, $x$, after September $1$.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ddd3952940ff9c8cfa135271fcb6594ba1bdf4efe2dbba545464479b69898c.png)\n\nWhat is the average rate of change, in hours per day, of the length of time the owl spent in nocturnal flight from September $3$ to September $8$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228827 1072 #uuid "d173a50b-c643-593e-9d06-91fc1143004a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228827 1074 "For the graph shown, $x$ represents the number of days (after September $1$) and $y$ represents the time in hours. Therefore, the average rate of change, in hours per day, of the number of hours that the tagged owl spent in nocturnal flight between two $x$-values is the difference in the corresponding $y$-values divided by the difference between the $x$-values.\n\nSince the $x$-values represent the days after September $1$, the origin at $x = 0$ corresponds to that date.\n\nNow, we convert the dates September $3$ and September $8$ into $x$-values.\n\n- September $3$ corresponds to $x = 2$.\n- September $8$ corresponds to $x = 7$.\n\nNotice that the graph shows that at $x = 2$, the corresponding $y$-value is $14$. Similarly, the graph shows that at $x = 7$, the corresponding $y$-value is $9$.\n\nTherefore, the average rate of change from $x = 2$ to $x = 7$ (from September $3$ to September $8$) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-14}{7-2} = \\frac{-5}{5} = -1\\,\\text{hour per day}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228828 1069 "e-20723" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228828 1071 "![](images/26/26b4dcd4e26437398c6ff758d4805cc35998a3b02f86c2985b7ffeae4fe57e3b.png)\n\nDuring an experiment, the air pressure in a tire was recorded to the nearest integer at certain times. The scatterplot shows the recorded pressure $y$, in kilopascals (kPa), of the tire $x$ seconds after the start of the experiment.\n\nWhat was the average rate of change, in kPa per second, of the recorded pressure from $x=2$ to $x=6$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228828 1072 #uuid "440358ae-cf73-5170-9794-d662da9a2877" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228828 1074 "For the graph shown, $x$ represents time, in seconds, and $y$ represents pressure, in kilopascals. Therefore, the average rate of change, in kPa per second, of the pressure between two $x$-values is the difference in the corresponding $y$-values ​​divided by the difference between the $x$-values.\n\nThe graph shows that at $x = 2$, the corresponding $y$-value is $105$. Similarly, the graph shows that at $x = 6$, the corresponding $y$-value is $125$.\n\nTherefore, the average rate of change from $x = 2$ to $x = 6$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{125-105}{6-2} = \\frac{20}{4} = 5\\ \\text{kPa/second}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228829 1069 "e-20724" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228829 1071 "![](images/53/53a7275e1a324665c54174268f23c0f449f55683cae4d5437529f88500d75c8c.png)\n\nThe graph shows the temperature $y$, in degrees Celsius, of water $x$ minutes after being placed on a stove, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 12$. What is the average rate of change, in degrees Celsius per minute, in the water’s temperature from $x=4$ to $x=12$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228829 1072 #uuid "13f7ea83-bd6b-5dac-bf96-37c53ba78f20" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228829 1074 "For the graph shown, $x$ represents time, in minutes, and $y$ represents temperature, in degrees Celsius. Therefore, the average rate of change, in degrees Celsius per minute, of the temperature of the water between two $x$-values is the difference in the corresponding $y$-values divided by the difference between the $x$-values.\n\nThe graph shows that at $x=4$, the corresponding $y$-value is $40$. Similarly, the graph shows that at $x=12$, the corresponding $y$-value is $100$.\n\nTherefore, the average rate of change from $x=4$ to $x=12$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{100-40}{12-4} = \\frac{60}{8} = 7.5\\,^\\circ\\text{C/min}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228830 1069 "e-20725" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228830 1071 "![](images/85/85648d02ef153e0d2b0019d66f647c6160caafbc25b16d5ccc370a962ec38e5a.png)\n\nA call center operated for twenty-four hours, and its time and handling rate are shown on the graph above. According to the graph, which of the following statements is **not** true concerning the call center’s operation?\n\n1. The handling rate increased at a constant rate during the first four hours.\n2. The handling rate was constant for eight consecutive hours.\n3. The handling rate reached its maximum during the last eight hours.\n4. The handling rate decreased at a constant rate during the last four hours." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228830 1072 #uuid "a615b8a0-f063-5cd2-8dad-a1d61d697c43" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228830 1074 "We proceed to analyze each statement based on the graph.\n\n- Statement I is true because the handling rate increased at a constant rate from $0$ to $4$ hours.\n- Statement II is false since no single $8$-hour interval is flat; the constant stretches are $4$–$8$ hours and $12$–$16$ hours, each only $4$ hours long.\n- Statement III is false because the maximum rate of $50$ calls per hour occurred from $12$ to $16$ hours, not during the last eight hours.\n- Statement IV is false since the last four hours ($20$ to $24$) show a constant rate, not a decrease.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228831 1069 "e-20726" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228831 1071 "![](images/73/73a38c905862e100f0f5251d7491504d1fb4b11834d925322fc6ebac51fb759b.png)\n\nThe scatterplot shows the river water level $H$, in feet, measured each week $t$ at a monitoring station. During which of the following time periods did the greatest decrease in recorded water level take place?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228831 1072 #uuid "de3a9ea5-7b45-5eb2-b87f-3eb3b3965d37" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228831 1074 "The scatterplot shows that there was a decrease in recorded water level from $t=2$ to $t=3$, and from $t=5$ to $t=6$.\n\nWhen $t=2$, the recorded water level was approximately $16$ feet, and when $t=3$, the recorded water level was approximately $14$ feet. This means that the decrease in recorded water level from $t=2$ to $t=3$ was approximately $(16-14)$ feet, or $2$ feet.\n\nOn the other hand, when $t=5$, the recorded water level was approximately $20$ feet, and when $t=6$, the recorded water level was approximately $11$ feet. This means that the decrease in recorded water level from $t=5$ to $t=6$ was approximately $(20-11)$ feet, or $9$ feet.\n\nTherefore, the greatest decrease in the recorded water level occurs from $t=5$ to $t=6$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228832 1069 "e-20727" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228832 1071 "![](images/24/240c65807d0e88813b6822f69435e71563a24f836d1025e7407885990bc5f8a3.png)\n\nIn a lab experiment, a heated metal rod is removed from a furnace, and its temperature is measured every $2$ minutes. The plotted points (temperature in $^\\circ\\!F$ versus time in minutes) show a curve that decreases quickly at first and then levels off. Over which time interval does the rod cool most rapidly?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228832 1072 #uuid "6b335131-77ed-538c-952c-d1a7962dec18" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228832 1074 "The average rate of change is simply the temperature difference divided by the time it took for that change to occur. In our case, the most significant change in the rod’s temperature happened between $0$ and $2$ minutes. This is because the temperature dropped more during this period than in any other $2$-minute interval.\n\nTherefore, from the graph, the greatest temperature difference occurs between $0$ and $2$ minutes." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228833 1069 "e-20728" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228833 1071 "![](images/c6/c64b4c476798c1438ea4f5ff57799cc04d9698a3aca98c6a6b030f104029aeb7.png)\n\nAccording to the line graph above, between which two consecutive days was there the greatest change in the number of visitors to the museum?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228833 1072 #uuid "a26dd352-a93e-5d32-b6fd-7a74909ccc3f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228833 1074 "The greatest change corresponds to the steepest upward/downward slope on the graph.\n\nTherefore, from the graph, the greatest change in the number of visitors to the museum occurs from Friday to Saturday." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228834 1069 "e-20735" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228834 1071 "Two towns grow as summarized in the table. The population of City A grows by a percentage each year.\n\n|  | Initial population | Yearly increase |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| City A | $50{,}000$ | $3\\%$ per year (applied to current population) |\n| City B | $55{,}000$ | $1{,}200$ people per year |\n\nWhich of the following is true about the populations?\n\n1. City A always gains more people per year than City B.\n2. City A gains more people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains less people per year.\n3. City A always gains less people per year than City B.\n4. City A gains less people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains more people per year." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228834 1072 #uuid "34421640-d6de-5282-ba7e-741df2c49648" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228834 1074 "City A starts with $50{,}000$ people and increases by $3\\%$ per year. So, in the first year, City A gains\n\n$$\n50{,}000 \\cdot 0.03 = 1{,}500 \\text{people}\n$$\n\nwhich is greater than the $1{,}200$ people that City B gains that year.\n\nA percentage increase applied to the current amount can be modeled by an increasing exponential function, so each year City A will gain more people than it did the previous year. Therefore, each year City A gains at least $1{,}500$ people. Since City B always gains $1{,}200$ people per year, thus we conclude that\n\n> City A always gains more people per year than City B." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228835 1069 "e-20740" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228835 1071 "The table shows selected values from the function $k$.\n\n| $x$ | $k(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |\n| $2$ | $-9$ |\n\nWhich of the following is the best description of function $k$?\n\n1. Decreasing exponential\n2. Increasing exponential\n3. Increasing linear\n4. Decreasing linear" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228835 1072 #uuid "d0d183e3-cd94-5b61-87ca-c9b0fd847834" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228835 1074 "The given values show that as $x$ increases, $k(x)$ decreases, which means that $k$ is a decreasing function.\n\nOn the other hand, $k(x)$ decreases at a constant rate of $5$ for each increase of $x$ by $1$. A function with a constant rate of change is linear. Thus, the function $k$ can be described as a decreasing linear function." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228836 1069 "e-20744" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228836 1071 "Which of the following is true about the values of $7x+3$ and $7^{x}$ for $x>0$?\n\n1. For all $x \\gt 0$, it is true that $7x+3 \\lt 7^{x}$.\n2. There is a constant $c$ such that if $0 \\lt x \\lt c$, then $7x+3 \\lt 7^{x}$, but if $x \\gt c$, then $7^x \\lt 7x+3$.\n3. There is a constant $c$ such that if $0 \\lt x \\lt c$, then $7x+3 \\gt 7^{x}$, but if $x \\gt c$, then $7^x \\gt 7x+3$.\n4. For all $x \\gt 0$, it is true that $7x+3 \\gt 7^{x}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228836 1072 #uuid "e2663960-8d4c-5f6a-ab0a-ec343bb69244" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228836 1074 "At $x = 1$, the value of $7x+3$ is greater than the value of $7^x$. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7(1) + 3 & >^(?)7^{1} \\\\ 7 + 3 & >^(?)7 \\\\ 10 & > 7.✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x = 3$, the value of $7^x$ is greater than the value of $7x+3$. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7^{3} & >^(?)7(3) + 3 \\\\ 343 & >^(?)21 + 3 \\\\ 343 & > 24.✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, there is a value $c$ between $1$ and $3$ such that if $0 < x < c$, we have $7x+3 > 7^x$, and if $x > c$, we have $7^x > 7x+3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is \"III only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228837 1069 "e-21317" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228837 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228837 1072 #uuid "185f4059-c0d2-59a0-932b-d1152c407db6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228837 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + x_{new}y_{new} - y_(⏟)_(g(y_{new})) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + 2y_{new} - 1 \\\\\n&= 3y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228838 1069 "e-21319" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228838 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228838 1072 #uuid "2e45e65d-ffd1-5418-9445-eee71cb36ded" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228838 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (2x_{new}y_{new} + (y_{new})^{3}) \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2\n$$\n\nThe old $y$ value is $y = -0.3$. Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + (2 - 1)y_{new} - 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + y_{new} - 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method.\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with\n\n$$\ny(1.5) = -0.3\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= -0.3 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 0.240529 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 0.288849 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 0.288050\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 0.29\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228839 1069 "e-21382" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228839 1071 "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}$, where $x\\neq 5$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{7}{(x-5)^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{(x-5)^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{2}{(x-5)^2}$\n4. $\\dfrac{1}{2(x-5)^2}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228839 1072 #uuid "df4337f1-efd3-599b-9988-7568d9727dbc" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228839 1074 "Here, we wish to simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{GCF}(7x,35)=7\n$$\n\nand so we may factor our denominator to obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7(x-5)}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal, and so we may rewrite our complex fraction as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((\\frac{14}{x - 5}))/(7(x - 5)) &= \\frac{14}{x - 5} \\cdot (1)/(7(x - 5)) \\\\\n&= (14)/(7(x - 5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (7 \\cdot 2)/(7(x - 5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (7 \\cdot 2)/(7(x - 5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2)/((x - 5)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"III.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228840 1069 "e-21383" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228840 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228840 1072 #uuid "99d2ed59-3062-55d3-b57a-b4d1e04fd4b7" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228840 1074 "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228841 1069 "e-21384" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228841 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t$ as a power of $\\dfrac43$ only." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228841 1072 #uuid "2682674b-095f-52ee-819b-75bef64bd999" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228841 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{4}{3})^{3}(\\frac{4}{3})^{t} &= (\\frac{4}{3})^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228842 1069 "e-21385" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228842 1071 "Express $5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$ in the form $n^k$, where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228842 1072 #uuid "2d98a0e4-0c65-5960-ab70-7655e4d69439" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228842 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n$5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$,\ninvolves the factor $5^{2k}$. However, using the power rule, we can express this as a power of $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5^{2k} = \\left(5^{2}\\right)^k\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2k}4^{k} &= (5^{2})^{k}4^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5^2 = 25$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(5^{2}\\right)^k \\, 4^k = 25^k \\, 4^k\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power of the product rule, we can write this as\n\n$$\n25^k \\, 4^k = \\left(100\\right)^k\n$$\n\nHence, $n = 100$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228843 1069 "e-21386" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228843 1071 "Simplify the expression $b^{3/4}\\left(b^{5/4} c + b^{1/4} c^2 \\right)$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228843 1072 #uuid "87dfe0a1-34a5-59ab-9a4e-091e363834bf" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228843 1074 "First, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\nb^{3/4}\\left(b^{5/4} c + b^{1/4} c^2 \\right) = b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{5/4} c + b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{1/4} c^2\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{3/4} \\cdot b^{5/4}c + b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{1/4}c^{2} &= (b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{5/4})c + (b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{1/4})c^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{(3/4 + 5/4)}c + b^{(3/4 + 1/4)}c^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{8/4}c + b^{4/4}c^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{2}c + bc^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228844 1069 "e-21387" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228844 1071 "Given that $15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x) = k(3^x)$, find the value of $k$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228844 1072 #uuid "f0b9a551-7c83-571d-9f5c-cf305f53b856" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228844 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228845 1069 "e-21388" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228845 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{p^7 q + p q^3}{p^4}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228845 1072 #uuid "43c75881-b687-5907-b9a8-63d57480674d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228845 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p^7 q + p q^3}{p^4} = \\left(p^7 q + p q^3 \\right) p^{-4}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\left(p^7 q + p q^3 \\right) p^{-4} = p^7 q \\cdot p^{-4} + p q^3 \\cdot p^{-4}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{7}q \\cdot p^{-4} + pq^{3} \\cdot p^{-4} &= (p^{7} \\cdot p^{-4})q + (p \\cdot p^{-4})q^{3} \\\\\n&= p^{7 - 4}q + p^{1 - 4}q^{3} \\\\\n&= p^{3}q + p^{-3}q^{3} \\\\\n&= p^{3}q + \\frac{q^{3}}{p^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228846 1069 "e-21389" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228846 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u}{v^{7/8}}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228846 1072 #uuid "093ecacc-48cd-5a1c-87c1-3fbbb18da257" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228846 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u}{v^{7/8}} = \\left(v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u \\right) v^{-7/8}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\left(v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u \\right) v^{-7/8} = v^{15/8} u^2 \\cdot v^{-7/8} + v^{5/4} u \\cdot v^{-7/8}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^{15/8}u^{2} \\cdot v^{-7/8} + v^{5/4}u \\cdot v^{-7/8} &= (v^{15/8} \\cdot v^{-7/8})u^{2} + (v^{5/4} \\cdot v^{-7/8})u \\\\\n&= v^{15/8 - 7/8}u^{2} + v^{10/8 - 7/8}u \\\\\n&= v^{8/8}u^{2} + v^{3/8}u \\\\\n&= vu^{2} + v^{3/8}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write fractional exponents as radicals:\n\n$$\nv u^2 + v^{3/8} u = v u^2 + u \\sqrt[8]{v^3}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228847 1069 "e-21393" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228847 1071 "![](images/4e/4edaea6bfc20875e49ddbace3698b12a4bf5d392b0fc1663a1127a0909120244.png)\n\nThe histogram above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. What is the smallest possible value of the median?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228847 1072 #uuid "165b09d2-ce59-5a46-9bc2-643822e34cde" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228847 1074 "To estimate the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $15-18$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $18-21$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $3$ | $15$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $17$ |\n| $27-30$ | $2$ | $19$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\n7+5+3+2+2=19\n$$\n\nSince this is an odd number, the median is the value in position\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{19+1}{2}=\\dfrac{20}{2}=10\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $10$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the first row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $10$th value falls in the second interval, which is\n$18 - 21$\nas shown below.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $15-18$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $18-21$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $3$ | $15$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $17$ |\n| $27-30$ | $2$ | $19$ |\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of the median is $18$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228848 1069 "e-21394" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228848 1071 "| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $22-25$ | $4$ |\n| $25-28$ | $7$ |\n| $28-31$ | $2$ |\n| $31-34$ | $5$ |\n| $34-37$ | $3$ |\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the largest possible value of the mean?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228848 1072 #uuid "33dabfa5-ee83-5fe6-80f3-8e2a2be24c46" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228848 1074 "To estimate the mean from grouped data, we first use the frequency table.\n\nSince we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the largest possible value of $x$ for each group.\n\nThen, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.\n\n| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ | $x_{\\textrm{max}}$ | $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $22-25$ | $4$ | $25$ | $4\\cdot 25 = 100$ |\n| $25-28$ | $7$ | $28$ | $7\\cdot 28 = 196$ |\n| $28-31$ | $2$ | $31$ | $2\\cdot 31 = 62$ |\n| $31-34$ | $5$ | $34$ | $5\\cdot 34 = 170$ |\n| $34-37$ | $3$ | $37$ | $3\\cdot 37 = 111$ |\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlargest possible mean &= \\frac{100 + 196 + 62 + 170 + 111}{4 + 7 + 2 + 5 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{639}{21} \\\\\n&\\approx 30.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228849 1069 "e-21395" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228849 1071 "![](images/fe/febc81f3c303794d406f02c8970d622339eee05728ec196ffc912f040b9a1739.png)\n\nThe histogram above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. What is the smallest possible value of the range?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228849 1072 #uuid "b162ccd4-16a2-5ec4-9b5f-de869cd81d39" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228849 1074 "To calculate the smallest possible range, we take the smallest possible maximum value and subtract the largest possible minimum value.\n\nLet's start by turning our histogram into a frequency table.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $30-\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{35}$ | $6$ |\n| $36-37$ | $8$ |\n| $38-40$ | $10$ |\n| $41-43$ | $7$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{44}-51$ | $4$ |\n\nLooking at the table,\n\n- the smallest possible maximum value is $44$, and\n- the largest possible minimum value is $35$.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible range is\n\n$$\n44 - 35 = 9\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228850 1069 "e-21396" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228850 1071 "![](images/b7/b752a3bb5f5ce53216e6765914da7d306acbf3063ca20293b503e69727a093dd.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the **smallest possible difference** between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228850 1072 #uuid "a84b3eb8-e84b-5b50-8b53-8b26961126df" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228850 1074 "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228851 1069 "e-21397" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228851 1071 "| **Ivy** | $3.6$ | $3.8$ | $4.0$ | $3.9$ | $4.2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Jack** | $4.4$ | $4.2$ | $4.0$ | $3.8$ | $x$ |\n\nIvy and Jack each attempted the math test five times, and their scores are represented in the table above. The mean score for Jack’s attempts was $0.2$ greater than the mean score for Ivy’s attempts. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228851 1072 #uuid "ac12ff06-08ab-5d7c-84ae-0a977fc74152" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228851 1074 "Let $I$ and $J$ represent the means of Ivy’s and Jack’s scores, respectively.\n\nFirst, we compute Ivy’s mean:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\frac{1}{5}(3.6 + 3.8 + 4.0 + 3.9 + 4.2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{19.5}{5} \\\\\n&= 3.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince Jack’s mean is $0.2$ greater than Ivy’s mean, Jack’s mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= I + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 3.9 + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 4.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the expression for Jack’s mean:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\frac{1}{5}(4.4 + 4.2 + 4.0 + 3.8 + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(16.4 + x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate it to $4.1$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= 4.1 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{5}(x + 16.4) &= 4.1 \\\\\n5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(x + 16.4) &= 5 \\cdot 4.1 \\\\\nx + 16.4 &= 20.5 \\\\\nx &= 20.5 - 16.4 \\\\\nx &= 4.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228852 1069 "e-21398" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228852 1071 "$x,\\: 5,\\: 10,\\: 4,\\: 7,\\: 12,\\: 4,\\: 5$\nIn the data set above, $x$ is an integer. If the median of the data set is $6$ and $x < 9$, what are all possible values of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228852 1072 #uuid "3baa38da-a612-5922-b09c-9037d4c02250" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228852 1074 "Notice that our data set contains $8$ values. So, the median is the average of the values in the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{2}=4 \\text{th position} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{8}{2}+1=5 \\text{th position}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the data in ascending order, ignoring $x{:}$\n$4,\\; 4,\\; 5,\\; 5,\\; 7,\\; 10,\\; 12$\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider all possibilities for the value of $x$.\n\n- If $x \\leq 4$, then we have the following ordered data:\n$x,\\; 4,\\; 4,\\; \\underline{5},\\; \\underline{5},\\; 7,\\; 10,\\; 12$\nThe $4$th and $5$th values are $5$ and $5$, so the median is $\\dfrac{5+5}{2}=5$, not $6$. $\\color{red}\\times$\n- If $x = 5$, then we have the following ordered data:\n$4,\\; 4,\\; 5,\\; \\underline{5},\\; \\underline{5},\\; 7,\\; 10,\\; 12$\nThe $4$th and $5$th values are $5$ and $5$, so the median is $\\dfrac{5+5}{2}=5$, not $6$. $\\color{red}\\times$\n- If $x = 6$, then we have the following ordered data:\n$4,\\; 4,\\; 5,\\; \\underline{5},\\; \\underline{6},\\; 7,\\; 10,\\; 12$\nThe $4$th and $5$th values are $5$ and $6$, so the median is $\\dfrac{5+6}{2}=5.5$, not $6$. $\\color{red}\\times$\n- If $x = 7$, then we have the following ordered data:\n$4,\\; 4,\\; 5,\\; \\underline{5},\\; \\underline{7},\\; 7,\\; 10,\\; 12$\nThe $4$th and $5$th values are $5$ and $7$, respectively. So, the median is $\\dfrac{5+7}{2}=6$. $\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark$\n- If $x = 8$, then we have the following ordered data:\n$4,\\; 4,\\; 5,\\; \\underline{5},\\; \\underline{7},\\; 8,\\; 10,\\; 12$\nThe $4$th and $5$th values are still $5$ and $7$, so the median is $\\dfrac{5+7}{2}=6$. $\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the possible values of $x$ are $7$ and $8$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228853 1069 "e-21399" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228853 1071 "$2, \\: 5,\\; x, \\: 7,\\; 9,\\; 5,\\; 7$\nThe rainfall amounts (in millimeters) measured over a week are shown above. The data set has at least two modes. Determine all possible values of $x$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228853 1072 #uuid "b931aa5b-26f6-52e9-82a2-2ddcb0f3b50f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228853 1074 "The mode is the value that occurs the most often in the data set.\n\nNotice that $5$ and $7$ each occur twice, while all other values occur less than twice. So, $5$ and $7$ are candidates for the modes.\n\nNow, $x$ can't be equal to $5$ or $7$. Otherwise, one of these values would occur three times in the data set, meaning that it's the only mode.\n\nBut $x$ could be either $2$ or $9$. If so, one of these values would also occur twice in the data set, meaning that we would have three modes.\n\nTherefore, $x$ could be any integer other than $5$ or $7$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228854 1069 "e-21401" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228854 1071 "The mean score in a science fair with $19$ participants was $29$ points. After the lowest-scoring participant is removed, the mean score of the remaining $18$ participants becomes $30$ points. What was the lowest score?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228854 1072 #uuid "cbd5e1d2-531d-5845-b13a-da8deb9298bd" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228854 1074 "The sum of the values in a dataset is the product of the mean and the number of values.\n\nLet $S_{\\text{orig}}$ be the total points for all $19$ participants, and $S_{\\text{new}}$ be the total for the remaining $18$ participants. So, the sums are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{orig} &= 19 \\cdot 29 = 551.0 \\\\\nS_{new} &= 18 \\cdot 30 = 540.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe removed participant’s score is the difference:\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{orig}} - S_{\\text{new}} = 551.0 - 540.0 = 11\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lowest score was ${11}$ points." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228855 1069 "e-21402" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228855 1071 "A dataset of $19$ different grocery basket totals (in dollars) has a mean of $\\[math]50$ and a median of $\\[/math]50$. A new dataset is created by multiplying each total by $2$. Which of the following measures has the same value in both the original and new datasets?\n\n1. Mean\n2. Median\n3. Range\n4. Standard deviation" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228855 1072 #uuid "dc6e1921-8533-5d9e-b860-431be361c63c" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228855 1074 "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$\\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228856 1069 "e-21403" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228856 1071 "![](images/08/08393d3efaa9c991339009fb6e8b825b313efff31328416df7d5ad33f4019cb5.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows an approximate trend line for the relationship between two variables $x$ and $y$ in a dataset $R$.\n\nA new dataset $S$ is formed by multiplying the $y$-coordinate of every data point of $R$ by $2.8$. Which of the following could be an equation of a trend line for dataset $S$?\n\n1. $y=-0.4x+44.8$\n2. $y=-1.12x+16$\n3. $y=-0.4x+16$\n4. $y=-1.12x+44.8$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228856 1072 #uuid "a2a74344-2686-5dba-82ee-bfef40fb0e83" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228856 1074 "First, we find an approximate trend line for $R$ from the plot.\n\n![](images/26/261e8519a74291ade62e045a2767de828aeb5feda7d70dd7206f934fad26f6cf.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) \\approx (15,10)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) \\approx (27.5,5)\n$$\n\nSo, an estimate of the slope $m$ of the line is\n\n$$\nm \\mid \\approx \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}; = \\frac{5 - 10}{27.5 - 15}; = ((-5))/(12.5); =-0.4\n$$\n\nSo, an approximate trend line, in slope-intercept form, is\n\n$$\ny = -0.4x + b\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is the $y$-intercept to be estimated. We do this by substituting in the coordinates of a point on the line, say $(15,10){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -0.4x + b \\\\\n10 &= -0.4(15) + b \\\\\n10 &= -6 + b \\\\\n16 &= b \\\\\nb &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the trend line for dataset $R$ is\n\n$$\ny = -0.4x + 16\n$$\n\nWe're told that dataset $S$ is formed by multiplying the $y$-coordinate of every data point of $R$ by $2.8$.\n\nLet $y_R$ be a $y$-value in $R$ and $y_S$ be the corresponding $y$-value in $S$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{S} &= 2.8y_{R} \\\\\n&= 2.8(-0.4x + 16) \\\\\n&=-1.12x + 44.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, a suitable line of best fit for $S$ is\n\n$$\ny = -1.12x + 44.8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228857 1069 "e-21404" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228857 1071 "The delivery times (in minutes) for $22$ parcels were recorded, and the mean, median, range, and standard deviation were calculated. Later, it was discovered that the shortest delivery time had been reported as $9$ minutes too high.\nWhich of the following measures does **NOT** have the same value in both the original and new datasets?\n\n1. Mean\n2. Median\n3. Range\n4. Standard deviation" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228857 1072 #uuid "d167bf3d-f5c4-549c-8771-efb263a7daaf" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228857 1074 "When a single data value is corrected, each of the four statistics may respond differently:\n\n- The mean depends on every value, so changing one value will change the mean.\n- The median depends only on the middle value(s). In this case, adjusting the shortest time to be smaller than it originally was does not affect the order of the middle values, so the median remains unchanged.\n- The range depends only on the largest minus the smallest. Here, since the smallest value decreases, the range becomes larger.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread from the mean, and changing one value changes both the mean and the spread.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III and IV only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228858 1069 "e-21407" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228858 1071 "The dot plot above shows the $19$ values in the data set $C$. Data set $D$ is formed by subtracting $12$ to every value in data set $C$. Write the statement that compares the medians and the ranges of data sets $C$ and $D$.\n\n![](images/51/513ba0338ebaaa07691872b14a393975c48c51a8197ad4769769a0a28e36ed23.png)" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228858 1072 #uuid "cd481c39-c4ff-56fe-92cd-6a2f31c4c015" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228858 1074 "Let's compare the medians and ranges of data sets $C$ and $D{:}$\n\n- Medians:There are $19$ values, so the median of $C$ is the $10$th value when ordered. Counting frequencies across $9,10,11,12,13$ gives cumulative counts $3,7,12,16,19$, so the $10$th value lies at\n$\\text{Median}(C)=11$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the median of $D$ will still be the $10$th value, but shifted down by $12{:}$\n$\\text{Median}(D)=11-12=-1$.Thus, the median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$.\n- Ranges: The range of data set $C{:}$\n$\\text{Range}(C)=\\max(C)-\\min(C)=13-9=4$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the range of data set $D{:}$\n$Range(D)|= (max(C) - 12) - (min(C) - 12); = max(C) - 12 - min(C) + 12; = max(C) - min(C); = Range(C)$.Thus, the ranges are equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is “The median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$, and the range of data set $D$ is equal to the range of data set $C$.”" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228859 1069 "e-21408" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228859 1071 "A server engineer recorded the response payload sizes, in kilobytes (KB), for two APIs. This information is displayed using the dot plots shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n![](images/7e/7ea58bd153e0dd0db52a407af9d83848d678d1959ec9012f9364da0ee2cee04d.png)\n\n1. The standard deviation of API $\\text{A}$ is greater than the standard deviation of API $\\text{B}$.\n2. The standard deviation of API $\\text{A}$ is smaller than the standard deviation of API $\\text{B}$.\n3. The standard deviation of API $\\text{A}$ is equal to the standard deviation of API $\\text{B}$.\n4. The mean of API $\\text{A}$ is smaller than the mean of API $\\text{B}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228859 1072 #uuid "91f53ac2-ed70-5ab1-b0c1-6403763f8d6d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228859 1074 "Standard deviation is a statistical measure that indicates how much the values in a dataset deviate from the mean.\n\nA larger standard deviation means the data points are more spread out, while a smaller standard deviation indicates that the data points are clustered more closely around the mean.\n\n![](images/b3/b3873d6f316a467c242d7fd84b56183ccfa727fe80f693c316bf7b1fe1d63d39.png)\n\nIn our case, the ranges of the datasets are the same, but the dot plots have different shapes.\n\nNotice that the data points corresponding to API $\\text{A}$ are concentrated further from the mean than the data points of API $\\text{B}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n> The standard deviation of API $\\text{A}$ is greater than the standard deviation of API $\\text{B}$.\n\nNote: The data sets have equal means since they're both symmetric and defined over the same range." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228860 1069 "e-21409" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228860 1071 "Three classrooms recorded how many pages each student read during a $1$-hour sprint. The information is summarized on the dot plots above. Put the corresponding standard deviations in order from the smallest to the largest.\n\n![](images/11/11456456a3eab8bc71411dc431c67eaa2f69ec163cc25f45726b5a2af037ad58.png)" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228860 1072 #uuid "2d22ae5d-d871-5dc6-8e7a-a34656881ca4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228860 1074 "Standard deviation is a statistical measure that indicates how much the values in a dataset deviate from the mean.\n\nA larger standard deviation means the data points are more spread out, while a smaller standard deviation indicates that the data points are clustered more closely around the mean.\n\nIn our case:\n\n- The most spread-out dataset corresponds to Classroom B (notice the heavier counts toward the ends).\n- A slightly less spread-out dataset corresponds to Classroom C (more mass near the center, but still some spread).\n- Finally, the data corresponding to Classroom A is mostly concentrated near the mean.\n\nTherefore, we can put the standard deviations in order from the smallest to the largest, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\text{Classroom A}} < {\\text{Classroom C}} < {\\text{Classroom B}}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228861 1069 "e-21411" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228861 1071 "A local survey polled $1,300$ voters about whether they support a new bond measure. The table below summarizes all $1,300$ responses.\n\n|  | Under $40$ | $40$ and Over |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Support | $370$ | $380$ |\n| Do Not Support | $$? | $300$ |\n\nHow many of the voters under $40$ surveyed responded that they do not support the bond measure?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228861 1072 #uuid "d57c8a2b-a893-5d03-927f-d5fb3bd6fccd" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228861 1074 "Let $x$ be the number of voters under $40$ who do not support the measure.\nA total of $1,300$ voters were surveyed.\n\nFurthermore, the total number of voters surveyed is the sum of the total number of voters under $40$ and the total number of voters $40$ and over.\nSo, we can set up the following equation, and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n370 + x + 380 + 300 &= 1, 300 \\\\\nx + 1, 050 &= 1, 300 \\\\\nx &= 1, 300 - 1, 050 \\\\\n&= 250\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of voters under $40$ who do not support the measure is $250$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228862 1069 "e-21415" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228862 1071 "![](images/73/7353703070fc133dac46f7b44c6845290fe3cd074229aef53c52486dfbe381af.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows $9$ data points in the relationship between two variables $x$ and $y$, along with a line of best fit. For how many of the $9$ data points is the actual $y$-value less than the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228862 1072 #uuid "7794de81-cb5e-5e79-a8e3-b477dab6e54f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228862 1074 "A data point's predicted $y$-value is the $y$-value of the point on the line of best fit at the same $x$-coordinate as the point.\n\nA data point's actual $y$-value is less than the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit if it lies below this line.\n\n![](images/24/24f596b39c457b6f528c2d8cd7c4f66ecd206909c4a6f884b736d252f3d9b52c.png)\n\nCounting all such cases gives a total of $3$ data points below the line.\n\nTherefore, the number of data points with an actual $y$-value less than the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit is $3$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228863 1069 "e-21416" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228863 1071 "![](images/04/044bf7e1eb0668ef4f62cb347b3cbb8b50b454f93a2999588346d04b20294d30.png)\n\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between $x$ and $y$. A line of best fit for the data is also shown. What is the difference between the $y$-coordinate of the data point with $x=5$ and the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit at $x=5$? Rounded to one decimal place." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228863 1072 #uuid "06a754a7-91da-5c41-af10-ec1eb9478bb4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228863 1074 "Explanation:\n\nWe want to compare the actual $y$-value with the predicted $y$-value from the line of best fit at $x = 5$.\n\nFrom the graph, we observe that:\n\n- The data point with $x=5$ has a $y$-coordinate of $18$.\n- At $x=5$, the line of best fit reaches exactly $17.5$ on the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, the difference between the data point and the predicted value is\n\n$$\n\\bigl|\\,18-17.5\\,\\bigr| \\approx 0.5\n$$\n\nAmong the given answer choices, the closest value to this estimate is $0.5$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228864 1069 "e-21417" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228864 1071 "![](images/a0/a0cab286e2d3b5f8f46e94595ef94ac8ec8a09b6259ca4372fe546ac7faa2c73.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows several data points in the relationship between two variables $x$ and $y$ along with a line of best fit.\nWhich of the labeled data points $A$, $B$, or $C$ has a $y$-value that is at least $10$ less than the value predicted by the line of best fit?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228864 1072 #uuid "cf8985db-bd21-5438-85aa-1fbc9fb3a224" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228864 1074 "A data point's predicted $y$-value is the $y$-value of the point on the line of best fit at the same $x$-coordinate as the point.\n\nA data point's actual $y$-value is less than the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit if it lies below this line.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given points in turn.\n\n- First, consider data point $A$ with coordinates $(22,35)$. At $x=22$, the line of best fit predicts a corresponding $y$-value of $y \\approx 48$.![](images/52/522e43eed33531850f5c8bbaede874d1801337ec6e8ce4385ee744e336187a37.png)On the other hand, the actual $y$-value of $A$ is $y=35$. So, the difference between the actual and predicted $y$-values is\n$48 - 35 = 13 \\geq 10$.\nHence, the actual $y$-value of $A$ *is* at least $10$ less than the predicted $y$-value.\n- Next, consider data point $B$ with coordinates $(25,65)$. Since $B$ lies above the line, its $y$-value is *greater than* the value predicted by the line of best fit, not less than.![](images/79/7909b3614cc46e94f7e2885bfb64fca50d9ef8750530f33352818592a0e4e963.png)Hence, the actual $y$-value of $B$ *is not* at least $10$ less than the predicted $y$-value.\n\n- Finally, consider data point $C$ with coordinates $(27,45)$. At $x=27$, the line of best fit predicts a corresponding $y$-value of $y \\approx 59$.![](images/8e/8ef254be0a4cc1016cc560b84a2438a594ccde256f62493f2c70f2faf87c7575.png) On the other hand, the actual $y$-value of $C$ is $y=45$. So, the difference between the actual and predicted $y$-values is\n$59 - 45 = 14 \\geq 10$.\nHence, the actual $y$-value of $C$ *is* at least $10$ less than the predicted $y$-value.\n\nTherefore, the actual $y$-values of $A$ and $C$ are at least $10$ less than the predicted $y$-values." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228865 1069 "e-21418" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228865 1071 "![](images/41/41b3740196c4531e157a7b3ffd718487987e6cf77124cb4d91e85fdabec1e2d9.png)\n\nA productivity audit tracked $27$ workdays. For each workday, the average time in meetings ($x$, in hours) and the average time in focused work ($y$, in hours) were recorded to the nearest half hour. The scatter plot above shows the data with a line of best fit.\n\nThe line of best fit **overestimates** one workday’s reported focused-work time by more than $1$ hour. How many hours of meetings were recorded this day?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228865 1072 #uuid "eaa71178-e65f-5192-b73a-0b9a5bc7ddd6" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228865 1074 "The line of best fit predicts a day's average time in focused work ($y$) given its average time in meetings ($x$).\n\nThe line *overestimates* a day’s reported focused-work time if its corresponding point on the scatter plot lies *below* the line.\n\n![](images/27/27bb381f24e6cd8c12fe4fd8446b2b8adfc821c664eb8d7bdd6ba402cc0c8a36.png)\n\nWe are told that, for one of these points, the line of best fit overestimates the day’s reported focused-work time by more than $1$ hour.\n\nConsider the data point at $(2,3)$. For a day with $x=2$ hours of meetings, the line of best fit predicts approximately\n\n$$\ny \\approx 4.2\n$$\n\nhours of focused work.\n\n![](images/81/810e16c6ce864b4c5dbd5178f6ee6bfd12251b079b3152d1e741b8e3f84791e5.png)\n\nBut the day corresponding to $(2,3)$ actually has $y=3$ hours of focused work. So, the line of best fit overestimates this day’s focused-work hours by\n\n$$\n4.2 - 3 = 1.2 > 1\n$$\n\nNo other point is that far below the line, so this must be the day in question. Therefore, $2$ hours of meetings were recorded this day." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228866 1069 "e-21419" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228866 1071 "![](images/11/11105ba18d0d1fec18f48309e2ec378d316e3357252a7a8e1328d2d6c8fa3302.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows an approximate trend line for the relationship between two variables $x$ and $y$ in a dataset $R$.\n\nA new dataset $S$ is formed by multiplying the $x$-coordinate of every data point of $R$ by $2$. Which of the following could be an equation of a trend line for dataset $S$?\n\n1. $y=1.5x + \\dfrac 2 3$\n2. $y=0.75x+2$\n3. $y=0.75x+\\dfrac 2 3$\n4. $y=0.5x+2$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228866 1072 #uuid "eb690bf5-082a-5ae1-90b2-c0214553331a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228866 1074 "First, we find an approximate trend line for $R$ from the plot.\n\n![](images/9a/9a21a7f3d6768769f0884dd4a2c712f05cd6e62002569abe9ab593fa5f1b9c30.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) \\approx (4,8)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) \\approx (12,20)\n$$\n\nSo, an estimate of the slope $m$ of the line is\n\n$$\nm \\mid \\approx \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}; = \\frac{20 - 8}{12 - 4}; = \\frac{12}{8}; = 1.5\n$$\n\nSo, an approximate trend line, in slope-intercept form, is\n\n$$\ny=1.5x+b\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is the $y$-intercept to be estimated. We do this by substituting in the coordinates of a point on the line, say $(4,8){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 1.5x + b \\\\\n8 &= 1.5(4) + b \\\\\n8 &= 6 + b \\\\\n2 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the trend line for dataset $R$ is\n\n$$\ny=1.5x+2\n$$\n\nWe're told that dataset $S$ is formed by multiplying the $x$-coordinate of every data point of $R$ by $2$.\n\nLet $x_R$ be a $x$-value in $R$ and $x_S$ be the corresponding $x$-value in $S$. Then, we have\n\n$$\nx_S = 2 x_R\n$$\n\nSubsituting\n\n$$\nx_R = \\dfrac {x_S} 2\n$$\n\ninto the equation for $R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{S} &= 1.5(\\frac{x_{S}}{2}) + 2 \\\\\n&= 0.75x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, a suitable line of best fit for $S$ is\n\n$$\ny=0.75x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only II.**" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228867 1069 "e-21421" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228867 1071 "A video's view count increases by $220\\%$ each day. If $V(t)$ denotes the number of views after $t$ days, with $t\\ge 0$, what type of function best models the relationship between $V(t)$ and $t$?\n\n1. Decreasing linear\n2. Increasing exponential\n3. Increasing linear\n4. Decreasing exponential" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228867 1072 #uuid "14b4164d-9cca-5388-9899-1c43fc5f94aa" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228867 1074 "The view count increases by $220\\%$ each day. $220\\%$ as a decimal is $2.20$. Therefore, an increase of $220\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1 + 2.20 = 3.20\n$$\n\nLet $V_0$ be the initial view count. Then, we have:\n\n- After $1$ day, there will be $V_0 \\cdot (3.20)$ views.\n- After $\\color{blue}2$ days, there will be $V_0 \\cdot (3.20)^{\\color{blue}2}$ views.\n- After $\\color{blue}3$ days, there will be $V_0 \\cdot (3.20)^{\\color{blue}3}$ views.$\\qquad\\vdots$\n- After $\\color{blue}t$ days, there will be $V_0 \\cdot (3.20)^{\\color{blue}t}$ views.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 \\cdot (3.20)^{\\color{blue}t}\n$$\n\nwhich is an exponential function.\n\nFinally, since the base $(3.20)$ is greater than $1$, $V$ increases as $t$ increases.\nTherefore, $V$ is an increasing exponential function." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228868 1069 "e-21425" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228868 1071 "The total rainfall recorded after $8$ weeks was $1.42$ times the total rainfall recorded after $4$ weeks. If the rainfall after $4$ weeks is $z\\%$ of the rainfall after $8$ weeks, what is the value of $z$, to the nearest whole number?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228868 1072 #uuid "3f487827-0616-5dde-828e-948066e88c09" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228868 1074 "Let $R_{4}$ be the total rainfall after $4$ weeks, and $R_{8}$ be the total rainfall after $8$ weeks.\n\nWe are told that the rainfall after $8$ weeks was $1.42$ times the rainfall after $4$ weeks. This can be expressed as\n\n$$\nR_{8} = 1.42 \\cdot R_{4}\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\nR_{4} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{1.42}} \\cdot R_{8}\n$$\n\nNow, if the rainfall after $4$ weeks is $z\\%$ of the rainfall after $8$ weeks, we obtain the equation\n\n$$\nR_{4} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{z}{100}} \\cdot R_{8}\n$$\n\nComparing both equations for $R_{4}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{z}{100}} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{1.42}}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving for $z$ in the last equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\frac{100}{1.42} \\\\\n&= 70.422… \\\\\n&\\approx 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228869 1069 "e-21426" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228869 1071 "An athlete increased the weight lifted during training from $160$ pounds to $192$ pounds. The new weight was $p\\%$ of the original weight. What is the value of $p$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228869 1072 #uuid "409c3afc-9a26-56d9-af22-ee7080b3b381" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228869 1074 "We are told that $192$ pounds was $p\\%$ of $160$ pounds.\n\nFirst, we express $p\\%$ of $160$ as a fraction and set it equal to $192$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 160 = 192\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 160 &= 192 \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= \\frac{192}{160} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= 1.2 \\\\\np &= 1.2 \\cdot 100 \\\\\np &= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is $120$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228870 1069 "e-21428" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228870 1071 "A table shows the average annual rainfall in a region for six different years.\n\n| Year | Average rainfall (cm) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1985$ | $100$ |\n| $1995$ | $120$ |\n| $2005$ | $150$ |\n| $2010$ | $180$ |\n| $2015$ | $200$ |\n| $2025$ | $220$ |\n\nWhat was the percentage increase in rainfall from $1995$ to $2010$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228870 1072 #uuid "d77a4165-5c86-5afe-a071-6657d7745b3c" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228870 1074 "To find the percentage increase, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Percentage Increase} = \\frac{\\text{New Value} - \\text{Original Value}}{\\text{Original Value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nThe original value is $120$ (in $1995$), and the new value is $180$ (in $2010$). Substituting these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPercentage Increase &= \\frac{180 - 120}{120} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{120} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.50 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 50%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rainfall increased by $50\\%$ from $1995$ to $2010$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228871 1069 "e-21431" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228871 1071 "A total of $34,\\!852$ students study at a certain university. The university conducted a survey among a randomly chosen sample of $1,\\!200$ students about their preference for morning or evening classes. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n|  | Morning | Evening | No Preference | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Freshmen and Sophomores | $280$ | $190$ | $30$ | $500$ |\n| Juniors and Seniors | $210$ | $150$ | $40$ | $400$ |\n| Graduates | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ | $300$ |\n| Total | $610$ | $440$ | $150$ | $1,\\!200$ |\n\nWhat is the difference between the percentage of undergraduates (freshmen, sophomores, juniors, and seniors combined) who preferred morning classes and the percentage of graduate students who preferred morning classes? Rounded to the nearest whole number." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228871 1072 #uuid "5e8c0206-a8ab-5b55-8e08-c9d5ffba5554" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228871 1074 "To calculate a percentage, we take the part as a fraction of the whole and then multiply the fraction by $100\\%$.\n\nWe will first calculate the percentage for undergraduates, then calculate the percentage for graduate students.\n\n- The total number of undergraduates is\n$500 + 400 = 900$,\nand the number of undergraduates who preferred morning classes is\n$280 + 210 = 490$.\nSo, the percentage of undergraduates who preferred morning classes is\n$\\dfrac{490}{900} \\times 100\\% \\approx 54.4\\%$.\n- Out of $300$ graduate students, the number who preferred morning classes is $120$. So, the percentage of graduate students who preferred morning classes is\n$\\dfrac{120}{300} \\times 100\\% = 40\\%$.\n\nFinally, the difference between the percentages is\n\n$$\n54.4\\% - 40\\% = 14.4\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to the nearest whole number, the difference is $14\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228872 1069 "e-21432" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228872 1071 "A hospital tracked the number of patient visits in two consecutive years, as shown below.\n\n| Year | Patient visits |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2020$ | $30,\\!000$ |\n| $2021$ | $31,\\!500$ |\n\nThe hospital administrator estimated that the percentage increase from $2021$ to $2022$ would be four times the percentage increase from $2020$ to $2021$. How many visits did the administrator expect in $2022$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228872 1072 #uuid "b789a112-e607-5050-852a-f8538179f903" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228872 1074 "To find a percentage increase, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Percentage Increase} = \\dfrac{\\text{New Value} - \\text{Original Value}}{\\text{Original Value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nFrom $2020$ to $2021$, the percentage increase is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPercentage Increase &= \\frac{31, 500 - 30, 000}{30, 000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{1, 500}{30, 000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.05 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 5%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the percentage increase from $2021$ to $2022$ would be four times $5\\%$, the percentage increase is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 =20\\%\n$$\n\nSo, in $2021$, the original value is $31,\\!500$ and the percentage increase is $20\\%$. We use it now to find the new value corresponding to the expected number of visits in $2022{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20% &= \\frac{New Value - 31, 500}{31, 500} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n0.20 &= \\frac{New Value - 31, 500}{31, 500} \\\\\n6, 300 &= New Value - 31, 500 \\\\\nNew Value &= 37, 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the administrator expected $37,\\!800$ visits in $2022$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228873 1069 "e-21433" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228873 1071 "A bike shop buys helmets at a wholesale cost of $\\[math]20.00$ each and sells them at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining helmet is sold at a $40\\%$ discount from the retail price. What is the discounted price of a helmet?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228873 1072 #uuid "fa84fe77-30e7-5f76-8dce-f5112a8913e0" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228873 1074 "First, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. Here, the wholesale cost is $\\[math]20.00$. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $20$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180% \\cdot 20 &= \\frac{180}{100} \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 1.8 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price of a helmet is $\\[/math]36.00$.\n\nNow, we're also told that there is a $40\\%$ discount on the retail price. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[math]36.00$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $36$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40% \\cdot 36 &= \\frac{40}{100} \\cdot 36 \\\\\n&= 0.40 \\cdot 36 \\\\\n&= 14.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[/math]14.40$. We subtract this amount from the retail price:\n\n$$\n36 - 14.4 = 21.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the discounted price of a helmet is $\\[math]21.60$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228874 1069 "e-21434" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228874 1071 "A pair of gloves has a regular price of $\\[math]35$. During a weekend sale, the gloves are sold at a price that is $40\\%$ less than the regular price. This sale price is also $40\\%$ more than what the retailer paid per pair. What was the retailer’s cost for each pair of gloves?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228874 1072 #uuid "a5712d30-490e-573a-9d59-5d39ab1c9164" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228874 1074 "There is a $40\\%$ discount on the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\[math]35$. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[/math]35$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $35$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40% \\cdot 35 &= \\frac{40}{100} \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= 0.40 \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[math]14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\[/math]21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\[math]21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n140% \\cdot p &= 21 \\\\\n\\frac{140}{100} \\cdot p &= 21 \\\\\n1.4p &= 21 \\\\\np &= \\frac{21}{1.4} \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retailer’s cost per pair of gloves was $\\[/math]15$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228875 1069 "e-21435" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228875 1071 "A craft store sells cutting mats at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During a seasonal sale, each mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. If the final selling price of a mat is $\\[math]33.75$, what is the wholesale cost per mat?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228875 1072 #uuid "91ded586-5b18-5754-9fc2-57ffbc50b980" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228875 1074 "Let $p$ be the wholesale cost per mat.\n\nFirst, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $p$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180% \\cdot p &= \\frac{180}{100} \\cdot p \\\\\n&= 1.8p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price is $1.8p$ dollars.\n\nNow, we're also told that the mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. The amount of the discount is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25% \\cdot (1.8p) &= \\frac{25}{100} \\cdot 1.8p \\\\\n&= 0.45p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final selling price is the retail price minus the discount:\n\n$$\n1.8p - 0.45p = 1.35p\n$$\n\nWe are told this equals $\\[math]33.75$, so we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35p &= 33.75 \\\\\np &= \\frac{33.75}{1.35} \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wholesale cost per mat is $\\[/math]25.00$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228876 1069 "e-21436" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228876 1071 "A luggage store lists a travel backpack with a set regular price. During a holiday promotion, the backpack is sold at a price that is $20\\%$ less than the regular price. The discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. If the final selling price is $\\[math]32.00$, what is the regular price?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228876 1072 #uuid "cf4330e8-7ed6-542a-bb01-4d06cca75756" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228876 1074 "Let $p$ be the regular price.\n\nThere is a $20\\%$ discount on the regular price. Subtract this from the regular price:\n\n$$\np - 0.20p = 0.80p\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price is $0.80p$ dollars.\n\nWe are also told that the discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. Here, the final selling price is $\\[math]32.00$. So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n140% \\cdot 32 &= \\frac{140}{100} \\cdot 32 \\\\\n&= 1.4 \\cdot 32 \\\\\n&= 44.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the discounted price is $\\[/math]44.80$. Since the discounted price also equals $0.80p$, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.80p &= 44.8 \\\\\np &= \\frac{44.8}{0.80} \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the regular price is $\\[math]\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{56.00}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228877 1069 "e-21438" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228877 1071 "Mateo has $310$ British pounds $(\\textrm{£}310)$ that he wants to exchange for US dollars at a currency kiosk. The exchange rate is $\\textrm{£}1 = \\[math]1.34$. The kiosk charges a $2.5\\%$ fee on the converted US dollar amount. How many US dollars will Mateo receive after the $2.5\\%$ fee?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228877 1072 #uuid "0cd26655-7de6-562f-b074-29c3bf354563" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228877 1074 "We are told that $\\textrm{£}1$ is equivalent to $\\[math]1.34$. So, to convert $\\textrm{£}310$ to US dollars, we multiply it by the exchange rate:\n\n$$\n310 \\cdot 1.34 = 415.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{£}310$ is equivalent to $\\[/math]415.4$.\n\nNow, because Mateo pays a $2.5\\%$ fee on the converted amount, he receives\n\n$$\n(100 - 2.5)\\% = 97.5\\%\n$$\n\nof the converted US dollars.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n415.4 \\cdot 97.5\\% = 415.4 \\cdot 0.975 = 405.015\n$$\n\nwe conclude that Mateo receives a total of $\\[math]405.015$ after the conversion." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228878 1069 "e-21440" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228878 1071 "A paint sprayer applies $132c\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of paint in $8d$ minutes at a constant rate. Which expression represents the number of milliliters applied in $3d$ minutes at this rate?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228878 1072 #uuid "4204afb0-3304-5972-a0b3-54769d8b9def" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228878 1074 "The ratio of volume of paint applied to the number of minutes is given by\n\n$$\n132c\\,\\textrm{mL} \\::\\: 8d\\,\\textrm{min}\n$$\n\nWe need to know the volume of paint applied in $3d$ minutes. To determine this, we\n\n- determine the *unit rate* (milliliters of paint applied per minute), and then\n- multiply by the number of minutes we're interested in $(3d)$.\n\nDividing the above ratio by $8d$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{132c}{8d}\\,\\textrm{mL} \\::\\: 1\\,\\textrm{min}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{132c}{8d}\n$$\n\nmilliliters of paint are applied every minute.\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $3d$, we have\n\n$$\n3d\\cdot \\dfrac{132c}{8d}\\,\\textrm{mL} \\::\\: 3d\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{min}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{396cd}{8d}mL:3dmin; \\frac{396cd}{8d}mL:3dmin; \\frac{99c}{2}mL:3dmin\n$$\n\nTherefore, in $3d$ minutes, the sprayer applies\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{99c}{2}\\,\\textrm{mL}\n$$\n\nof paint." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228879 1069 "e-21441" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228879 1071 "A solar panel mounting tray in the shape of a rectangular solid has a fixed height and a fixed length-to-width ratio of $27:18$. If the width is increased by $14$ units, how should the length be adjusted to keep the same ratio?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228879 1072 #uuid "0ef3ad17-4050-5ab2-8150-e4f3915040eb" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228879 1074 "The fixed ratio of length to width is\n\n$27:18$.\nTo maintain this ratio when dimensions change, the *change* in length and width must share the same ratio.\n\nThe width is increased by $14$ units. To determine the resulting change in length, we\n\n- determine the *unit rate* (change in length per unit change in width), and then\n- multiply by the change in width we're interested in ($14$ units).\n\nDividing the above ratio by $18$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{27}{18} \\::\\: 1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2} \\::\\: 1\n$$\n\nThis tells us that a\n\n$$\n\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nunit change in length results from a $1$ unit change in width.\n\nThen, multiplying this ratio by $14$, we have\n\n$$\n14\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{2} \\::\\: 14 \\cdot 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n21 \\::\\: 14\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the width increases by $14$ units, the length must $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\textrm{increase}}$ by $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{21}$ units." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228880 1069 "e-21442" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228880 1071 "![](images/23/23a66c2b47d2083651c6fe2343fdb6a5b7b8babae51aa2626a0ba5d35ce86ede.png)\n\nThe circle above has center $O$, the length of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is $7\\pi$, and $x = 84$. What is the length of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228880 1072 #uuid "ab48f415-f82f-5c36-86f1-e9f4fe1754e5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228880 1074 "We'll solve this problem by considering the following ratio:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{arc length} \\::\\: \\textrm{proportion of a full turn}\n$$\n\nThe arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is $7\\pi$, which represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{84^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. Therefore, we have the following ratio of arc length to full turn proportion:\n\n$$\n7\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nThus, the remaining arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{7}{30} = \\dfrac{23}{30}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. Our goal is to manipulate the above ratio to yield the arc length corresponding to this proportion.\n\nDividing our ratio by $7$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7π &:\\frac{7}{30} \\\\ \\frac{7π}{7} &:\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ π &:\\frac{1}{30}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that an arc length of $\\pi$ corresponds to one-thirtieth of a full turn.\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $23$, we have\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot \\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{23 \\cdot 1}{30}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n23\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{23}{30}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is $23\\pi$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228881 1069 "e-21443" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228881 1071 "![](images/f7/f79abef0f80761b6897bd7f9a6fb16772066522c1f1e5534dddafb1fd4d20257.png)\n\nThe circle above has center $O$, the length of arcs $\\overset{\\frown}{PTQ}$ and $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ are $20\\pi$ and $15\\pi$, respectively, and $x = 100$. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228881 1072 #uuid "fb132113-6ee4-5fbb-b569-0f03792888e7" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228881 1074 "We'll solve this problem by considering the following ratio:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{arc length} \\::\\: \\textrm{proportion of a full turn}\n$$\n\nThe arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{PTQ}$ is $20\\pi$, which represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100^\\circ}{360^\\circ}=\\dfrac{5}{18}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. We can express this relationship as the following ratio:\n\n$$\n20\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{5}{18}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ is $15\\pi$. Our goal is to manipulate the above ratio to yield the full turn proportion corresponding to this arc length, and use that to compute the value of $y$.\n\nDividing the above ratio by $20$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20π &:\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{20π}{20} &:\\frac{5}{18 \\cdot 20} \\\\ π &:\\frac{1}{72}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $15$, we have\n\n$$\n15\\cdot \\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{15\\cdot 1}{72}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nThis means that the arc length $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nof a full circle. Hence, the measure of the corresponding central angle is\n\n$$\ny^\\circ=\\dfrac{5}{24}\\cdot 360^\\circ=75^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=75$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228882 1069 "e-21444" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228882 1071 "For the values $m$ and $p$, the ratio of $m$ to $p$ is $13$ to $17$. If $m$ is multiplied by $45$, what is $p$ multiplied by in order to maintain the same ratio?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228882 1072 #uuid "2dee4257-78b8-5d14-8ec5-217ff0a05502" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228882 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m}{p}=\\dfrac{13}{17}\n$$\n\nWhen $m$ is multiplied by $45$, the corresponding proportion is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{45\\,m}{x\\,p}=\\dfrac{13}{17}\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the factor by which $p$ must be multiplied to maintain the same ratio.\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{45m}{xp} &= \\frac{13}{17} \\\\\n\\frac{m}{p} \\cdot \\frac{45}{x} &= \\frac{13}{17} \\\\\n\\frac{13}{17} \\cdot \\frac{45}{x} &= \\frac{13}{17} \\\\\n\\frac{45}{x} &= 1 \\\\\n45 &= x \\\\\nx &= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, to maintain the same ratio, $p$ must be multiplied by $45$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228883 1069 "e-21447" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228883 1071 "According to a recent report, $40\\%$ of viewers said they enjoyed documentaries. Of those, $50\\%$ preferred documentaries about nature. What percentage of all viewers preferred nature documentaries?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228883 1072 #uuid "4f528740-2fa6-5441-ab8b-86ce294dbf75" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228883 1074 "Let $x$ be the total number of viewers.\n\nWe're told that $40\\%$ of viewers enjoyed documentaries. Thus, the number of viewers who enjoy documentaries is\n$0.4x$.\n\nWe are also told that of those viewers who enjoy documentaries, $50\\%$ preferred nature topics. So, the number of viewers who preferred nature documentaries is\n\n$$\n0.50(0.4x) = 0.20x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.20x$ represents $20\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $20\\%$ of all viewers preferred nature documentaries." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228884 1069 "e-21452" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228884 1071 "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228884 1072 #uuid "728d9eaf-6814-53b8-a3ac-f270c36c6e83" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228884 1074 "To find an amount that's $14\\%$ *less than* $\\[math]78$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 14\\% = 86\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n86\\% = \\dfrac{86}{100} = 0.86\n$$\n\nSo, to find $86\\%$ of $\\[/math]78$, we multiply $\\[math]78$ by $0.86{:}$\n\n$$\n0.86 \\cdot \\[/math]78 = \\[math]67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the hiking boots is $\\[/math]67.08$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228885 1069 "e-21454" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228885 1071 "A school had $50$ students in its initial year. The following year, it had $80$ students. What was the percentage increase in the number of students between the first and second years?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228885 1072 #uuid "705f1842-0dfb-5016-97df-33550343892e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228885 1074 "$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = 80$ students\n- $\\textrm{original value} = 50$ students\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage change &= \\frac{\\mid 80 - 50\\begin{vmatrix}}{50} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 30\\end{vmatrix}}{50} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{50} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.6 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 60%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $60\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228886 1069 "e-21455" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228886 1071 "Bruce made $24$ muffins for his family last week, later realizing that he made too many. So this week, Bruce only made $15$ muffins. What is the percentage decrease in the number of muffins Bruce produced between the first and second weeks?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228886 1072 #uuid "931578f8-d168-59ca-976f-9cae7332d6ca" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228886 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = 15$ muffins\n- $\\textrm{original value} = 24$ muffins\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage change &= \\frac{\\mid 15 - 24\\begin{vmatrix}}{24} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid - 9\\end{vmatrix}}{24} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{24} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.375 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 37.5%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage decrease is $37.5\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228887 1069 "e-21456" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228887 1071 "Jan is completing a research project. She initially estimated it would take her $27$ weeks to complete the project. However, it took her only $20$ weeks. What is the percentage error in her original estimate?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228887 1072 #uuid "a8b1d5b1-8e9f-513a-85f6-17dc08e59344" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228887 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nWe have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{estimate} = 27$ weeks\n- $\\textrm{exact value} = 20$ weeks\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage error &= \\frac{\\mid 27 - 20\\begin{vmatrix}}{20} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 7\\end{vmatrix}}{20} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{20} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.35 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 35%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage error is $35\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228888 1069 "e-21458" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228888 1071 "A service adjusted a subscription fee to $90\\%$ of its original cost. A surcharge of $18\\%$ was applied to the adjusted fee. If the subscription originally cost $\\[math]180$, what is the surcharge?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228888 1072 #uuid "97d0ab51-97cd-5781-be30-4d70adc5d214" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228888 1074 "First, the adjusted fee is $90\\%$ of the original cost $(\\[math]180){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90% \\cdot 180 &= \\frac{90}{100} \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 0.9 \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 162\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the adjusted fee is $\\[/math]162$. Then, the surcharge is $18\\%$ of this fee:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18% \\cdot 162 &= \\frac{18}{100} \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 0.18 \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 29.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surcharge is $\\[math]29.16$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228889 1069 "e-21461" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228889 1071 "A positive number is quadruple another positive number. What is the percentage increase from the smaller to the larger number?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228889 1072 #uuid "6af0e15e-d996-5861-95a5-604ea914c42f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228889 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original value. Then, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = 4x$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = x$\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage change &= \\frac{\\mid 4x - x\\begin{vmatrix}}{x} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 3x\\end{vmatrix}}{x} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3x}{x} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3x}{x} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 300%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smaller number is increased by $300\\%$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228890 1069 "e-21463" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228890 1071 "Find $160\\%$ of $75$, and then $150\\%$ of the result." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228890 1072 #uuid "91ca6940-1bc7-583b-8bac-81d7da934167" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228890 1074 "First, we find $160\\%$ of $75{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n160% \\cdot 75 &= \\frac{160}{100} \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 1.6 \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $150\\%$ of the result $(120){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n150% \\cdot 120 &= \\frac{150}{100} \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 1.5 \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 180\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $180$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228891 1069 "e-21464" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228891 1071 "A farmer counted crates of harvested produce. In week $2$, there were $1{,}044$ crates. The percent increase from week $1$ to week $2$ was $45\\%$. How many crates were there in week $1$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228891 1072 #uuid "ace2e4e1-2f38-5218-9277-7f68c2bf996e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228891 1074 "Increasing a number by $45\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 45\\% = 145\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n145\\% = \\dfrac{145}{100} = 1.45\n$$\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of crates in week $1$. So, after a $45\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.45x\n$$\n\nWe are told the increased value is $1{,}044$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.45x &= 1,044 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1,044}{1.45} \\\\\nx &= 720\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there were $720$ crates in week $1$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228892 1069 "e-21466" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228892 1071 "After increasing a number $x$ by $1{,}900\\%$, the result is $740$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228892 1072 #uuid "344de810-d399-57e5-9436-2c7fe0d8b88e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228892 1074 "Increasing a number by $1{,}900\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 1{,}900\\% = 2{,}000\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n2{,}000\\% = \\dfrac{2{,}000}{100} = 20\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $740$, we have the equation\n$20x = 740$.\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{740}{20} \\\\\n&= 37\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $x$ is $37$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228893 1069 "e-21467" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228893 1071 "A collector sold part of a marble collection. After $45\\%$ of the original marbles were sold, $484$ marbles remained. How many marbles were sold?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228893 1072 #uuid "2085f7c8-836a-59d6-a84c-0370c03e11df" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228893 1074 "Decreasing a number by $45\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 45\\% = 55\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n55\\% = \\dfrac{55}{100} = 0.55\n$$\n\nLet the original number of marbles be $x$. So, after a $45\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.55x\n$$\n\nWe are told that $484$ marbles remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.55x = 484\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.55x &= 484 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{484}{0.55} \\\\\nx &= 880\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of marbles was $880$, we find the number removed by taking $45\\%$ of $880{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{45}{100} \\cdot 880 = 396\n$$\n\nSo, $396$ marbles were sold." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228894 1069 "e-21485" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228894 1071 "What number is $30\\%$ less than $120$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228894 1072 #uuid "9701b962-e3cf-5374-8168-ac5cb575cc3e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228894 1074 "To find a number $30\\%$ *less than* $120$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 30\\% = 70\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70\\% = \\dfrac{70}{100} = 0.7\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $70\\%$ of $120$, we multiply $120$ by $0.7{:}$\n\n$$\n0.7 \\cdot 120 = 84\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228895 1069 "e-21520" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228895 1071 "After increasing a quantity $x$ by $20\\%$, the result is $102$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228895 1072 #uuid "98fb5204-91c4-5971-814d-c7f14b80f1df" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228895 1074 "Increasing a number by $20\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 20\\% = 120\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n120\\% = \\dfrac{120}{100} = 1.20\n$$\n\nWe're told that by increasing a number $x$ by $20\\%$, we get the number $102$. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n1.20x = 102\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{102}{1.20} \\\\\n&= 85\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x= 85$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228896 1069 "e-21521" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228896 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $16\\%$, the result is $84$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228896 1072 #uuid "1037c9ed-3f3c-5b13-9bba-a07a96f2add7" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228896 1074 "Decreasing a number by $16\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 16\\% = 84\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n84\\% = \\dfrac{84}{100} = 0.84\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $16\\%$, we get the number $84$. So, we have the equation\n$0.84x = 84$.\n\nNow, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{84}{0.84} \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x = 100$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228897 1069 "e-21522" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228897 1071 "After a $15\\%$ discount, the price of a smartphone is $\\[math]425$. What was the original price?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228897 1072 #uuid "92b898c9-1f62-5aa3-825f-cde57c88ccb8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228897 1074 "Decreasing a number by $15\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 15\\% = 85\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85\\% = \\dfrac{85}{100} = 0.85\n$$\n\nLet the smartphone's original price be $x$. So, after a $15\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n$0.85x$.\n\nWe are told the discounted price is $\\[math]425$. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x = 425\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.85x &= 425 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{425}{0.85} \\\\\nx &= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original price was $\\[/math]500$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228898 1069 "e-21611" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228898 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y' + y = -10$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228898 1072 #uuid "cd54adb4-ef2b-5f9d-b920-4ec460bcd199" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228898 1074 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny'_c + y_c = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -y_c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y_c} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y_c} {\\textrm{d}x} = -1\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{y_{c}}\\frac{dy_{c}}{dx}dx &= \\int - 1dx \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{y_{c}}dy_{c} &= \\int - 1dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y_{c} \\mid &= -x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid y_{c} \\mid &= e^{-x + C} \\\\\n&= e^{-x}e^{C} \\\\\n&= Ke^{-x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K= e^{C}$.\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A = \\pm K$. Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228899 1069 "e-21612" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228899 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + y = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3$.\nFind the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228899 1072 #uuid "e0e14ce3-61de-571e-b34e-f56630cc2fe9" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228899 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{α = 1\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx^{2}) \\\\ 2α + β = 2\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx); β + γ =-3 \\mid (equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228900 1069 "e-21613" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228900 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = -2x + 5$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228900 1072 #uuid "344fb3a9-81db-5744-822c-1f989c0cc4de" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228900 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{2α =-2\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx) \\\\ α + 2β = 5\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-2x} - x + 3\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228901 1069 "e-21614" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228901 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 4y = -8x + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y' + 4y = -8x + 6$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-4x}$, solve the initial value problem." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228901 1072 #uuid "f89b7459-5b3d-5da3-a76a-7df3378cb048" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228901 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1$. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{4α =-8\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx) \\\\ α + 4β = 6\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAe^{-4(0)} - 2(0) + 2 &= 1 \\\\\nA + 2 &= 1 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228902 1069 "e-21615" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228902 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) = A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228902 1072 #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228902 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}$, and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(αe^{x} + 3βe^{3x}) - 2(αe^{x} + βe^{3x}) &= e^{x} - 2e^{3x} \\\\\n(α - 2α)e^{x} + (3β - 2β)e^{3x} &= e^{x} - 2e^{3x} \\\\\n- αe^{x} + βe^{3x} &= e^{x} - 2e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =-e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228903 1069 "e-21616" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228903 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228903 1072 #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228903 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x}$, and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e ^ {6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= (αxe^{6x})^{′} \\\\\n&= α(xe^{6x})^{′} \\\\\n&= α(e^{6x} + 6xe^{6x}) \\\\\n&= α(1 + 6x)e^{6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(α(1 + 6x)e^{6x}) - 6αxe^{6x} &= 9e^{6x} \\\\\n(1 + 6x - 6x)αe^{6x} &= 9e^{6x} \\\\\nαe^{6x} &= 9e^{6x} \\\\\nα &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x e ^ {6 x}\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228904 1069 "e-21617" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228904 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y' - 2y = 4e^{3x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x}$, solve the initial value problem." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228904 1072 #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228904 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3αe^{3x}) - 2(αe^{3x}) &= 4e^{3x} \\\\\n(3 - 2)αe^{3x} &= 4e^{3x} \\\\\nαe^{3x} &= 4e^{3x} \\\\\nα &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + 4 &= 1 \\\\\nA &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228905 1069 "e-21618" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228905 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} + 3 y = 2\\cos{x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) = A e ^ {-3x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228905 1072 #uuid "90e0e1d2-ca8a-5919-9891-599af549f2d5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228905 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\cos{x}$. So, we assume the particular solution to be\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =-\\alpha \\sin {x} + \\beta \\cos {x}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\sin {x} + \\beta \\cos {x}) + 3(\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x) = 2\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha+\\beta)\\cos x + (-\\alpha + 3\\beta) \\sin x = 2\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{3α + β = 2\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos x) \\\\ - α + 3β = 0\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin x)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\dfrac 3 5\n$$\n\n$$\n\\beta = \\dfrac 1 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\cos(x) + \\dfrac{1}{5} \\sin(x)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228906 1069 "e-21619" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228906 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2 \\sin(3x)$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228906 1072 #uuid "5769fd27-5fa4-5c85-b927-c87bedca3ea3" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228906 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x)$. So, we assume the particular solution to be\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta \\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =-3\\alpha \\sin(3x) + 3\\beta \\cos(3x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 (-3\\alpha \\sin(3x) + 3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{6β + 4α = 8\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (3x)) \\\\ -6α + 4β =-2\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (3x))\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13}}\\right)\\cos(3x) + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13}}\\right) \\sin(3x)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228907 1069 "e-21620" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228907 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 3y = 26 \\sin(2x), \\qquad y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y' + 3y = 26 \\sin(2x)$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-3x}$, solve the initial value problem." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228907 1072 #uuid "3b584b5f-8398-5f18-923d-47a3fb09adca" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228907 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 3y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $26 \\sin(2x)$. So, we assume the particular solution to be\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(2x) + \\beta \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' =-2\\alpha \\sin(2x) + 2\\beta \\cos(2x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\sin(2x) + 2\\beta \\cos(2x)) + 3 (\\alpha \\cos(2x)+ \\beta \\sin(2x)) = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta+3\\alpha)\\cos(2x) + (-2\\alpha +3 \\beta) \\sin(2x) = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{2β + 3α = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (2x)) \\\\ -2α + 3β = 26\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (2x))\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-4, \\beta=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =-4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAe^{-3 \\cdot 0} - 4\\cos (2 \\cdot 0) + 6\\sin (2 \\cdot 0) &= 1 \\\\\nA - 4 + 0 &= 1 \\\\\nA &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 5e^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228908 1069 "e-21621" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228908 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$y' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x}$,\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) = Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228908 1072 #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228908 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x}$, and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= (αxe^{-5x} + βe^{x})^{′} \\\\\n&= α(xe^{-5x})^{′} + β(e^{x})^{′} \\\\\n&= α(e^{-5x} - 5xe^{-5x}) + βe^{x} \\\\\n&= α(1 - 5x)e^{-5x} + βe^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(α(1 - 5x)e^{-5x} + βe^{x}) + 5(αxe^{-5x} + βe^{x}) &= 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x} \\\\\n(1 - 5x + 5x)αe^{-5x} + (β + 5β)e^{x} &= 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x} \\\\\nαe^{-5x} + 6βe^{x} &= 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228909 1069 "e-21636" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228909 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), \\, y(0) = 8, \\ x \\geq 0, \\, \\lambda = 6$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. The update formula can be written as $y_{n+1} = \\left(1 - 6\\Delta x\\right)^n y_n$.\n2. The method is stable for $\\mid 1 - 6\\Delta x \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.1$.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228909 1072 #uuid "bf22c16e-fc49-5ba5-9c64-89ee66d0a6e7" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228909 1074 "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 8, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -6y, \\qquad y(0) = 8\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nEuler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n,y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n$y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + (-6y_{n}) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 6y_{n} \\cdot Δx; = (1 - 6Δx)y_{n}$.\nHence, statement I is false.\n- For stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Here, we have\n$y_{n}|= (1 - 6Δx)y_{n - 1}; = (1 - 6Δx)((1 - 6Δx)y_{n - 2}); = (1 - 6Δx)^{2}y_{n - 2}; ⋮⋮; = (1 - 6Δx)^{n}y_{0}; = 8(1 - 6Δx)^{n}$.\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1$, as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n- Expanding the inequality $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & < 1 - 6Δx < 1 \\\\ -2 & < - 6Δx < 0 \\\\ 0 & < 6Δx < 2 \\\\ 0 & < Δx < \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:Since $\\Delta x = 0.1$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.Since $\\Delta x = 0.2$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228910 1069 "e-21637" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228910 1071 "For the initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0$, the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$Δy_{n}^{(p)}|= f(x_{n}, y_{n}) \\cdot Δx,; y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x_{n}, y_{n}) + f(x_{n + 1}, y_{n} + Δy_{n}^{(p)})) \\cdot Δx$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation $y'=-\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 12$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2$.\n2. The stability region is $\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac16\\right)^2\\right| < 1$.\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228910 1072 #uuid "6d580b90-1fbf-541c-8e63-97bb40ce0f3f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228910 1074 "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -12y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n$y_{n} + Δy_{n}^{(p)}|= y_{n} + f(x_{n}, y_{n}) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx$\nThus,\n$y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x_{n}, y_{n}) + f(x_{n + 1}, y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx)) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(-12y_{n} - 12(y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx)) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(-24y_{n} + 144y_{n} \\cdot Δx) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx + 72y_{n} \\cdot (Δx)^{2}; = [1 - 12Δx + 72(Δx)^{2}]y_{n}$.\nSo, the stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2$. Hence, statement I is true.\n- The method is stable when\n$\\mid R(12Δx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - 12Δx + 72(Δx)^{2} \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid \\mid < 1$.\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac1{12}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac12\\right| < 1$.\nHence, statement II is false.\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} & < 1 \\\\ 72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} & < \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} & < \\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{12} < Δx - \\frac{1}{12} & < \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ 0 < Δx & < \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15$, and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228911 1069 "e-21638" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228911 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n2. $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n3. $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n4. $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228911 1072 #uuid "0575b9de-7811-547e-90f4-e7a431dac733" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228911 1074 "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\mid R(λΔx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - λΔx \\mid \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n$0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$.\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$.\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda=1$, this region simplifies to\n$0 \\mid < Δx < 2$.\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$ satisfies the inequality, and\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler's method is stable for all given step sizes.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\"." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228912 1069 "e-21639" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228912 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n2. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n3. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n4. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n$R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228912 1072 #uuid "e522ef7a-a93a-5711-9193-6afb8faa4327" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228912 1074 "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(0.6) & |= & 1 - 0.6 + ((0.6)^{2})/(2) - ((0.6)^{3})/(6) + ((0.6)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{2747}{5000} \\mid; = 0.5494 < 1.✓$\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(1.4) & |= & 1 - 1.4 + ((1.4)^{2})/(2) - ((1.4)^{3})/(6) + ((1.4)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{4241}{15000} \\mid; = 0.282… < 1.✓$\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(2.8) & |= & 1 - 2.8 + ((2.8)^{2})/(2) - ((2.8)^{3})/(6) + ((2.8)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{639}{625} \\mid; = 1.0224 > 1.\\cdot$\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(3.3) & |= & 1 - 3.3 + ((3.3)^{2})/(2) - ((3.3)^{3})/(6) + ((3.3)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{167747}{80000} \\mid; = 2.096… > 1.\\cdot$\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$, so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228913 1069 "e-21649" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228913 1071 "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x$, calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228913 1072 #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228913 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t)$, where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\cos t + 2x)^{2}] &= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\cos t + 2x) \\\\\n&= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot (-\\sin t) \\\\\n&=-2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ^{′}(t) &= \\int_{0}^{1} - 2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin tdx \\\\\n&=-2\\sin t\\int_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx &= \\cos t \\cdot \\left.x\\right|_{0}^{1} + \\left.x^{2}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\cos t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228914 1069 "e-21677" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228914 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228914 1072 #uuid "d77c4a1b-863b-57ad-be77-44f2d802f554" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228914 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((2 - xy^{2}) + (2 - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2})) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - xy^{2} - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5 \\cdot 2(y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 1 - 2 + 0.5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5} {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= 1 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 1.166667 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 1.158333 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 1.158312\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228915 1069 "e-21679" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228915 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228915 1072 #uuid "9148676f-5899-539c-9b95-4a0d8d225056" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228915 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2y^{2} - \\sqrt{x} + 2(y_{new})^{2} - \\sqrt{x_{new}}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - 1 - 1^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228916 1069 "e-21707" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228916 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-7x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-7y(t)$\nFind the general solution to the system of ODEs above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228916 1072 #uuid "26df399d-cd87-5b5d-9f3f-7ced4ca5fd06" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228916 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= Φ_{0}(t)c \\\\\n&= e^{At}c \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}te^{-7t} \\\\ e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{-7t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-7t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228917 1069 "e-21711" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228917 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\qquad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n$.\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,\\mathbf{c}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At}\\,\\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $(e^{A t_0})^{-1} \\mathbf{x}(t_0)=(e^{At_0})^{-1} e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{c} = e^{-A t_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} e^{-At_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At - At_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{A(t-t_0)} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n\nFill in the blanks to justify each step of the following reasoning.\n\n$\\qquad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from $\\textrm{L4}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\qquad$ The property of the matrix exponential used in $\\textrm{L8}$ is $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\qquad$ Line $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228917 1072 #uuid "ace067cd-e1d0-5b3e-bae2-bf58dcac3585" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228917 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L4}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. Starting from $\\textrm{L4}$,\n$\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$,\nwe multiply both sides on the left by $(e^{At_0})^{-1}$ to get\n$(e^{A t_0})^{-1}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=(e^{At_0})^{-1}e^{A t_0}\\,\\mathbf{c}$,\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L5}$. Therefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from $\\textrm{L4}$ by $(e^{At_{0}})^{-1}$\n- Next, we justify the step $\\textrm{L7}$ to $\\textrm{L8}$. Since $At$ and $-At_0$ commute, we may combine exponentials:\n$e^{At}e^{-At_0}=e^{At-At_0}$.\nSubstituting this into line $\\textrm{L7}$ produces line $\\textrm{L8}$. Hence, the property of the matrix exponential used in $\\textrm{L8}$ is $X$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L8}$ and $\\textrm{L9}$. Starting from $\\textrm{L8}$,\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At-At_0}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$,\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-At_0=A(t-t_0)$ to get\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$,\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L9}$. Thus, $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt lightgray]{At-At_0=A(t-t_0)\\text{for any}t\\text{and}t_0}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228918 1069 "e-21716" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228918 1071 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n1. $yy' = x^2y + e^x$\n2. $y = x\\ln(1+y') + (y')^2$\n3. $y + \\dfrac{x}{1+y'} - y' = 0$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228918 1072 #uuid "e9cb7978-20da-52f5-be9a-4ce65484a5e1" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228918 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nNote that, in the special case where\n\n$$\nf(y') = y'\n$$\n\nthe equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y$, we get\n$y = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2}$,\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$.\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2$.\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'}$,\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228919 1069 "e-21717" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228919 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)$.\n\nIf $y'(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n$y(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228919 1072 #uuid "81f19562-1aee-5d9c-955c-4de09467906f" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228919 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t$, our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= e^{\\intP(t)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int2/(t - 1)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln \\mid t - 1 \\mid} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= (t - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= (t - 1)^{2}(1 + t)/(t(1 - t)) \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2}) &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t} \\\\\n\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2})dt &= \\int\\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}dt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= \\int\\frac{1}{t} - tdt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= \\ln \\mid t\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + C \\\\ x|= (2\\ln \\mid t\\end{vmatrix}- t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= ((2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2}))(t - 1)^{2} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228920 1069 "e-21720" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228920 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228920 1072 #uuid "3638fd11-5f61-5656-89c4-18ee0a4318ac" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228920 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t = t_0 = 0$, we have\n$e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(0)|= e^{At}x(0); = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{3(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{3(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we have\n$e^{A(t - s)}g(s)|= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{3(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{3(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3(t - s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3s + 3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThus, we obtain\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3s + 3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}ds; = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}e^{-3s + 3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_{s = 0}^{s = t}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228921 1069 "e-21721" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228921 1071 "Consider the inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\displaystyle \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\displaystyle \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\displaystyle \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\displaystyle \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228921 1072 #uuid "c24150ed-748b-5069-9fc5-48d4e6deaef2" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228921 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L2}$ and $\\textrm{L3}$. Starting from\n$\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$\nin $\\textrm{L2}$ and substituting $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$ from the original system gives\n$y^{′}(t)|=-Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}(Ax(t) + g(t)); =-Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}g(t); = e^{-A(t - t_{0})}g(t)$.\nThus, $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + g(t)$\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. Evaluating $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$ at $t=t_0$ gives\n$\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=I\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $t = t_{0}$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L8}$. Multiplying both sides of\n$\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$\nfrom $\\textrm{L1}$ by $e^{A(t-t_0)}$ gives\n$e^{A(t - t_{0})}y(t)|= e^{A(t - t_{0})}e^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t); = x(t)$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from $\\textrm{L1}$ by $e^{A(t - t_{0})}$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228922 1069 "e-21722" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228922 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nFill in the blanks in the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} & te^{4t} \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$*\n\n$x(t)|= \\begin{bmatrix}X X X X \\\\ 3e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix} + \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ X X\\end{bmatrix}ds$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228922 1072 #uuid "e151cd67-cf96-5378-9380-e9960c1eb6b4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228922 1074 "Given the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} & te^{4t} \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ So,\n$e^{A(t - 1)}x(1)|= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{4(t - 1)} & (t - 1)e^{4(t - 1)} \\\\ 0 & e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3(t - 1)e^{4(t - 1)} \\\\ 3e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{4(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{4(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we have\n$e^{A(t - s)}g(s)|= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{4(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{4(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{4(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 (t - 1) e 4 (t - 1) \\\\ 3e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix} + \\int_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}ds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228923 1069 "e-21723" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228923 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 7y(t); y^{′}(t) = 7x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7t) & \\sinh (7t) \\\\ \\sinh (7t) & \\cosh (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228923 1072 #uuid "b061f2b1-b015-582f-921f-9b444db631ee" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228923 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\mathbf{c} =e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t = t_0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nand since $e^{At}$ is invertible for all $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $\\mathbf{x}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0}) \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{A(t - 1)}x(1) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7(t - 1)) & \\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ \\sinh (7(t - 1)) & \\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ 3\\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228924 1069 "e-21725" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228924 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 3y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228924 1072 #uuid "ead811e0-8551-5b15-8b49-711cbe0b8498" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228924 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{x}(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}x(0) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (3t) - \\sin (3t) \\\\ -2\\sin (3t) - \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1069 "e-21729" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1071 "A supply crate of mass $24\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhat differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1072 #uuid "9959f237-b247-5fda-9912-afe816986904" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$\n$F = ma$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the crate:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the crate, which equals\n$w|= mg; = 24g$.\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D$, which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the crate (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n$D = -kv^2 = -\\dfrac{4}{5}v^2$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity:\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the crate, we have\n\n$$\n24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2 =24\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g - \\dfrac{1}{30}v^2\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228926 1069 "e-21730" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228926 1071 "A concrete cylinder of mass $50\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released vertically downward with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. Let $v(t)$ be the cylinder's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the cylinder is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=4.9\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the cylinder are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228926 1072 #uuid "55e88cab-9a3b-577b-b4ff-e61e892d201c" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228926 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the package: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -4.9v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =50g - 4.9v^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n50g - 4.9v^{2} &= 50\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g - 0.098v^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g(1 - 0.01v^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 - 0.01v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} &= g \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{1 - 0.01v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}dt &= g\\intdt \\\\\n\\int(1)/(1 - (0.1v)^{2})dv &= g\\intdt \\\\\n\\frac{1}{0.1}artanh(0.1v) &= gt + C_{1} \\\\\nartanh(0.1v) &= \\frac{1}{10}gt + C \\\\\n0.1v &= \\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}gt + C) \\\\\nv(t) &= 10\\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}gt + C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.1C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the package is released with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 9$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= 10\\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}g \\cdot 0 + C) \\\\\n9 &= 10\\tanh (C) \\\\\n\\tanh (C) &= 0.9 \\\\\nC &= artanh(0.9) \\approx 1.472\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = 10\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{10}g t+1.472\\right)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228927 1069 "e-21731" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228927 1071 "A body of mass $80\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=1\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228927 1072 #uuid "1e4dc2a1-9425-5fab-8181-ff4cffa9dd85" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228927 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma$, the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 80 \\cdot 9.8 = 784\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =784 - v^2\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$, the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n784 - (v_\\text{term})^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v_\\text{term} = \\sqrt{784} =28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is\n\n$$\nv_\\text{term} =28\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228928 1069 "e-21732" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228928 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n2. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n3. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228928 1072 #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228928 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have\n$f_1(x,t) = t\\cos x$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_1$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t$.\n- For the second function, we have\n$f_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1)$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t$, since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R$, and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n- For the third function, we have\n$f_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1]$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_3$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228929 1069 "e-21740" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228929 1071 "An air mattress of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the mattress's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the air mattress is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the air mattress’s velocity is $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$.\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the air mattress are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228929 1072 #uuid "33225154-98de-5f11-9654-21283dc5e406" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228929 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the air mattress: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n$w = mg = 5g$\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n$D=-kv^2$,\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the air mattress is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 5g - kv^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n5g - kv^{2} &= 5\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g - \\frac{k}{5}v^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the air mattress's velocity is $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Substituting these values and $g = 9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n7.35 = g - \\dfrac{k}{5} \\cdot (0.5)^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = 49\\,\\textrm{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g - 9.8v^2\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the air mattress is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 0$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= \\tanh (g \\cdot 0 + C) \\\\\n0 &= \\tanh (C) \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\tanh\\left(gt\\right)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228930 1069 "e-21748" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228930 1071 "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(6.3\\times10^6)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 9.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Which differential equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet, where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object? Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228930 1072 #uuid "21311084-5c4f-54e2-9c03-75ade3828140" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228930 1074 "According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is\n\n$$\nF=-F_\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= -F_{in} \\\\\nma &= -G\\frac{mM}{r^{2}} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{GM}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0$.\n\nWhen at the surface,\n\n$$\nr=(6.3\\times10^6)\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nthe acceleration of the object due to gravity is\n\n$$\na=-9.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-9.7 &= - (k)/((6.3 \\cdot 10^{6})^{2}) \\\\\nk &= 9.7 \\cdot (6.3 \\cdot 10^{6})^{2} \\\\\n&= 9.7 \\cdot (6.3)^{2} \\cdot (10^{6})^{2} \\\\\n&= 384.993 \\cdot 10^{12} \\\\\n&= 3.84993 \\cdot 10^{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{3.84993\\times10^{14}}{r^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v$, which is the derivative of distance $r$, we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\\n&= v\\frac{dv}{dr}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{3.84993\\times10^{14}}{r^2}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228931 1069 "e-21749" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228931 1071 "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(8\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 16\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(9\\times10^{4})\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228931 1072 #uuid "e71f19d1-391d-5c39-8fd6-c7adb8dd8a70" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228931 1074 "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is\n\n$$\nF=-F_\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= -F_{in} \\\\\nma &= -G\\frac{mM}{r^{2}} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{GM}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0$.\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R$, the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g$. Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n-g = -\\dfrac{k}{R^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = gR^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v$, which is the derivative of distance $r$, we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\\n&= v\\frac{dv}{dr}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intv\\frac{dv}{dr}dr &= \\int(-\\frac{gR^{2}}{r^{2}})dr \\\\\n\\intvdv &= -gR^{2}\\int\\frac{1}{r^{2}}dr \\\\\n\\frac{v^{2}}{2} &= -gR^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r}) + C_{1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{r} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0$, we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(R)^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{R} + C \\\\\nv_{0}^{2} &= 2gR + C \\\\\nC &= v_{0}^{2} - 2gR\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\nv^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n\n$$\nR=(8\\times10^5)\n$$\n\n$g=16$, and\n\n$$\nv_0=(9\\times10^{4})\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^{2} &= (2 \\cdot 16 \\cdot (8 \\cdot 10^{5})^{2})/(r) + (9 \\cdot 10^{4})^{2} - 2 \\cdot 16 \\cdot (8 \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2048 \\cdot 10^{10}}{r} + (81 \\cdot 10^{8}) - (256 \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.048 \\cdot 10^{13}}{r} + (8.1 \\cdot 10^{9}) - (0.0256 \\cdot 10^{9}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.048 \\cdot 10^{13}}{r} + ((8.1 - 0.0256) \\cdot 10^{9}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.048 \\cdot 10^{13}}{r} + (8.0744 \\cdot 10^{9})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228932 1069 "e-21750" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228932 1071 "Consider a planet with radius $(1.2\\times10^{8})\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 18.9\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object,\n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228932 1072 #uuid "95939a44-8f36-59a8-80a8-1fd81d5f89a4" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228932 1074 "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is\n\n$$\nF=-F_\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= -F_{in} \\\\\nma &= -G\\frac{mM}{r^{2}} \\\\\na &= -\\frac{GM}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0$.\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R$, the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g$. Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n-g = -\\dfrac{k}{R^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = gR^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v$, which is the derivative of distance $r$, we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\\n&= v\\frac{dv}{dr}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intv\\frac{dv}{dr}dr &= \\int(-\\frac{gR^{2}}{r^{2}})dr \\\\\n\\intvdv &= -gR^{2}\\int\\frac{1}{r^{2}}dr \\\\\n\\frac{v^{2}}{2} &= -gR^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r}) + C_{1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{r} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0$, we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(R)^{2} &= \\frac{2gR^{2}}{R} + C \\\\\nv_{0}^{2} &= 2gR + C \\\\\nC &= v_{0}^{2} - 2gR\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\nv^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r \\to \\infty$, the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2gR^2}{r}\n$$\n\napproaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}v_{0}^{2} - 2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ v_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ v_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\nv_e = \\sqrt{2gR}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n\n$$\nR=(1.2\\times10^{8})\n$$\n\nand $g=18.9$, we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{e} &= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot (1.2 \\cdot 10^{8}) \\cdot 18.9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2 \\cdot 1.2 \\cdot 18.9) \\cdot 10^{8}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{45.36} \\cdot \\sqrt{10^{8}} \\\\\n&\\approx (6.73 \\cdot 10^{4})m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228933 1069 "e-21759" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228933 1071 "![](images/7e/7e54c9613c18a0ea26df6a4088598b566e532fba555dc08db0bcfd539b4c64d5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228933 1072 #uuid "353e21f2-2d1d-55e3-ac86-19932d117997" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228933 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $80\\,\\Omega$, and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(3t) = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin(3t)\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228934 1069 "e-21762" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228934 1071 "![](images/42/424cb52325c95f03708ed9ae5fe1d41e7b0d1c8bb8878e1dd8dc002aef214481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228934 1072 #uuid "28fd19b3-762a-572e-9b52-27e17093ad93" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228934 1074 "The current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega$.So,\n$V_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.00125} = 800q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24 = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 800q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac3{10}\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-10t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha$.\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n$0 + 10\\alpha = \\dfrac3{10} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\alpha = \\dfrac3{100}$\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac3{100}$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-10t} + \\dfrac3{100}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\non the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition\n\n$$\nq(0) = \\dfrac{1}{50}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(0) &= \\frac{1}{50} \\\\\nAe^{-10(0)} + \\frac{3}{100} &= \\frac{1}{50} \\\\\nA &= -\\frac{1}{100}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(t) &= -\\frac{1}{100}e^{-10t} + \\frac{3}{100} \\\\\n&= 1 100 (3 - e - 10 t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = -\\dfrac1{100}e^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac3{100}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty$, the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228935 1069 "e-21763" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228935 1071 "![](images/96/962694e1706c47a4b060da6636e0b6e2f1653cb94137dca8dc59781cf74bd252.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228935 1072 #uuid "a3e7a060-f36d-5fd6-a6f6-5b321261fb0d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228935 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, and a capacitor with capacitance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}\n$$\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t)\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t)$.\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute\n$q_{p}|= α\\cos (6t) + β\\sin (6t),; \\frac{dq_{p}}{dt}|=-6α\\sin (6t) + 6β\\cos (6t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-6α\\sin (6t) + 6β\\cos (6t)) + 5(α\\cos (6t) + β\\sin (6t))|= 3\\cos (6t); (-6α + 5β)\\sin (6t) + (5α + 6β)\\cos (6t)|= 3\\cos (6t)$\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-6α + 5β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (6t)) \\\\ 5α + 6β = 3\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (6t))$\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228936 1069 "e-21765" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228936 1071 "A water-treatment holding tank initially holds $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved coagulant.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228936 1072 #uuid "2f5d8d59-2cc7-53bf-92ad-6062df331851" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228936 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t$, in minutes. Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$. This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0.05\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0.05\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= 1.5\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t$.\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}$.\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (20\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700 + 10t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= (20\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700 + 10t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{20Q}{700 + 10t}\\frac{kg}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2Q}{70 + t}\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 1.5 - \\frac{2Q}{70 + t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{2}{70 + t}Q = 1.5\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= e^{\\int2/(70 + t)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln \\mid 70 + t \\mid} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (70 + t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= (70 + t)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by\n\n$$\nI(t) = (70+t)^2\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(70 + t)^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{2}{70 + t}Q) &= (70 + t)^{2} \\cdot 1.5 \\\\\n(70 + t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt} + 2(70 + t)Q &= 1.5(70 + t)^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70 + t)^{2}) &= 1.5(70 + t)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70 + t)^{2})dt &= \\int1.5(70 + t)^{2}dt \\\\\nQ(70 + t)^{2} &= 1.5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70 + t)^{3} + C \\\\\nQ(t) &= 0.5(70 + t) + (C)/((70 + t)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(0) &= 12 \\\\\n0.5(70 + 0) + (C)/((70 + 0)^{2}) &= 12 \\\\\n35 + \\frac{C}{70^{2}} &= 12 \\\\\n\\frac{C}{70^{2}} &= -23 \\\\\nC &= -23(70)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(t) &= 0.5(70 + t) - (23(70)^{2})/((70 + t)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.5(70 + t) - 23(\\frac{70}{70 + t})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(8) &= 0.5(70 + 8) - 23(\\frac{70}{70 + 8})^{2} \\\\\n&= 39 - 23(\\frac{70}{78})^{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 20.48kg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228937 1069 "e-21766" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228937 1071 "A syrup mixing tank in a confectionery factory can hold up to $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of syrup solution with $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated syrup with concentration $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228937 1072 #uuid "bbf602f0-1101-55d5-a6b3-9bfc5731e9ec" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228937 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t$, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$, in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$. So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (1.5\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\cdot (24\\frac{gal}{min}) \\\\\n&= (1.5\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\cdot (24\\frac{gal}{min}) \\\\\n&= 36\\frac{lb}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t$.\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$.\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$, *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (16\\frac{gal}{min}) \\cdot ((Q)/(8(12 + t))\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\\\\n&= (16\\frac{gal}{min}) \\cdot ((Q)/(8(12 + t))\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\\\\n&= (16Q)/(8(12 + t))\\frac{lb}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2Q}{12 + t}\\frac{lb}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 36 - \\frac{2Q}{12 + t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= e^{\\int2/(12 + t)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln \\mid 12 + t \\mid} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (12 + t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= (12 + t)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by\n\n$$\nI(t) = (12+t)^2\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12 + t)^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{2}{12 + t}Q) &= (12 + t)^{2} \\cdot 36 \\\\\n(12 + t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt} + 2(12 + t)Q &= 36(12 + t)^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12 + t)^{2}) &= 36(12 + t)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12 + t)^{2})dt &= \\int36(12 + t)^{2}dt \\\\\nQ(12 + t)^{2} &= 36 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12 + t)^{3} + C \\\\\nQ(t) &= 12(12 + t) + (C)/((12 + t)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(0) &= 32 \\\\\n12(12 + 0) + (C)/((12 + 0)^{2}) &= 32 \\\\\n12 \\cdot 12 + (C)/((12)^{2}) &= 32 \\\\\n144 + \\frac{C}{12^{2}} &= 32 \\\\\n\\frac{C}{12^{2}} &= -112 \\\\\nC &= -112(12)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(t) &= 12(12 + t) - (112(12)^{2})/((12 + t)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 12(12 + t) - 112(\\frac{12}{12 + t})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$, so we can use the condition\n\n$$\nV(t_o) = 240\n$$\n\nto solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(t_{o}) &= 240 \\\\\n96 + 24t_{o} - 16t_{o} &= 240 \\\\\n96 + 8t_{o} &= 240 \\\\\n8t_{o} &= 144 \\\\\nt_{o} &= 18min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(18) &= 12(12 + 18) - 112(\\frac{12}{12 + 18})^{2} \\\\\n&= 360 - 112(\\frac{12}{30})^{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 342.1lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228938 1069 "e-21773" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228938 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228938 1072 #uuid "5aad284d-a5d9-5a90-817b-7786c2e5b062" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228938 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t)$. We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x$.\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t + 5t^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t) + \\frac{d}{dx}(5t^{2}) \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2x\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin t) + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t + 2x\\cos t\\frac{dt}{dx} + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt - 2\\sin t &= (2x\\cos t + 10t)\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{t - 2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t + 10t} &= \\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2x\\cos t + 10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2x\\cos t + 10t \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} - 2x\\cos t &= 10t \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} - \\frac{2\\cos t}{t - 2\\sin t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t - t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228939 1069 "e-21786" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228939 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n2. $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n3. $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228939 1072 #uuid "3c82270a-33f2-53ee-8876-9d8c7cf8291b" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228939 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y)$, which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n$D_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y$\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x)$, and $f_0(x) = 3$.\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n$D_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y$\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0$, and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1069 "e-21797" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0, \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1072 #uuid "b40412bb-d2d7-519e-aad6-60f53f4a1fcd" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad \\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$.\n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i=0$, $\\beta_i=1$, $a=1$, and $b=3$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}$. The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 2m - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1$, and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3(1)} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{3(3)} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{9} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (3e^{3})(-e^{-3}) - (-e^{-1})(3e^{9}) \\\\\n&=-3 + 3e^{8} \\\\\n&= 3 (e 8 - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228941 1069 "e-21798" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228941 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' + 16y = x^3 e^{x}, \\qquad y'(0) = y'(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\quad$ The absolute value of $\\det(A)$ is $AAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP $AAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP $AAAAAAAAAAAA$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228941 1072 #uuid "837c1385-88d1-5909-b79a-83df25a66838" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228941 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q$, and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$. Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y \\equiv 0$, then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting\n\n$$\n\\alpha_i = 0, \\beta_i = 1, a = 0\n$$\n\nand $b = \\pi$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\pm 4\\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = -4\\sin 4x, \\qquad y_2'(x) = 4\\cos 4x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4\\sin (4 \\cdot 0) & 4\\cos (4 \\cdot 0) \\\\ -4\\sin (4 \\cdot π) & 4\\cos (4 \\cdot π)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}det(A) & &= & 0 \\cdot 4 - 4 \\cdot 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 0\n$$\n\nthe corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions}$.\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions or no solution}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228942 1069 "e-21799" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228942 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228942 1072 #uuid "b6c06f26-9ff1-5e60-b0e3-5ee7482dff34" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228942 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n$y''(x) = 0$.\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n$y' = A$.\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4)\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(3) + y'(3) = 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228943 1069 "e-21801" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228943 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' + 16y = \\sin 4x, \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = -\\dfrac\\pi 8,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$\n\nThe solution is given by\n\n$y = f(x) + B \\cdot g(x)$\nwhere $B\\in \\mathbb R$ is arbitrary. Find the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228943 1072 #uuid "2e0819a0-8b9b-520c-880e-f2a802761a2e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228943 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q$, and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions.\n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution $y_h(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n- Then, we find a particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\pm 4\\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nand the general solution to the homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny_h = A\\cos 4x + B\\sin 4x\n$$\n\nNext, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = Cx\\cos 4x + Dx\\sin 4x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′}(x) &= C\\cos 4x - 4Cx\\sin 4x + D\\sin 4x + 4Dx\\cos 4x \\\\\ny_{p}^{″}(x) &= -8C\\sin 4x - 16Cx\\cos 4x + 8D\\cos 4x - 16Dx\\sin 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we reach (after some cancellation)\n\n$$\n-8C\\sin 4x + 8D\\cos 4x = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nC = -\\dfrac18\n$$\n\nand $D = 0$. Therefore, our general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos 4x + B\\sin 4x -\\dfrac18x\\cos4x\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\nA\\cos (4\\cdot 0) + B\\sin (4\\cdot 0) - \\dfrac18 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\cos (4\\cdot 0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0$. So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin 4x -\\dfrac18x\\cos 4x\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(\\pi) = -\\dfrac\\pi8\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nB\\sin (4π) - \\frac{π}{8}\\cos (4π) =-\\frac{π}{8}; 0 - \\frac{π}{8} =-\\frac{π}{8}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $B$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= -\\frac{1}{8}x\\cos 4x + B \\cdot \\sin 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is arbitrary.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac18 x\\cos 4x, \\qquad g(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228944 1069 "e-21802" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228944 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 9y = -9x - 3, \\qquad y(1) = \\dfrac{4}{3}, \\quad y(2) = \\dfrac{13}{3},\\quad 1\\leq x \\leq 2$\n\nThe solution is given by\n\n$y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-3x} + g(x)$.\nFind the constants $A$ and $B$, and the function $g(x)$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228944 1072 #uuid "a0d38dee-b1be-5d15-94ea-7a1f23d40f3e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228944 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q$, and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$.\n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution $y_h(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n- Then, we find a particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nThe homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny'' - 9y = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = 3$ and $m = -3$. So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{-3x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny_h = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-3x}\n$$\n\nNow, we seek a particular solution $y_p$ of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = Cx + D\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our inhomogeneous equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Cx + D)^{″} - 9(Cx + D) &= -9x - 3 \\\\\n- 9(Cx + D) &= -9x - 3 \\\\\n- 9Cx - 9D &= -9x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $x$ and constants, respectively, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-9C =-9 & ⇒C = 1 \\\\ -9D =-3 & ⇒D = \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = x + \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{h} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{3x} + Be^{-3x} + x + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(1) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nAe^3 + Be^{-3} +\\dfrac43 = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\qquad \\Rightarrow\\qquad B =-Ae^6\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{3x} - Ae^{-3x+6} + x +\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(2) = \\dfrac{13}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nAe^6 - Ae^{0} +2 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac{13}{3} \\qquad \\Rightarrow\\qquad Ae^6 - A = 2\n$$\n\nSolving for $A$, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2}{e^6-1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the first equation and solving for $B$, we have\n\n$$\nB =-Ae^6 = -\\dfrac{2e^6}{e^6-1}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the function $g(x)$, we have\n\n$$\ng(x) = y_p(x) = x + \\dfrac13\n$$\n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\ny'' - 9y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) = 0,\\quad 1\\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nhas only the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution. Since we have found a solution satisfying both boundary conditions, the solution to this inhomogeneous BVP is unique." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228945 1069 "e-21803" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228945 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228945 1072 #uuid "cf8f2367-68af-5ef4-ac2e-5a73e37695d9" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228945 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x) \\sim \\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\Big((\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx) + (\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx) \\Big)\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n- $-3a_n+4c_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n- $-3b_n+4d_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228946 1069 "e-21804" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228946 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$. Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228946 1072 #uuid "f43f5028-9314-5123-bbd8-85dc5c4a9b2b" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228946 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (2(-x)) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (-2x) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (2x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nwe have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0$. Thus, $a_n=0$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\ge1\n$$\n\nand $a_0=0$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228947 1069 "e-21805" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228947 1071 "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)$\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extesion of a function from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th sine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228947 1072 #uuid "848cc0af-b940-5ead-81ac-49cc6d2f4989" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228947 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- $a_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n- $a_n$ $n \\ge 1$, for the cosine coefficients, and\n- $-b_n$ $n \\ge 1$, for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th sine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is $-b_5$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228948 1069 "e-21841" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228948 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n\n${x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 5 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228948 1072 #uuid "e3a9105b-7f9c-59bc-b262-b664a2ec53d0" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228948 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty$\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1$, where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228949 1069 "e-21842" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228949 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$, where $f(t)$ is given by\n${e^{2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228949 1072 #uuid "38be687e-4fea-5e8a-a810-4c7f50667ea8" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228949 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = \\int_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function\n\n$$\n{e^{2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{2t} \\cdot e^{-st}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}1 \\cdot e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{2t - st}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{(2 - s)t}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if\n\n$$\ns \\leq 0\n$$\n\nFor $s > 0$, we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{(2 - s)t}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 - s}[e^{(2 - s)t}]_{0}^{3} + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{3}^{a}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + \\left.lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{-e^{-st}}{s})\\right|_{3}^{a} \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) - \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(e^{-as} - e^{-3s}) \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) - \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot (0 - e^{-3s}) \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228950 1069 "e-21843" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228950 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty)$.\n\n$f(x)|= {1,\\begin{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is even \\\\ -1,\\end{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is odd; g(x)|= u(x - 1) + u(x - \\frac{1}{2}) + u(x - \\frac{1}{3}) + ⋯; h(x)|= ⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉$\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228950 1072 #uuid "e5f66c99-fbc2-5d2b-ac0d-15d76748b58d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228950 1074 "Suppose $I\\subseteq \\mathbb R$. A function $f: I \\to \\mathbb R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\\subset I$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty)$. The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/3c/3ce7aa18de332c3eb1bc57b6bf7c50acf1573717d642c731a18a85f4f9fb813a.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty)$. Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1]$. Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13,\\dfrac14,\\ldots$, so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty)$. The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2,4,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/9a/9a5cc55dc36927437d16d09b4e491305efebac9d9a8995a741d62b6395a101bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228951 1069 "e-21853" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228951 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$x^2y'' + x(x-4)y' - 4y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n$y = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x)$,\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are arbitrary constants and $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution $y_1$, where $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0$.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228951 1072 #uuid "1e8187a1-aa1d-54ad-859e-b9b59d82af9b" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228951 1074 "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1$.\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0\n$$\n\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 5λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 1, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^4\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228952 1069 "e-21860" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228952 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n$y' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2$,\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228952 1072 #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228952 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′} - 3y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t) \\cdot \\sin(2t - π)} \\\\\nL{y^{′}} - 3L{y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t) \\cdot \\sin(2t - π)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term\n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi)\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - \\frac{π}{2}) \\cdot 2\\sin (2t - π)} &= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot L{2\\sin(2(t + \\frac{π}{2}) - π)} \\\\\n&= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot L{2\\sin (2t)} \\\\\n&= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′}} - 3L{y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t)\\sin (2t - π)} \\\\\n(sY(s) + 2) - 3Y(s) &= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s - 3)Y(s) + 2 &= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s - 3)Y(s) &= -2 + \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\nY(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s - 3} + (4e^{- πs/2})/((s^{2} + 4)(s - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228953 1069 "e-21861" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228953 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2$,\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n$Y(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228953 1072 #uuid "07f59403-66ac-531c-81d3-e286231595ff" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228953 1074 "To find the function $g(t)$, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s)$.\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2$, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B$, and $C$.\n\n- Setting $s=0$, we have\n$1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}$.\n- Setting $s=-4$, we have\n$1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}$.\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2$, we have\n$0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}$.\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}\n$$\n\nusing standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t) &= L^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{16s} + \\frac{1}{4s^{2}} + (1)/(16(s + 4))} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{t}{4} + \\frac{1}{16}e^{-4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$. Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t - 3)f(t - 3) &= u(t - 3)[- \\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{t - 3}{4} + \\frac{1}{16}e^{-4(t - 3)}] \\\\\n&= u(t - 3)(\\frac{1}{16}e^{-4(t - 3)} + \\frac{4t - 13}{16})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement\n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t)\n$$\n\nwe identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228954 1069 "e-21873" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228954 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$x^2y'' - 5xy' + (3x+9)y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation above is given by\n$y = c_1x^3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n + c_2y_2(x)$,\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are arbitrary constants and $a_n$ are constants.\nWhat is the form of the second Frobenius solution $y_2$, where $b_n$ for $n \\geq 0$ are the constant coefficients of the $n$th term determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 9 = 0$.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228954 1072 #uuid "8e142ab9-1bd4-5486-a7bf-fc27910ec91a" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228954 1074 "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\not\\in \\mathbb{Z}$, then\n$\\displaystyle y_2(x) = x^{\\lambda_2} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_2)x^n$.\n- **Case 2**: If $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2$, then\n$\\displaystyle y_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n$.\n- **Case 3**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\in \\mathbb{N}$, then\n$\\displaystyle y_2(x) = b_{-1}y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_2}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n$.\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 6λ + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $\\lambda=3$ is a double root. So, the indicial roots are equal\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2 = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nNow, since the roots are equal, the second solution is given by\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $b_n$ are found by substitution." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228955 1069 "e-21880" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228955 1071 "A fertilizer tank initially holds $800\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. Which expression gives the amount of nutrient $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228955 1072 #uuid "6b62d830-be21-5734-8a12-115b6a5136bf" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228955 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t$, in minutes. Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0.01\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (40\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0.01\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (40\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= 0.4\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\textrm{L}$. Then, since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{800}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (40\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= (40\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{40Q}{800}\\frac{kg}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{Q}{20}\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 0.4 - \\frac{Q}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q = 0.4\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $Q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$Q_c(t) = Ae^{-t/20}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.,\n$Q_p(t) = \\alpha$.\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $Q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n$0 + \\dfrac{1}{20}\\alpha = 0.4 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\alpha = 8$.\nTherefore, the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 8$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t) = Q_c(t) + Q_p(t) = Ae^{-t/20} + 8\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 7$, and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(0) &= 7 \\\\\nAe^{-0/20} + 8 &= 7 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 8 - e^{-t/20}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228956 1069 "e-21898" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228956 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$5xy'' + 2y' - 6y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n$y = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x)$,\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are arbitrary constants and $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the first solution $y_1$?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0$, and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n$a_n(\\lambda) = \\dfrac{6}{(\\lambda+n)(5(\\lambda+n)-3)}\\,a_{n-1}$.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228956 1072 #uuid "3a1106e0-40c6-5ffc-8bf0-c8e3509f6099" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228956 1074 "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1$.\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is\n\n$$\n5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5λ^{2} - 3λ &= 0 \\\\\nλ(5λ - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 0, \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\dfrac35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^{3/5}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nWith\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac35\n$$\n\nthe recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (6)/((\\frac{3}{5} + n)(5(\\frac{3}{5} + n) - 3))a_{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (6)/(5n(n + \\frac{3}{5}))a_{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (6)/(n(5n + 3))a_{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\narbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} \\mid \\ne 0, \\\\\na_{1} &= (6)/(1(5 \\cdot 1 + 3))a_{0} = \\frac{6}{8}a_{0} = \\frac{3}{4}a_{0}, \\\\\na_{2} &= (6)/(2(5 \\cdot 2 + 3))a_{1} = \\frac{6}{26} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4}a_{0} = \\frac{9}{52}a_{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1}(x) &= x^{3/5}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} \\\\\n&= x^{3/5}(a_{0} + \\frac{3}{4}a_{0}x + \\frac{9}{52}a_{0}x^{2} + ⋯) \\\\\n&= a_{0}(x^{3/5} + \\frac{3}{4}x^{8/5} + \\frac{9}{52}x^{13/5} + ⋯)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228957 1069 "e-6569" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228957 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)$?\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta$.*" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228957 1072 #uuid "04f55d6c-825e-587c-8ef4-a5527d1f374e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228957 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L$, we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(2y_{1} - y_{2}) &= 2L(y_{1}) - L(y_{2}) \\\\\n&= 2\\tan^{2} x - 2\\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwe can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(2y_{1} - y_{2}) &= 2\\tan^{2} x - 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228958 1069 "e-6572" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228958 1071 "Show that the differential operator\n\n$D(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$\n\nis nonlinear." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228958 1072 #uuid "d01adef6-d3fb-5cd6-b778-5d44fb6702dd" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228958 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L$, we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x)$, and where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(ay_{1} + by_{2}) &= (ay_{1} + by_{2})\\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}(ay_{1} + by_{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(ay_{1} + by_{2}) \\\\\n&= (ay_{1} + by_{2})(a\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}}) + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= + a^{2}y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + aby_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + aby_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b^{2}y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} \\\\\n&= + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= a^{2}y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b^{2}y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= + ab(y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\naD(y_{1}) + bD(y_{2}) &= a[y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy_{1}}{dx}] + b[y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy_{2}}{dx}] \\\\\n&= ay_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + by_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\nD(ay_1+by_2) \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2)\n$$\n\nthe operator $D$ is nonlinear." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228959 1069 "e-6617" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228959 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n$y''+y=\\tan{x}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|$" 131