\\mid \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= \\int(f(x)y_{1}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\tan x\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= \\int\\sin xdx \\\\\n&=-\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x - \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\tan x + \\sec x &) + (-\\cos x)(\\sin x) \\\\ =-\\cos x\\ln & \\tan x + \\sec x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= A\\cos x + B\\sin x- \\cos x\\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\left.\\sec x\\right|_(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228960 1069 "e-6618" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228960 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228960 1072 #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228960 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx, \\qquad u_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(xe^{x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(e^{x} + xe^{x}) - (e^{x})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= e^{2x} + xe^{2x} - xe^{2x} \\\\\n&= e^{2x} \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= -\\int\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot xe^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&=-18\\intx^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-6x^{3} \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= \\int\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot e^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&= 18\\intxdx \\\\\n&= 9x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (-6x^{3})e^{x} + (9x^{2})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&=-6x^{3}e^{x} + 9x^{3}e^{x} \\\\\n&= 3x^{3}e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x} + 3x^{3}e^{x}_(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228961 1069 "e-6641" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228961 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ for $f(t) = u(t - 2) + u(t-6)$, where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228961 1072 #uuid "f8786fd7-c9d5-5ada-9aa0-faa2a45f4cd9" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228961 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = \\int_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t \\ge 0\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t - 2) &= {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 2 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t \\ge 2 \\\\\nu(t - 6) &= {0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 6 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}t \\ge 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}t < 2 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le t < 6; 2, \\mid t \\ge 6\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}0 \\cdot e^{-st}dt + \\int_{2}^{6}1 \\cdot e^{-st}dt + \\int_{6}^{∞}2 \\cdot e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{6}e^{-st}dt + \\int_{6}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for\n\n$$\ns \\leq 0\n$$\n\nFor $s > 0$, we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{2}^{6}e^{-st}dt + \\int_{6}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{6}e^{-st}dt + 2lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{6}^{a}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{e^{-st}}{s}]_{2}^{6} + \\left.2lim_(a → ∞)(-\\frac{e^{-st}}{s})\\right|_{6}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{e^{-6s}}{s} + \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} - \\frac{2}{s} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(e^{-sa} - e^{-6s}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} - \\frac{e^{-6s}}{s} - \\frac{2}{s}(0 - e^{-6s}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} - \\frac{e^{-6s}}{s} + \\frac{2e^{-6s}}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} + \\frac{e^{-6s}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228962 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048536 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186228962 1037 #edb/ref 1759218605