 second. At this speed, how many seconds will it take the cyclist to travel $84$ meters?It will take the cyclist\n{{field-1}}\nseconds to travel $84$ meters." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229449 1072 #uuid "f5a6bd1e-4651-500e-94cd-3e3cfdef8f59" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227153 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229450 1069 "q-306369" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229450 1071 "A total of $34, 852$ students study at a certain university. The university conducted a survey among a randomly chosen sample of $1, 200$ students about their preference for morning or evening classes. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n|  | Morning | Evening | No Preference | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Freshmen and Sophomores | $280$ | $190$ | $30$ | $500$ |\n| Juniors and Seniors | $210$ | $150$ | $40$ | $400$ |\n| Graduates | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ | $300$ |\n| Total | $610$ | $440$ | $150$ | $1, 200$ |\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the difference between the percentage of undergraduates (freshmen, sophomores, juniors, and seniors combined) who preferred morning classes and the percentage of graduate students who preferred morning classes?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229450 1072 #uuid "6d9572a9-e4c8-5536-87ee-7babf0d3f7c5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227159 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229451 1069 "q-306525" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229451 1071 "The population density of Greenland, in people per square kilometer of land area, increased from $0.022$ in $1995$ to $0.026$ in $2020$. During this time period, the land area of Greenland was $2, 100, 000$ square kilometers.\n\nBy how many people did Greenland's population increase from $1995$ to $2020$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229451 1072 #uuid "4f3c0155-434b-5a3e-b8c7-07fda46e2851" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227165 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229452 1069 "q-306542" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229452 1071 "A planet has two satellites. The first satellite orbits the planet every $180$ days, and the second satellite orbits the planet every $205$ days. How many more days does it take the second satellite to orbit the planet $29$ times than it takes the first satellite to orbit the planet $29$ times?It takes the second satellite\n{{field-1}}\nmore days than the first satellite to orbit the planet $29$ times." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229452 1072 #uuid "d85ce0cf-b83f-5843-b530-41a9e4083b63" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227166 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229453 1069 "q-306621" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229453 1071 "![](images/be/be685437569ea719ce9d41e7e5b4b19bf664517a69a7b99f1938440ea8dcfcd4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. The median of data set $C$ is equal to the median of data set $D$.\n2. The standard deviation of data set $C$ is equal to the standard deviation of data set $D$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229453 1072 #uuid "fd1fa672-a4bc-55e0-a2f9-ab11128a0353" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227172 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229454 1069 "q-306841" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229454 1071 "A transportation researcher recorded the commuting distance of a random sample of employees at a company. Based on the data, the estimated mean commuting distance was $15.4$ miles, with a margin of error of $1.6$ miles.\n\nGiven this information, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. It is plausible that the mean commuting distance for all employees is between $13.8$ miles and $17.0$ miles.\n2. It is possible that some employees commute less than $12$ miles.\n3. It is **NOT** possible that some employees commute $17$ miles or more.\n4. It is plausible that all employees' commuting distance is between $13.8$ miles and $17.0$ miles." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229454 1072 #uuid "4b102d3a-f45c-5e31-8f45-f63b6b4fd67d" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227178 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229455 1069 "q-306844" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229455 1071 "A factory wants to estimate the percentage of smartphone batteries produced in a day that are defective. A random sample of $420$ batteries from that day was inspected. Based on the sample, it is estimated that $18%$ of all batteries produced that day are defective, with a margin of error of $2.7%$.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions about all batteries produced that day is most reasonable?\n\n1. It is plausible that between $15.3%$ and $20.7%$ of the batteries are defective.\n2. Exactly $18%$ of the batteries are defective.\n3. It is plausible that more than $20.7%$ of the batteries are defective.\n4. Exactly $2.7%$ of the batteries are defective." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229455 1072 #uuid "29ff5507-aab0-59c2-91da-da56e93450fe" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227184 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229456 1069 "q-306845" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229456 1071 "In a study, a biologist estimated that the mean weight of a certain species of fish in a lake is $7.8$ kilograms, with a margin of error of $0.4$ kilograms.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\n\n1. It is not possible that the true mean weight of the fish is less than $7.4$ kilograms or greater than $8.2$ kilograms.\n2. It is plausible that the true mean weight of the fish is between $7.4$ kilograms and $8.2$ kilograms.\n3. Every fish in the lake must weigh between $7.4$ kilograms and $8.2$ kilograms.\n4. The true mean weight of the fish is exactly $7.8$ kilograms." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229456 1072 #uuid "124b6513-3be4-524e-b5a9-b52277ca8089" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227190 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229457 1069 "q-306862" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229457 1071 "![](images/17/173cc75fb5a0e9006dbe4dfee9a857bd791e846fd293da57a404b7c416ccfbb9.png)\n\nDuring a live marketing campaign, a website’s total visits are recorded every $5$ minutes. The plotted points (visits versus time in minutes) show a curve that rises quickly at first and then levels off. Over which time interval do the visits increase most rapidly?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229457 1072 #uuid "c567cf4b-8067-5e60-b312-dd84c7b62200" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227196 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229458 1069 "q-306877" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229458 1071 "![](images/24/240c65807d0e88813b6822f69435e71563a24f836d1025e7407885990bc5f8a3.png)\n\nIn a lab experiment, a heated metal rod is removed from a furnace, and its temperature is measured every $2$ minutes. The plotted points (temperature in $^{∘}F$ versus time in minutes) show a curve that decreases quickly at first and then levels off. Over which time interval does the rod cool most rapidly?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229458 1072 #uuid "a2a8df49-7b85-5a93-98a9-b920a8e05cd9" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227202 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229459 1069 "q-306982" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229459 1071 "![](images/0f/0f45a8d74d162530062aa9c24f019d00be86573988c63e05626278b09c387c35.png)\n\nDiego rode a stationary bike for forty minutes, and his time and speed are shown on the graph above. According to the graph, which of the following statements is **not** true concerning Diego's ride?\n\n1. Diego's speed increased at a constant rate during the first eight minutes.\n2. Diego rode at a constant speed for ten minutes.\n3. Diego's speed reached its maximum during the last ten minutes.\n4. Diego's speed decreased at a constant rate during the last ten minutes." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229459 1072 #uuid "9434af3f-5941-507e-9e71-3d893dfebce0" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227208 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229460 1069 "q-307029" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229460 1071 "![](images/85/85648d02ef153e0d2b0019d66f647c6160caafbc25b16d5ccc370a962ec38e5a.png)\n\nA call center operated for twenty-four hours, and its time and handling rate are shown on the graph above. According to the graph, which of the following statements is **not** true concerning the call center’s operation?\n\n1. The handling rate increased at a constant rate during the first four hours.\n2. The handling rate was constant for eight consecutive hours.\n3. The handling rate reached its maximum during the last eight hours.\n4. The handling rate decreased at a constant rate during the last four hours." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229460 1072 #uuid "df0858ff-53b1-5498-9525-784902e9bcbc" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227214 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229461 1069 "q-307201" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229461 1071 "The data set $G$ is given by\n$52, 55, 58, 61, 64$,\nand the data set $H$ is given by\n$40, 57, 60, 63, y$,\ncontain $5$ values each. If the mean of data set $G$ is the same as the mean of data set $H$, what is the value of $y$?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229461 1072 #uuid "8833cad2-c10f-546c-b9a2-2cd231e1d493" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227220 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229462 1069 "q-307239" 131941395