$ is one half the speed of runner $N$. Runner $M$ covers a distance of $8x$ meters in $t$ seconds. Which expression represents the time, in seconds, it takes runner $N$ to travel a distance of $48x$ meters?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229507 1072 #uuid "2c57302e-c281-5e3b-9e11-552346945fe5" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227421 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229508 1069 "q-309038" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229508 1071 "Two runners, $A$ and $B$, each move at constant speeds. The speed of runner $A$ is one fourth the speed of runner $B$. Runner $A$ covers a distance of $8q$ meters in $t$ seconds. Which expression represents the time, in seconds, it takes runner $B$ to travel a distance of $48q$ meters?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229508 1072 #uuid "7a3a3680-2e82-535e-89a8-0b768328fe1e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227427 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229509 1069 "q-309040" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229509 1071 "Veterinarians sometimes estimate a dog's age in years by multiplying its weight in pounds by a constant growth factor that varies depending on the breed. The table below shows the growth factors for several dog breeds.\n\n| Dog Breed | Growth<br>factor |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Labrador Retriever | $5$ |\n| German Shepherd | $5.5$ |\n| Golden Retriever | $5$ |\n| Bulldog | $5$ |\n| Beagle | $4$ |\n| Boxer | $5.5$ |\n| Poodle | $4.5$ |\n| Rottweiler | $6$ |\n\nSuppose a Golden Retriever and a Poodle each currently weigh $14$ pounds. Which of the following is closest to the difference, in pounds, between their weights $11$ years from now?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229509 1072 #uuid "43dc7d05-6722-57e4-9dc5-02b3eca6d402" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227433 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229510 1069 "q-309248" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229510 1071 "Which of the following is true about the values of $4^{x}$ and $4x + 1$ for $x > 0$?\n\n1. There is a constant $c$ such that if $0 < x < c$, then $4^{x} > 4x + 1$, but if $x > c$, then $4^{x} < 4x + 1$.\n2. There is a constant $c$ such that if $0 < x < c$, then $4^{x} < 4x + 1$, but if $x > c$, then $4^{x} > 4x + 1$.\n3. For all $x > 0$, it is true that $4^{x} > 4x + 1$.\n4. For all $x > 0$, it is true that $4^{x} < 4x + 1$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229510 1072 #uuid "7cab75cf-392e-573a-877b-e46939361cc3" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227439 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229511 1069 "q-309257" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229511 1071 "Which of the following is true about the values of $7x + 3$ and $7^{x}$ for $x > 0$?\n\n1. For all $x > 0$, it is true that $7x + 3 < 7^{x}$.\n2. There is a constant $c$ such that if $0 < x < c$, then $7x + 3 < 7^{x}$, but if $x > c$, then $7^{x} < 7x + 3$.\n3. There is a constant $c$ such that if $0 < x < c$, then $7x + 3 > 7^{x}$, but if $x > c$, then $7^{x} > 7x + 3$.\n4. For all $x > 0$, it is true that $7x + 3 > 7^{x}$." 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229511 1072 #uuid "81826ad3-d8dc-5a66-bb84-cd14152a312e" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227445 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229512 1069 "q-309439" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229512 1071 "A dairy farm produced milk from $30$ cows with a mean yield of $125.5$ liters. After one cow’s data is excluded, the mean yield for the remaining cows drops to $124.0$ liters. How many liters did that cow produce?" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229512 1072 #uuid "5683e711-99ca-5383-8567-d62233946c06" 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227451 13194139533381 17592186045585 true] [17592186229513 1069 "q-